{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T15:46:13.851Z","ordinal":16658,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a130c9-841c-7440-b5e7-c596eae592f9","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_0ad9c8d4b1f25792016acd00c412c487d0bcc6ab6f8e1d87ef","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Current main is green: all 874 tests passed with localhost access. I’ll now add the plan, clearly separating measured results from revenue assumptions and documenting the cfo.ai concurrency and link-sharing risks.\n"}],"phase":"commentary"},"started_at_ms":1791819972267,"completed_at_ms":1791819973851}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T15:46:13.854Z","ordinal":16659,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_0ad9c8d4b1f25792016acd00c412c487d0bcc6ab6f8e1d87ef","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Current main is green: all 874 tests passed with localhost access. I’ll now add the plan, clearly separating measured results from revenue assumptions and documenting the cfo.ai concurrency and link-sharing risks.\n"}],"phase":"commentary","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a130c9-841c-7440-b5e7-c596eae592f9","create_time":1791819969.550473,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}},"metadata":{"retained_source":{"id":{"message_id":"msg_0ad9c8d4b1f25792016acd00c412c487d0bcc6ab6f8e1d87ef","turn_id":"01a130c9-841c-7440-b5e7-c596eae592f9","role":"assistant"},"revision":"retained_01a130cb-04de-76b1-9526-c2be3d75217e","complete":true},"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":1664}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T15:47:58.585Z","ordinal":16660,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0ad9c8d4b1f25792016acd00c599f487d0bf601a4d34c9d123","status":"completed","call_id":"call_96asSmuVrjKyrmCIUAmY3n41","name":"exec","input":"text(await tools.apply_patch(\"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md\\n+# Tally Business Plan\\n+\\n+Submission version, 12 October 2026. This is a business hypothesis supported by\\n+a small working-product experiment, not a forecast backed by paying customers.\\n+**Measured** means recorded in real Tally runs; **assumption** means a scenario\\n+input that still needs validation. Run costs are estimates from recorded tokens\\n+and configured model list prices, not provider invoices.\\n+\\n+## Problem and Customer\\n+\\n+Coding-agent teams can spend top-model prices on routine tasks while losing\\n+visibility into retries, abandoned work and verification outcomes. Subscription\\n+or account-level limits do not tell a team how much one task should cost.\\n+\\n+Our proposed customer is the engineering lead or budget owner of a team already\\n+using coding agents, with repeatable, testable tasks and enough agent spend to\\n+justify cost controls. This customer profile is a hypothesis: we have not\\n+verified willingness to pay or signed customers. Engineering headcount alone is\\n+not evidence of coding-agent seats, agent spend or savings.\\n+\\n+## Product\\n+\\n+Tally is a local CLI that chooses a configured model tier for each task, sets a\\n+spend ceiling, watches usage and verifies the result before publishing a task\\n+PR. A capped or failed attempt can escalate to a stronger tier with the earlier\\n+branch and metadata-only Entire checkpoint context. Generated summaries remain\\n+off by default. Polling and metering can overshoot a ceiling; it is not a hard\\n+provider-side billing limit.\\n+\\n+Workers run sequentially through direct Claude Code or Codex commands, or\\n+through Agent Orchestrator (AO), including parallel AO workers. Neatlogs history\\n+supplies observed pass rates and costs for task size and actual model when\\n+enough observations exist; otherwise Tally explicitly uses priors/configured\\n+caps. Entire supplies graph sizing and checkpoint evidence. cfo.ai supplies\\n+the monthly budget and records real spend. Local ledger and CSV records remain\\n+available when integrations are unavailable.\\n+\\n+## Pricing and the Baseline\\n+\\n+**Proposed pricing: share of savings.** The customer pays a share of what Tally\\n+saved, and nothing when it saves nothing. The example share below is an\\n+**assumption**, not a committed commercial rate.\\n+\\n+```text\\n+Monthly fee = agreed share x max(0, agreed baseline cost - actual Tally cost)\\n+```\\n+\\n+The baseline must be agreed before billing, using a matched sample of tasks\\n+executed on the customer's chosen top model, with the same starting code,\\n+task text and verification criteria. The agreement must specify task mix,\\n+accepted outcomes, model prices, caching treatment and how repeats are sampled.\\n+Tally cost includes failed attempts, escalations and operational retries for\\n+that workload; unstarted or unresolved work cannot be counted as savings.\\n+Matched successful outcomes are required before comparing value delivered.\\n+\\n+For a pilot, report estimated savings first and reconcile costs with provider\\n+bills before charging. A previous month's bill can help plan a budget but is\\n+not a matched-task baseline. Pricing a cheap model's tokens at top-model rates\\n+is a price comparison, not measured savings: a stronger model may use different\\n+tokens. Agreeing a reliable baseline and a billing/audit process is still work\\n+to do; the proposed commercial fee is not automatically collected by Tally.\\n+\\n+## What Is Measured\\n+\\n+Three matched tasks were run once per pass against the same pinned starting\\n+commit and identical task text. Two other planned tasks were not run.\\n+\\n+| Pass | Routing | Estimated total USD | USD per task | Final tasks passing | Cheaper than A |\\n+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n+| A | Every task on the top tier | 1.21693600 | 0.40564533 | 3/3 | Baseline |\\n+| B | Tally v1, built-in priors | 0.30932564 | 0.10310855 | 3/3 | 74.58% (about 75%) |\\n+| C | Tally v2, history enabled | 0.42632384 | 0.14210795 | 3/3 | 64.97% (about 65%) |\\n+\\n+Evidence identifiers: source `9f18111e948a0a7c7e09c99dc86f854ca595e1a8`,\\n+pinned task base `565e6ccd757ff794ead643dac7359efe67ec9db2`, runs\\n+`run-20261012T104159Z-comparison-A`,\\n+`run-20261012T105538Z-comparison-B` and\\n+`run-20261012T111142Z-comparison-C`.\\n+\\n+Each pass also had three separately recorded zero-token startup retries from\\n+an experiment spawn timing bug. These are infrastructure retries, not model\\n+failures or additional model attempts-to-success; recorded costs remain in the\\n+totals. The 3/3 pass rate is the final verified task rate, not a claim of clean\\n+starts. cfo.ai was off in these comparison passes to avoid concurrent writes.\\n+\\n+**Limits:** three tasks, one run each per pass, in one repository; no confidence\\n+intervals or repeatability claim. In C only the large task used observed\\n+Neatlogs statistics: its top-cost expectation changed from $1.50 to about\\n+$0.303 from two samples. No selected tier or cap changed between B and C.\\n+The other tasks still used priors; learned caps require five successful\\n+observations for the relevant size/model. C read shared history, not an\\n+isolated dataset containing only B. C cost more than B: this experiment does\\n+not establish that trace-driven routing improves on prior-only routing.\\n+Model variability and caching are confounders. Most earlier tasks were small\\n+documentation changes. Graph size did not show a clear linear cost signal in\\n+this sample; it is not a validated general cost predictor.\\n+\\n+Other real-run evidence establishes capability, not commercial performance:\\n+\\n+- All four runner/harness paths completed verified work: Codex via AO, Claude\\n+  Code via AO, Claude Code direct and Codex direct; see the README's\\n+  [real-run evidence](../README.md#real-run-evidence).\\n+- Cap-and-escalate worked in `run-20261012-100856Z`: the cheap attempt was\\n+  stopped at a $0.018 ceiling (recorded spend $0.01956044), then the middle\\n+  attempt passed at $0.16957640. Its ceiling diagnosis came from the ledger,\\n+  not an error that the worker trace exposed.\\n+- Parallel AO workers completed verified tasks in\\n+  `run-20261012-120222Z`, with worker trace IDs recorded and cfo.ai spend\\n+  reconciled to the ledger. This is a capability proof, not a throughput study.\\n+\\n+## Unit Economics: One Hypothetical Customer\\n+\\n+| Input or Calculation | Value | Basis |\\n+| --- | ---: | --- |\\n+| Engineers using coding agents | 25 | Assumption; not a measured seat count |\\n+| Top-model spend per engineer per month | $200 | Assumption; must be checked against actual agent usage |\\n+| Baseline monthly agent spend | $5,000 | Scenario: 25 x $200 |\\n+| Share of spend/workload routable to cheaper tiers | 60% | Assumption; requires workload-weighted validation |\\n+| Saving on that routable portion, including retries | 80% | Assumption; not established by the experiment |\\n+| Monthly saving | $2,400 | Scenario: $5,000 x 60% x 80% |\\n+| Tally share of savings | 20% | Assumption |\\n+| Monthly revenue per customer | $480 | Scenario: $2,400 x 20% |\\n+| Customer's retained monthly saving | $1,920 | Scenario: $2,400 - $480 |\\n+\\n+The draft's 80% assumed saving was discounted from an earlier roughly 95%\\n+**same-token list-price gap**, allowing for retries and escalation. That price\\n+gap was not a measured task saving. **80% is above, not below, the measured\\n+65-75% aggregate saving in this three-task experiment.** It must not be called\\n+conservative relative to that result. Savings on the routable subset and\\n+savings across the whole workload also have different denominators. Keep\\n+$480 as an illustrative assumption-driven scenario, not an evidenced revenue\\n+projection. Replace these inputs with customer workload and bill data.\\n+\\n+## Pilot Funnel: First Six Months\\n+\\n+CrustData Job Search returned 25 posts; two ambiguous/incomplete posts were\\n+dropped and three duplicate-company posts grouped, leaving **20 distinct\\n+company-domain hiring-intent leads**. Their engineering job descriptions name\\n+coding tools. This establishes a prospecting signal, not deployed use, buying\\n+intent, qualified customers, paying pilots or market size. Posting companies\\n+may be staffing firms, cross-posts can represent the same employer, and sources\\n+were not independently visited. No customer names are claimed here.\\n+\\n+| Stage | Number | Basis |\\n+| --- | ---: | --- |\\n+| Hiring-intent leads discovered | 20 | API-derived evidence, not buyer qualification |\\n+| Leads contacted in six months | 200 | Assumption; needs additional prospect discovery, not extrapolation from the 20 |\\n+| Pilot conversion | 5% | Assumption |\\n+| Pilots started | 10 | Scenario: 200 x 5% |\\n+| Pilot-to-paying conversion | 40% | Assumption |\\n+| Paying customers | 4 | Scenario: 10 x 40%; no signed customers evidenced |\\n+| Monthly revenue at month six | $1,920 | Scenario: 4 x $480; not cumulative six-month revenue |\\n+\\n+The job-intent search cost **0.75 credits**, including the account's reported\\n+fixed query cost; do not quote a result-only rate as the total. The secondary\\n+10-company firmographic search cost 6.3 credits and had no verified coding-tool\\n+use. Neither query match counts nor the historical 2,085 raw matches are TAM\\n+or qualified buyer counts. `tally market --replay` produces the cached\\n+JSON/CSV/Markdown report without another search or credit spend.\\n+\\n+## Costs and Delivery\\n+\\n+- **Model cost:** Tally makes no model calls of its own in the demonstrated\\n+  deterministic routing and metadata-only handoff path. Workers use the\\n+  customer's model accounts; their spend is a customer expense, not free\\n+  compute. Optional model-assisted sizing or generated summaries would add\\n+  costs if explicitly enabled and are not assumed here.\\n+- **Integrations:** Neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai plan costs have not been\\n+  established for a commercial customer; no invented monthly amount is used.\\n+- **Prospecting:** CrustData costs credits; the cached lead report is reusable.\\n+  Future qualification budgets must use account-specific pricing and limits.\\n+- **Hosting:** the CLI runs locally; the project website is static. Commercial\\n+  hosting, support, onboarding, security, billing and maintenance costs remain\\n+  unmeasured. $480 is revenue, not profit or validated gross margin.\\n+\\n+## cfo.ai in the Product and the Plan\\n+\\n+In the product, Tally reads the current month's agent budget and existing spend\\n+from the selected cfo.ai workspace/scenario. Real attempt spend is written back\\n+and read back for reconciliation with the local ledger. Simulated costs are\\n+never written to cfo.ai; failed writes leave local CSV evidence. Read access\\n+and write-back have worked on real runs, separately from the cfo.ai-off\\n+three-pass comparison.\\n+\\n+The [Tally business plan page in cfo.ai](https://app.cfo.ai/s/REDACTED)\\n+holds the earlier planning scenario with editable assumptions. This document\\n+updates its evidence and caveats; it does not claim that those updates have\\n+already been synchronized to the page or write any financial model data.\\n+The plan is separate from the live agent budget/spend tables.\\n+\\n+## Risks and Next Validation\\n+\\n+1. Repeat more matched tasks across repositories, task types and models;\\n+   measure verification failures, escalation costs, caching and startup errors.\\n+   Test whether history actually changes model/cap choices and improves results.\\n+2. Agree auditable baselines and reconciliation rules with pilot users before\\n+   charging a share. Validate actual spend, budget pain and willingness to pay,\\n+   rather than deriving agent spend from headcount.\\n+3. Qualify the hiring-intent leads directly and measure the funnel. The 200\\n+   contacts and all conversion/revenue figures remain assumptions.\\n+4. Fix concurrent cfo.ai write-back: two separate Tally processes can overwrite\\n+   one page's spend cells because cached base-plus-run writes are not atomic\\n+   increments. Use one writer per shared model until this is addressed.\\n+5. Add explicit init disclosure/consent for finance-link sharing. Enabled cfo.ai\\n+   currently causes configured budget/spend URLs to be posted in task PR bodies\\n+   without a separate question. Links do not grant access, but exposing finance\\n+   workspace addresses still matters; avoid configuring shareable links until\\n+   this behavior is acceptable to the repository owner.\\n+6. Establish integration costs, support/security obligations and a repeatable\\n+   onboarding path. Mixed-harness AO switching after a Claude attempt, direct\\n+   escalation and no-account onboarding still need broader live validation.\\n+\\n+The measured result supports a pilot experiment: lower estimated cost with\\n+the same final pass count on this tiny sample. It does not establish a market\\n+size, guaranteed savings, profitability or product-market fit.\\n*** Update File: /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/README.md\\n@@\\n ## Routing Evidence\\n \\n+See the [Tally business plan](docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md) for share-of-savings pricing,\\n+the measured three-task comparison, explicitly labelled unit-economics and\\n+pilot-funnel assumptions, cfo.ai usage and the remaining commercial risks.\\n+\\n From a source checkout, build and analyze an audited list of real run directories:\\n*** End Patch\"));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a130c9-841c-7440-b5e7-c596eae592f9","create_time":1791819969.550473}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T15:47:58.658Z","ordinal":16661,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a130c9-841c-7440-b5e7-c596eae592f9","session_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","root_turn_id":"01a130c9-841c-7440-b5e7-c596eae592f9","response_id":"resp_0ad9c8d4b1f25792016acd00c1745c87d08080856c9bb57dc9","usage":{"input_tokens":119536,"cached_input_tokens":119168,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":3368,"reasoning_output_tokens":0,"total_tokens":122904},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":935845,"cached_input_tokens":815104,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":4260,"reasoning_output_tokens":153,"total_tokens":940105},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":261995619,"cached_input_tokens":254624640,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":930651,"reasoning_output_tokens":204977,"total_tokens":262926270}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T15:47:58.688Z","ordinal":16662,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a130c9-841c-7440-b5e7-c596eae592f9","item":{"type":"FileChange","id":"exec-2e446e2c-6dc2-4305-a744-964f172f1800","changes":{"/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/README.md":{"type":"update","unified_diff":"@@ -419,2 +419,6 @@\n \n+See the [Tally business plan](docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md) for share-of-savings pricing,\n+the measured three-task comparison, explicitly labelled unit-economics and\n+pilot-funnel assumptions, cfo.ai usage and the remaining commercial risks.\n+\n From a source checkout, build and analyze an audited list of real run directories:\n","move_path":null},"/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md":{"type":"add","content":"# Tally Business Plan\n\nSubmission version, 12 October 2026. This is a business hypothesis supported by\na small working-product experiment, not a forecast backed by paying customers.\n**Measured** means recorded in real Tally runs; **assumption** means a scenario\ninput that still needs validation. Run costs are estimates from recorded tokens\nand configured model list prices, not provider invoices.\n\n## Problem and Customer\n\nCoding-agent teams can spend top-model prices on routine tasks while losing\nvisibility into retries, abandoned work and verification outcomes. Subscription\nor account-level limits do not tell a team how much one task should cost.\n\nOur proposed customer is the engineering lead or budget owner of a team already\nusing coding agents, with repeatable, testable tasks and enough agent spend to\njustify cost controls. This customer profile is a hypothesis: we have not\nverified willingness to pay or signed customers. Engineering headcount alone is\nnot evidence of coding-agent seats, agent spend or savings.\n\n## Product\n\nTally is a local CLI that chooses a configured model tier for each task, sets a\nspend ceiling, watches usage and verifies the result before publishing a task\nPR. A capped or failed attempt can escalate to a stronger tier with the earlier\nbranch and metadata-only Entire checkpoint context. Generated summaries remain\noff by default. Polling and metering can overshoot a ceiling; it is not a hard\nprovider-side billing limit.\n\nWorkers run sequentially through direct Claude Code or Codex commands, or\nthrough Agent Orchestrator (AO), including parallel AO workers. Neatlogs history\nsupplies observed pass rates and costs for task size and actual model when\nenough observations exist; otherwise Tally explicitly uses priors/configured\ncaps. Entire supplies graph sizing and checkpoint evidence. cfo.ai supplies\nthe monthly budget and records real spend. Local ledger and CSV records remain\navailable when integrations are unavailable.\n\n## Pricing and the Baseline\n\n**Proposed pricing: share of savings.** The customer pays a share of what Tally\nsaved, and nothing when it saves nothing. The example share below is an\n**assumption**, not a committed commercial rate.\n\n```text\nMonthly fee = agreed share x max(0, agreed baseline cost - actual Tally cost)\n```\n\nThe baseline must be agreed before billing, using a matched sample of tasks\nexecuted on the customer's chosen top model, with the same starting code,\ntask text and verification criteria. The agreement must specify task mix,\naccepted outcomes, model prices, caching treatment and how repeats are sampled.\nTally cost includes failed attempts, escalations and operational retries for\nthat workload; unstarted or unresolved work cannot be counted as savings.\nMatched successful outcomes are required before comparing value delivered.\n\nFor a pilot, report estimated savings first and reconcile costs with provider\nbills before charging. A previous month's bill can help plan a budget but is\nnot a matched-task baseline. Pricing a cheap model's tokens at top-model rates\nis a price comparison, not measured savings: a stronger model may use different\ntokens. Agreeing a reliable baseline and a billing/audit process is still work\nto do; the proposed commercial fee is not automatically collected by Tally.\n\n## What Is Measured\n\nThree matched tasks were run once per pass against the same pinned starting\ncommit and identical task text. Two other planned tasks were not run.\n\n| Pass | Routing | Estimated total USD | USD per task | Final tasks passing | Cheaper than A |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| A | Every task on the top tier | 1.21693600 | 0.40564533 | 3/3 | Baseline |\n| B | Tally v1, built-in priors | 0.30932564 | 0.10310855 | 3/3 | 74.58% (about 75%) |\n| C | Tally v2, history enabled | 0.42632384 | 0.14210795 | 3/3 | 64.97% (about 65%) |\n\nEvidence identifiers: source `9f18111e948a0a7c7e09c99dc86f854ca595e1a8`,\npinned task base `565e6ccd757ff794ead643dac7359efe67ec9db2`, runs\n`run-20261012T104159Z-comparison-A`,\n`run-20261012T105538Z-comparison-B` and\n`run-20261012T111142Z-comparison-C`.\n\nEach pass also had three separately recorded zero-token startup retries from\nan experiment spawn timing bug. These are infrastructure retries, not model\nfailures or additional model attempts-to-success; recorded costs remain in the\ntotals. The 3/3 pass rate is the final verified task rate, not a claim of clean\nstarts. cfo.ai was off in these comparison passes to avoid concurrent writes.\n\n**Limits:** three tasks, one run each per pass, in one repository; no confidence\nintervals or repeatability claim. In C only the large task used observed\nNeatlogs statistics: its top-cost expectation changed from $1.50 to about\n$0.303 from two samples. No selected tier or cap changed between B and C.\nThe other tasks still used priors; learned caps require five successful\nobservations for the relevant size/model. C read shared history, not an\nisolated dataset containing only B. C cost more than B: this experiment does\nnot establish that trace-driven routing improves on prior-only routing.\nModel variability and caching are confounders. Most earlier tasks were small\ndocumentation changes. Graph size did not show a clear linear cost signal in\nthis sample; it is not a validated general cost predictor.\n\nOther real-run evidence establishes capability, not commercial performance:\n\n- All four runner/harness paths completed verified work: Codex via AO, Claude\n  Code via AO, Claude Code direct and Codex direct; see the README's\n  [real-run evidence](../README.md#real-run-evidence).\n- Cap-and-escalate worked in `run-20261012-100856Z`: the cheap attempt was\n  stopped at a $0.018 ceiling (recorded spend $0.01956044), then the middle\n  attempt passed at $0.16957640. Its ceiling diagnosis came from the ledger,\n  not an error that the worker trace exposed.\n- Parallel AO workers completed verified tasks in\n  `run-20261012-120222Z`, with worker trace IDs recorded and cfo.ai spend\n  reconciled to the ledger. This is a capability proof, not a throughput study.\n\n## Unit Economics: One Hypothetical Customer\n\n| Input or Calculation | Value | Basis |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Engineers using coding agents | 25 | Assumption; not a measured seat count |\n| Top-model spend per engineer per month | $200 | Assumption; must be checked against actual agent usage |\n| Baseline monthly agent spend | $5,000 | Scenario: 25 x $200 |\n| Share of spend/workload routable to cheaper tiers | 60% | Assumption; requires workload-weighted validation |\n| Saving on that routable portion, including retries | 80% | Assumption; not established by the experiment |\n| Monthly saving | $2,400 | Scenario: $5,000 x 60% x 80% |\n| Tally share of savings | 20% | Assumption |\n| Monthly revenue per customer | $480 | Scenario: $2,400 x 20% |\n| Customer's retained monthly saving | $1,920 | Scenario: $2,400 - $480 |\n\nThe draft's 80% assumed saving was discounted from an earlier roughly 95%\n**same-token list-price gap**, allowing for retries and escalation. That price\ngap was not a measured task saving. **80% is above, not below, the measured\n65-75% aggregate saving in this three-task experiment.** It must not be called\nconservative relative to that result. Savings on the routable subset and\nsavings across the whole workload also have different denominators. Keep\n$480 as an illustrative assumption-driven scenario, not an evidenced revenue\nprojection. Replace these inputs with customer workload and bill data.\n\n## Pilot Funnel: First Six Months\n\nCrustData Job Search returned 25 posts; two ambiguous/incomplete posts were\ndropped and three duplicate-company posts grouped, leaving **20 distinct\ncompany-domain hiring-intent leads**. Their engineering job descriptions name\ncoding tools. This establishes a prospecting signal, not deployed use, buying\nintent, qualified customers, paying pilots or market size. Posting companies\nmay be staffing firms, cross-posts can represent the same employer, and sources\nwere not independently visited. No customer names are claimed here.\n\n| Stage | Number | Basis |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Hiring-intent leads discovered | 20 | API-derived evidence, not buyer qualification |\n| Leads contacted in six months | 200 | Assumption; needs additional prospect discovery, not extrapolation from the 20 |\n| Pilot conversion | 5% | Assumption |\n| Pilots started | 10 | Scenario: 200 x 5% |\n| Pilot-to-paying conversion | 40% | Assumption |\n| Paying customers | 4 | Scenario: 10 x 40%; no signed customers evidenced |\n| Monthly revenue at month six | $1,920 | Scenario: 4 x $480; not cumulative six-month revenue |\n\nThe job-intent search cost **0.75 credits**, including the account's reported\nfixed query cost; do not quote a result-only rate as the total. The secondary\n10-company firmographic search cost 6.3 credits and had no verified coding-tool\nuse. Neither query match counts nor the historical 2,085 raw matches are TAM\nor qualified buyer counts. `tally market --replay` produces the cached\nJSON/CSV/Markdown report without another search or credit spend.\n\n## Costs and Delivery\n\n- **Model cost:** Tally makes no model calls of its own in the demonstrated\n  deterministic routing and metadata-only handoff path. Workers use the\n  customer's model accounts; their spend is a customer expense, not free\n  compute. Optional model-assisted sizing or generated summaries would add\n  costs if explicitly enabled and are not assumed here.\n- **Integrations:** Neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai plan costs have not been\n  established for a commercial customer; no invented monthly amount is used.\n- **Prospecting:** CrustData costs credits; the cached lead report is reusable.\n  Future qualification budgets must use account-specific pricing and limits.\n- **Hosting:** the CLI runs locally; the project website is static. Commercial\n  hosting, support, onboarding, security, billing and maintenance costs remain\n  unmeasured. $480 is revenue, not profit or validated gross margin.\n\n## cfo.ai in the Product and the Plan\n\nIn the product, Tally reads the current month's agent budget and existing spend\nfrom the selected cfo.ai workspace/scenario. Real attempt spend is written back\nand read back for reconciliation with the local ledger. Simulated costs are\nnever written to cfo.ai; failed writes leave local CSV evidence. Read access\nand write-back have worked on real runs, separately from the cfo.ai-off\nthree-pass comparison.\n\nThe [Tally business plan page in cfo.ai](https://app.cfo.ai/s/REDACTED)\nholds the earlier planning scenario with editable assumptions. This document\nupdates its evidence and caveats; it does not claim that those updates have\nalready been synchronized to the page or write any financial model data.\nThe plan is separate from the live agent budget/spend tables.\n\n## Risks and Next Validation\n\n1. Repeat more matched tasks across repositories, task types and models;\n   measure verification failures, escalation costs, caching and startup errors.\n   Test whether history actually changes model/cap choices and improves results.\n2. Agree auditable baselines and reconciliation rules with pilot users before\n   charging a share. Validate actual spend, budget pain and willingness to pay,\n   rather than deriving agent spend from headcount.\n3. Qualify the hiring-intent leads directly and measure the funnel. The 200\n   contacts and all conversion/revenue figures remain assumptions.\n4. Fix concurrent cfo.ai write-back: two separate Tally processes can overwrite\n   one page's spend cells because cached base-plus-run writes are not atomic\n   increments. Use one writer per shared model until this is addressed.\n5. Add explicit init disclosure/consent for finance-link sharing. Enabled cfo.ai\n   currently causes configured budget/spend URLs to be posted in task PR bodies\n   without a separate question. Links do not grant access, but exposing finance\n   workspace addresses still matters; avoid configuring shareable links until\n   this behavior is acceptable to the repository owner.\n6. Establish integration costs, support/security obligations and a repeatable\n   onboarding path. Mixed-harness AO switching after a Claude attempt, direct\n   escalation and no-account onboarding still need broader live validation.\n\nThe measured result supports a pilot experiment: lower estimated cost with\nthe same final pass count on this tiny sample. It does not establish a market\nsize, guaranteed savings, profitability or product-market fit.\n"}},"status":"completed","stdout":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md\nM /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/README.md\n","stderr":""},"started_at_ms":1791820078658,"completed_at_ms":1791820078688}}
