{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:51:01.217Z","ordinal":17119,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a131ab-2161-7f00-bedb-ca3dded38bb7","call_id":"call_13e6e90e6a5f4a30a16929fbff324da7","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.7 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 18053)\nTotal output lines: 4\n\n{\"chunk_id\":\"43c66f\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000015708,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":8594,\"output\":\"# Tally\\n\\nA CFO for a fleet of coding agents. Give it a backlog and a budget. It sizes\\neach task, sends it to the cheapest model likely to finish it, stops any task\\nthat overruns its spend ceiling, hands failed work to a stronger model, and\\nends with a ledger tying spend to each attempt, its test result and any opened PR.\\n\\n**Start here: [Getting Started](docs/GETTING_STARTED.md)** covers the no-account demo, every init question, your first real run, troubleshooting and uninstall.\\n\\nStatus: early build. The next release is `tally-agents` version `0.2.0`; npm\\n`latest` remains `0.1.0` until the maintainer publishes it.\\n\\n0.2.0 adds direct runners, opt-in parallel AO workers, model-isolated\\ntrace-history routing and caps, failure diagnosis, and reviewed CrustData\\npilot-company reports. Configurations produced by 0.1.0 init continue to run\\nsequentially unchanged; its legacy `concurrency` field does not opt into\\n`parallelWorkers`. New 0.2.0 configuration fields are not accepted by 0.1.0.\\nThe GitHub release alternative is also available below. The seven-task demo uses simulated workers; real\\nruns use Agent Orchestrator by default, or the direct runner (Claude Code or Codex),\\nand record their attempts and evidence separately.\\n\\n## Install\\n\\nPrerequisites before you install:\\n\\n- Node 20 or later and npm.\\n- Git, for installing from GitHub or from a source checkout.\\n\\nThe built CLI runtime is checked on Node 20 (20.20.2), including the seven-task\\ndry demo. Vitest 5 is development-only and requires Node 22.12+ on the supported\\n22.x line (or supported 24/26+ versions); source contributors should use that\\nruntime for tests. The built npm release includes neither Vitest nor other dev\\ndependencies and keeps its runtime requirement at Node 20+.\\n\\nThat is all the simulated demo and the dashboard need. They use no Agent\\nOrchestrator account, coding-agent login or GitHub credentials.\\n\\nA real run on your own repository also needs, today:\\n\\n- **Agent Orchestrator (AO), installed and running**, for the default `runner: ao`.\\n  Alternatively, set `runner: direct` in `tally.yaml`; Tally creates worktrees\\n  and starts `claude -p` or `codex exec` itself, by each tier's harness, without\\n  an AO daemon. A missing or signed-out harness stops the run; Tally never\\n  substitutes one for another.\\n- At least one coding agent that AO can start, installed and signed in: Codex\\n  or Claude Code. `tally init` proposes model tiers only for the agents it\\n  finds installed.\\n- GitHub CLI, signed in with `gh auth login`, with access to the repository.\\n  Tally reads issues and opens pull requests through it.\\n- A test command for the repository that exits 0 when the work is good. Tally\\n  accepts a task only when that command passes, so `tally init` writes no\\n  configuration without one.\\n\\nneatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai are optional. Each is off unless you turn it on\\nduring `tally init`, and the dashboard shows which are on, off or broken.\\nWith Entire off or unavailable, sizing uses named paths and bounded identifier\\nsearches in tracked repository files. It counts explicit new files and nearby\\ntests without claiming graph dependencies. Specific target evidence increases\\nconfidence; ambiguous or truncated searches remain cautious. `run --plan`\\nprints the size, confidence, route, ceiling and price-history source without\\nstarting a worker or writing history.\\n\\n### Direct Codex Runner\\n\\nThe direct Codex path has completed a real run: `run-20261011-130259Z`, using\\nluna, produced [chip-design-groundup PR #18](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/18),\\nwith both decision and worker trace ids and a Tally-created commit.\\n\\nWith `runner: direct`, a tier with `harness: codex` runs `codex exec --json` in\\nthe task's worktree. It needs Codex on PATH and signed in with `codex login`.\\nThe worker runs in Codex's `workspace-write` sandbox with approvals off and the\\nnetwork off; it can write only in its worktree and one scratch directory, and\\nTally never passes a bypass or full-access flag. That sandbox cannot write git\\nmetadata, so the worker leaves its edits in the working tree and Tally commits\\nthem. The repository's test command reaches the worker as a Tally-written\\nscript outside the worktree; a test that needs the network fails inside the\\nworker, and Tally's own verification afterwards is what decides the result.\\n\\nLive spend is read from that session's own Codex rollout file and priced at the\\ntier's list price. Counts arrive at model-call boundaries, so a ceiling stop\\ncan overshoot by one call. If the rollout file is missing or unreadable the\\nworker is stopped rather than left unmetered. The rollout is an internal Codex\\nformat and may change between Codex versions.\\n\\n### Headless Claude Runner\\n\\n`runner: direct` needs Claude Code installed on PATH and signed in with\\n`claude auth login`. Keep each tier's `harness: claude-code` and explicit model;\\nthe usual test command, list prices, budgets and integrations still apply.\\n`claudeCode.allow` must contain the tool rules consented to in `tally init`;\\nTally passes that list verbatim and refuses to launch Claude without it.\\nDirect workers use scoped file-edit and git/test/setup allow rules with\\n`--permission-mode dontAsk`, never bypass-permissions. Push and `gh` are denied\\nto workers; Tally verifies and publishes. Setup runs in the new worktree before\\nthe agent starts and is recorded separately as `setupDurationMs`.\\n\\nThe live meter reads [Claude's partial stream events](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/streaming-output)\\nand deduplicates API message ids; final JSON usage settles the same session,\\nnot an additional charge. Usage arrives at message boundaries, so a ceiling\\ncan overshoot during a long generation or between watcher polls. Prompt-cache\\nwrites use [Anthropic's 5-minute / 1-hour multipliers](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching)\\nof 1.25x / 2x the configured input price. Existing ledger costs are unchanged.\\nSubagents and MCP tools are disabled so their unmetered work cannot escape the\\ntask ceiling. These are harness permission rules, not an OS isolation guarantee;\\nconfigured commands and installed hooks must be trusted. Entire and neatlogs\\nhooks are retained (no bare mode); verify capture through the run's recorded\\nevidence and read-back rather than assuming that installed hooks prove delivery.\\nPublished worktrees are removed; stopped/failed checkouts stay available for\\nhandoff or recovery. A live direct process cannot be reattached after Tally exits.\\n\\n### AO Workers\\n\\nWith `runner: ao`, each tier chooses its own `harness` and model. Codex workers\\nuse the project's consented `auto` mode. Claude Code workers use `default` at\\nlaunch and a worktree-local `.claude/settings.local.json` with the consented\\n`dontAsk` rules and exact Tally test/setup scripts. The local settings and\\ninstructions are excluded from commits. No bypass-permissions mode is used.\\nMixed AO tiers select the harness mode immediately before spawning under an\\nin-process project lock; separate Tally processes and manual AO changes are not\\ncoordinated by that lock. Both Codex AO and Claude Code AO have real-run evidence.\\nCorrelated worker-trace read-back covers all four runner/harness paths; delivery\\nstill needs capture hooks and read access, and unverified traces remain missing.\\n\\n### Real-Run Evidence\\n\\nAll four runner/harness paths have completed real runs on chip-design-groundup:\\n\\n| Path | Real-run PR evidence |\\n|---|---|\\n| Codex via AO | [#10](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/10) |\\n| Claude Code via AO | [#13](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/13), [#16](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/16) |\\n| Claude Code direct | [#14](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/14) |\\n| Codex direct | [#18](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/18) |\\n\\nThese runs do not prove every workflow. The following remain unproven live:\\n\\n- Mixed AO spawning a real Codex worker after a Claude Code worker. The mixed\\n  setup proof completed on Claude Code without escalating to Codex.\\n- Parallel workers.\\n- Escalation on the direct runner.\\n- The no-account onboarding path.\\n\\nThe simulated demo is not evidence of any real worker path.\\n\\nThree ways in:\\n\\n1. **npm (primary):** `npm install -g tally-agents`.\\n2. **GitHub (alternative, available now):** `npm install -g github:Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack#release`.\\n   The `release` branch includes the compiled CLI and records its source commit\\n   in `release-source.json`; no TypeScript or manual build is needed. The main\\n   branch is source-only and is not the global-install target.\\n3. **Source checkout:** `git clone https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack`,\\n   enter `neatlogs-hack`, and run `npm install` followed by `npm run build`.\\n   Then use `node bin/tally.js` in place of `tally` in the commands below.\\n   After editing TypeScript sources, run `npm run build` again. Generated\\n   `dist/` files are not committed to main.\\n\\nThe npm package is named `tally-agents`; the installed command is still\\n`tally`.\\n\\nMaintainers can build a release tree with\\n`npm run release:branch -- --source origin/main --out /tmp/tally-release`.\\nAdd `--push` to update the dedicated `release` branch (never publishes to npm).\\nThe main-push workflow builds and smoke-checks that tree before updating it.\\nFor npm publication, pack the built `release` tree, not the source-only main\\ncheckout. Check that `release-source.json` identifies the intended main commit,\\nthen inspect and smoke-test the tarball before publishing that exact artifact.\\nThe built package includes `dist/` and has no build or install lifecycle scripts.\\n`npm run test:git-install` checks the GitHub `#release` URL with an empty cache\\nand temporary global prefix, then checks version, help, demo and dashboard\\noutside the checkout. Pass another Git URL after `--` to check a candidate.\\n\\n## Quickstart\\n\\nAfter either global installation, start in an empty directory:\\n\\n```bash\\ntally demo\\ntally dashboard\\n```\\n\\nThe demo writes `.tally/runs/` in that directory. Open the URL printed by\\n`tally dashboard`; it reads the latest run and lists recorded runs. Keep it\\nrunning while viewing the page, and press Ctrl-C to stop it. If its default\\nport is occupied, use `tally dashboard --port 0` for a free port.\\n\\n`tally demo --live` keeps workers simulated while checking the configured\\nread-only integrations. Bundled cfo.ai workspace/table values are placeholders;\\nmissing credentials are reported, and no real worker is launched.\\n\\n### A Provided Tarball\\n\\nWith only a packed `tally-agents-0.2.0.tgz` in your current directory, these\\ncommands install into a temporary prefix without changing your normal global\\ninstallation, then follow the same quickstart:\\n\\n```bash\\nnpm install -g --prefix \\\"$PWD/tally-install\\\" ./tally-agents-0.2.0.tgz\\nexport PATH=\\\"$PWD/tally-install/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ntally demo\\ntally dashboard --port 0\\n```\\n\\nNo checkout, manual build or `tally init` is needed for the demo.\\n\\n### First Run On Your Own Repo\\n\\nCheck the prerequisites for a real run above first. Then, from the root of the\\nrepository you want Tally to work on:\\n\\n1. **Label the issues Tally should take.** Tally reads open issues that carry\\n   one label (`tally` unless you choose another). The label must exist and be\\n   on at least one open issue:\\n\\n   ```bash\\n   gh label create tally --description \\\"Work for Tally\\\"\\n   gh issue edit 12 --add-label tally\\n   gh issue list --label tally --state open\\n   ```\\n\\n   The last command is the query Tally makes. If it prints nothing, Tally has\\n   no tasks to work on.\\n2. **`tally init`** asks for a budget and the issue label, shows the\\n   short configuration summary and the AO changes it would make, and applies\\n   nothing until you confirm. Type the label exactly as it is on GitHub.\\n3. **`tally doctor`** prints one line per integration: connected, missing or\\n   broken, with the fix.\\n4. **`tally run --plan`** lists the tasks it found and the model each would go\\n   to. It starts no workers and spends nothing. Check the task count here\\n   before going further.\\n5. **`tally run`** starts real workers through the configured AO or direct runner. It spends against your\\n   agent accounts and can push branches and open pull requests on the\\n   repository.\\n6. **`tally dashboard`**, in a second terminal in the same directory, prints a\\n   local URL showing the run as it happens.\\n\\nMore on `tally init`: neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai are separate opt-in prompts,\\ndefaulting to No. When both coding CLIs are available, a numbered menu defaults\\nto Codex; if only one is signed in, init selects it automatically. A stopped\\nor unavailable AO daemon selects the direct runner without asking. New configs\\ndefault to one budget-checked retry on the `cheap` tier after explicit model\\naccess rejection; re-init preserves an existing fallback setting. Set\\n`modelAccessFallbackTier` in `tally.yaml` or use\\n`--model-access-fallback-tier <name>` to change it. It displays a compact proposal (full YAML with `--verbose`), install record in\\n`.tally/`, AO project registration/configuration and setup hook, then asks for\\nfinal confirmation (default No) before applying changes. Use `tally init\\n--dry-run` to preview without applying, or `--yes` only when you consent to\\napplying detected defaults without prompts. Review model tiers, prices, tests\\nand ceilings before starting workers. Claude's consented rules are installed\\nlocally before launch. If Neatlogs is enabled, init also installs its hooks in\\nthe selected agents' global settings; those hooks trace matching sessions on\\nthis computer, including sessions outside Tally.\\nThe final summary shows the permission policy for this AO project's workers.\\nUninstall restores owned project settings; it never enables bypass-permissions.\\n\\nInteractive init requires a terminal. Piped input exits with a plain message\\ninstead of waiting for answers. For scripted onboarding, pass values explicitly:\\n\\n```sh\\ntally init --yes --test-command \\\"node --check js/app.js\\\" \\\\\\n  --setup-command \\\"\\\" --budget 5 --issue-label tally \\\\\\n  --neatlogs yes --entire no --cfoai yes\\n```\\n\\nAn empty setup flag means no setup. Selecting Neatlogs runs a synthetic key\\nprobe and installs global worker hooks for the selected coding agent(s).\\nPast-run history connects automatically when its read credentials are already\\nconfigured; otherwise Tally uses local history. Selecting cfo.ai enables its\\nmanaged scenario guide and per-attempt reports; creating missing spend tables\\nstill has a separate confirmation. Entire installation, login, repository\\ncapture, settings publication, and mirror creation keep their own steps.\\n\\nInit detects test/setup commands from repository evidence and proposes tiers\\nonly for installed Codex or Claude Code agents. When no test command is found,\\nit asks for a shell command: Tally cannot verify work without one. Blank input\\nor `--yes` without either a detected command or `--test-command` fails without writing configuration\\nor changing AO settings. An undetected setup command is optional and never\\nguessed.\\n\\nAO does not need to be on your ordinary terminal's PATH. Tally resolves the\\nCLI from PATH first, then `TALLY_AO_BIN`, then `repo.aoBin` in `tally.yaml`\\n(relative to that file), then the macOS Agent Orchestrator app bundle under\\n`/Applications` or `~/Applications`. For a custom installation:\\n\\n```bash\\nexport TALLY_AO_BIN=\\\"/Applications/Agent Orchestrator.app/Contents/Resources/daemon/ao\\\"\\ntally init\\n```\\n\\nDoctor and real runs use the same resolver. Init checks AO, git, GitHub auth\\nand requested integrations before applying changes. An Entire opt-in requires\\nEntire to be enabled in the target repo; init prints `entire enable` and stops\\nif it is not, rather than installing capture hooks without consent.\\n\\nOnce confirmed, init records progress in `.tally/init-pending.json`. If an AO\\nor file operation fails, it lists confirmed changes and unwritten files and\\nprints the exact init command to resume. Re-running resumes only its owned,\\nunchanged files; edited configs or changed AO settings are never overwritten.\\nThe recovery record is removed after init and doctor complete. This is a\\nrecoverable transaction, not an atomic transaction spanning the AO daemon and\\nthe filesystem: an interrupted call may require checking its outcome.\\n\\nRun `tally init` again to connect a product you skipped. It proposes an\\nintegration-only diff, preserves the rest of your config and AO ownership,\\nand asks for confirmation (default No). `--dry-run` shows the diff without\\nwriting. With no proposed changes it exits cleanly. Interrupted re-init\\nwrites can be resumed using the command printed in the error.\\n\\n`tally run --dry-run` runs the same loop with simulated workers instead of\\nreal ones.\\n\\n`tally uninstall` lists the files and AO settings it owns and asks for\\nconfirmation before removing them. It refuses to overwrite edits made since\\ninit. It leaves run history, credentials and the globally installed CLI in\\nplace; remove that CLI separately with `npm uninstall -g tally-agents`.\\n\\n### Static Snapshot\\n\\n`tally publish --out site` writes a self-contained static dashboard,\\ndefaulting to real recorded runs. Select folders explicitly with\\n`tally publish --out site --runs run-id-1,run-id-2`; use this for simulated demo\\ndata. It scans exported files for sensitive content and prints file sizes and\\nthe total. Serve the directory on a static HTTP host, including under a\\nsub-path. This creates local files and does not deploy them.\\n\\nDuring development, `npm run tally -- <command>` runs the CLI from source.\\n\\nFor routine validation, run:\\n\\n```bash\\nnpm test\\n```\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n| Command | What it will do |\\n|---|---|\\n| `tally run` | Run the backlog against the budget in `tally.yaml`. Flags: `--dry-run`, `--budget <usd>`, `--only <task>`, `--baseline`, `--config <path>`, `--plan`, `--dashboard`/`--open`. |\\n| `tally experiment` | Compare baseline and Tally modes with `--dry-run --repeats N` (default 2). Paid AO experiments require explicit `--yes --budget <usd>` consent and a configured test timeout. The budget is an aggregate safety cap; baseline has no per-task ceiling. Both modes verify locally without pushing or opening PRs, run sequentially from the same pinned base, and close workers on interruption. Real comparison safety monitoring has not yet been exercised live. |\\n| `tally demo` | Run the full loop with simulated workers on `demo/tally.yaml` and `demo/sample-backlog.yaml`. No accounts or keys. Writes `.tally/runs/<run-id>/run.json` and `ledger.json`; add `--dashboard` for the one-command demo, or `--live` to use read-only integrations while workers stay fake. `--live` does not write CFO spend, but may send decision traces to Neatlogs. |\\n| `tally dashboard` | Serve `dashboard/` and `.tally/runs/` read-only on `127.0.0.1`. The printed URL passes `?data=/runs/latest/` so the page reads the stable latest-run mirror. |\\n| `tally init` | Preview files and AO changes, then ask final default-No consent. `--dry-run` previews; `--yes` applies defaults without prompts. Claude workers require the consented scoped allow-list. |\\n| `tally uninstall` | Confirm removal of init-owned configuration and AO settings; preserve history and credentials. |\\n| `tally logo` | Replay the first-run logo animation. It plays once by itself, the first time you run bare `tally` or `tally init` in a terminal; after that bare `tally` and `--help` show a small still mark. Nothing is drawn when output is piped, in CI, on `TERM=dumb`, or for a…8053 tokens truncated…plus correlated read-back. `workerTraceDetail` and run integration status now retain actionable lock, auth, project, network, and indexing diagnostics. Null means not established, not an invisible success. |\\n| Entire | `checkpointId`, `entireSessionId`, a session URL when the repo has a ready Entire mirror, and handoff metadata. Off integrations or attempts without a captured checkpoint can legitimately have null IDs. The PR body and dashboard link to Entire only when the mirror is ready. |\\n| cfo.ai | Configured monthly `Agent budget`, `Agent spend`, `Budget remaining`, and tier rows in the spend-detail table. Dashboard/run state retain available page/table links. Real spend is written after attempts; a failed write is exposed with `cfoai-spend-fallback.csv`, not silently treated as success. |\\n\\nCosts are list-price token estimates, not provider invoices or subscription cash charges. Changing prices does not reprice past ledgers. With cfo.ai off, no model cells are read or written; with neatlogs off, no decision traces are exported; with Entire off, no checkpoint evidence is promised.\\n\\n## The Four Runner Paths\\n\\nAll four have completed individual real tasks on chip-design-groundup:\\n\\n| Runner / harness | How workers run | Recorded PR evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| AO / Codex | AO owns a Codex tui session and exposes usage; Tally verifies/publishes. | [#10](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/10) |\\n| AO / Claude Code | AO starts Claude with worktree-local consented settings/scripts; Tally verifies/publishes. | [#13](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/13), [#16](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/16) |\\n| Direct / Claude Code | Tally starts `claude -p`, meters streamed usage including cache writes, host-commits edits and publishes. | [#14](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/14) |\\n| Direct / Codex | Tally starts `codex exec --json`, reads live usage only from its own session's rollout, host-commits edits and publishes. | [#18](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/18) |\\n\\nDirect Codex uses a workspace-write sandbox with network disabled and no bypass/full-access mode. Direct Claude uses consented `dontAsk` tool rules, not that same OS sandbox. Both receive Tally's exact verification script; Tally handles publishing. Usage and trace evidence are separate: a worker can be metered without having delivered its neatlogs trace.\\n\\n## Troubleshooting\\n\\n| Symptom | What to do |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Agent missing or signed out | Install the selected CLI and run `claude auth login` or `codex login`, then doctor. Tally stops rather than substituting another harness. Login alone does not establish entitlement to a particular model. |\\n| No test command found | Supply your repository's real verification command with interactive init or `--test-command '...'`. `--yes` without a detected/supplied command fails. Do not guess `npm test` for a static repository. |\\n| Claude consent missing/outdated, or raw-command rules rejected | Rerun `tally init`, review the current script contents and approve the allow-list. Do not add broad Bash permissions or bypass flags. The old literal pipeline rules did not reliably authorize complex commands. |\\n| Worker tries a raw test pipeline or refuses an off-list command | Current prompts ask it to run the exact external script. Check `permissionWarnings` and the verdict; a recovered refusal is not automatically fatal. Do not loosen permissions blindly. |\\n| `ao` not on PATH in a normal terminal | Use direct, or set `TALLY_AO_BIN` to the installed AO executable. On macOS Tally also looks inside the Agent Orchestrator app bundle; doctor and init use the same resolver. |\\n| AO session stalls on setup/permissions | Check doctor and init's owned setup/permission configuration. Setup must happen before the worker. Chat-mode prompt behavior is not proof of unattended execution; use the configured/proven tui path and never bypass safety. |\\n| GitHub timeout/reset/5xx | Read-only intake and PR discovery retry network-class failures up to three attempts with a printed retry line. Auth/not-found errors and publishing writes are not blindly retried. Follow the surfaced `gh` diagnostic command; check auth/network before retrying a run that might already have published. |\\n| Plan has no tasks | Check `gh issue list --label tally --state open`, using your chosen label. Already-closing open PRs are excluded. A zero-task run prints the repository, label and issue-labeling command and starts no worker. |\\n| Larger-than-expected route or budget skip | Inspect named target files, confidence, test coverage, selected tiers and ceilings. `--budget` is a run cap; the monthly cfo.ai budget is separate. The next escalation ceiling must fit too. |\\n| `Entire checkpoint recall: unavailable` while Graph/capture are connected | Hosted history cannot see this repository. This does not block local checkpoints, Graph sizing, or normal runs. Mirror the repository only if you want hosted past-failure recall. |\\n| Entire verification mentions a baseline command failure | The baseline test runs on the base checkout before the candidate check. Inspect that base run separately; Tally records its nonzero exit in the attempt reason, then applies the candidate verdict against the recorded baseline. |\\n| Worker trace missing | Read `workerTraceDetail` in that attempt's ledger row and the Neatlogs integration detail in `run.json`. They distinguish pending spool batches/lock contention, missing credentials, API status codes, missing correlation, and spans still indexing. Tally retries a lock-contended drain once after all workers exit; if read-back still fails, check hook trust, project binding and read scopes, then use the exact `tally traces drain --run RUN_ID` command shown in the run details for finished-run recovery. |\\n| A configured model is rejected by the signed-in account | Tally records the rejection and tries the configured `modelAccessFallbackTier` once if its ceiling fits the remaining budget. Init defaults this to `cheap`; change the tier in `tally.yaml` or pass `--model-access-fallback-tier <name>`. This handles explicit model-access rejection only; fix authentication, network, quota and rate-limit errors at their source. |\\n| cfo.ai unreachable or write failed | Check `tally login cfoai` and doctor; review the displayed fallback/source. Keep the CSV fallback and reconcile before changing cells manually or rerunning work. |\\n| Dashboard still running after completion | Expected: inspect the saved run, then Ctrl+C. Use `--port 0` if the default port is occupied. |\\n| Init fails after an approved operation | Read its explicit changed/unwritten list and recovery command. Re-init preserves owned matching files; it refuses to overwrite unrelated edits. Optional integration side effects have their own approvals. |\\n\\nNever paste credentials into issue bodies, configs or diagnostic reports. Tally loads secrets from `~/.tally/.env`; provider logins remain owned by their CLIs.\\n\\n## What Is and Is Not Proven\\n\\n- Tally never merges task PRs; review them yourself. Its optional Entire setup flow may publish capture settings to the default branch only under a separate, explicit approval.\\n- Verification is only as strong as your configured test command. Syntax validation passing does not prove runtime behavior, accessibility or documentation accuracy.\\n- All four individual runner paths above are proven, but **mixed AO with an actual Codex spawn after Claude, parallel workers, and direct-runner escalation are not yet proven live**. Tests and simulated runs are not that proof.\\n- AO same-branch stop/escalation has real evidence; direct implementations have fake-port coverage but not equivalent live escalation proof.\\n- Direct live metering depends on harness event timing; Codex's rollout JSONL is an internal format that may change. Missing metering stops the worker. A direct process cannot be reattached after Tally exits.\\n- Past-failure recall needs hosted repository mirroring/access. Entire capture and Graph availability do not establish it, and Brain is not part of the current Tally integration.\\n- Paid AO experiments require explicit `--yes --budget <usd>` consent and a test timeout; they verify without pushing or opening PRs. Real comparison safety monitoring is not yet proven. Do not treat `tally demo` or an experiment dry run as evidence of real savings for your repository.\\n\\n## Uninstall\\n\\nIn each repository you initialised:\\n\\n```sh\\ntally uninstall\\n```\\n\\nIt lists owned config/installation files and AO registration/hooks/settings, then asks default-No confirmation. It refuses to overwrite user changes or remove a project with active sessions. If you edited the config after init, preserve it and follow the refusal message rather than deleting ownership records.\\n\\nRun history, credentials, optional integration data and globally installed hooks are not removed by this repository uninstall. Review separately consented global hooks through their own tools; do not assume uninstall reverted them.\\n\\nTo remove the CLI itself:\\n\\n```sh\\nnpm uninstall -g tally-agents\\n```\\n\\nYour existing PRs are not closed or merged, and your saved run evidence remains yours.\\n\"}\n{\"chunk_id\":\"58668d\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000005792,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":2513,\"output\":\"/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/dashboard-priorities-2.md:18:- Under each group, its attempts as today's ledger rows (tier, model, tokens, cost against ceiling, result, evidence).\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/site-brief-v2.md:49:   Fill each with real evidence only. Verified facts you may use: four runner paths each proven by a real pull request on `Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup` (Codex via AO PR #10 $0.0121; Claude Code via AO PR #13 $0.0871; Claude Code direct PR #14 $0.0765; Codex direct PR #18 $0.0109; costs are estimates at list price); stop at the ceiling and escalate proven once (fly-doom-jev PR #14); cfo.ai spend matched the ledger to the cent on each run; decision and worker traces read back from neatlogs; `tally-agents` 0.1.0 on npm; run `npm test` for the current test count. Where evidence does not exist yet (video, public posts, a business plan in cfo.ai, a measured baseline saving), leave a clearly marked empty slot rather than inventing anything. Do not state a savings percentage.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-terminal-video-prompt.md:57:   #17: small (confidence 0.74) -> cheap codex/gpt-5.6-luna ceiling $0.30\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-terminal-video-prompt.md:58:   Highlight \\\"small\\\", then \\\"cheap\\\", then \\\"ceiling $0.30\\\" in turn.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-terminal-video-prompt.md:59:   Caption: \\\"Each issue goes to the cheapest model that can do it, with a spending ceiling.\\\"\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/chip-design-tasks.md:44:Nothing starts. It prints each task's size, tier, ceiling and reason. Check that before the real run:\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/chip-design-tasks.md:53:- Task 2 names several files, so it should measure larger and score lower confidence. It may start on a dearer tier, or start cheap and escalate if the attempt fails or hits its ceiling. Either is correct behaviour.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/first-user-flow-brief.md:26:4. When `--plan` is clean and shows both issues with a size, tier, ceiling and reason, STOP and report to the orchestrator with the plan output. Do not start the real run. The human decides that.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/site-brief.md:29:7. **Proof.** Real numbers only, each linked: four runner paths each proven by a real run on `Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup`: Codex via AO (PR #10, $0.012), Claude Code via AO (PR #13, $0.087), Claude Code direct (PR #14, $0.077), Codex direct (PR #18, $0.011). Stop-at-ceiling and escalation proven once (fly-doom-jev PR #14). 662 tests. State nothing that is not in the README.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-demo-guide.md:38:A banner says \\\"Simulated run: the workers are scripted and no money is spent.\\\" That is accurate: in the demo only the worker, its token use and the test result are faked. The sizing, routing, ceilings, the stop and the escalation come from the same code a real run uses.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-demo-guide.md:46:| T5 | Starts on middle with a $2.00 cap, crosses it, is **stopped**, then continues on the top tier and passes. | The ceiling is enforced, and a stopped task is recovered by a stronger model. This is the key scene. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-demo-guide.md:58:The counts differ because of T5. It is one task but two attempts: the middle-tier attempt that was stopped at its ceiling, and the top-tier attempt that finished it. Both cost money, so both are in the ledger. Add up the ledger and you get exactly the budget spent.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-demo-guide.md:71:| B | `neatlogs-hack-5` | `agent/` (sizer, router, ceiling watcher, escalation), plus the neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai adapters |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-demo-guide.md:81:4. kills the worker if the cost crosses its ceiling (`ao session kill <id>`);\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-demo-guide.md:90:| neatlogs | A trace for every CFO decision (size, route, ceiling check, escalation). | Adapter merged. One live decision trace was sent successfully (trace id `125ffaeea327b3b60a98c694804263b2`). Off in the demo config, so the dashboard shows it as off. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-remaining.md:42:Not proven: a real Codex spawn after a Claude one in a mixed AO project; escalation and stop-at-ceiling on the direct runner; the cause of one missing Claude worker trace (third direct run).\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:11:- **One run loop.** Intake, sizing, routing, ceiling watcher, test verification, one escalation, ledger. A dry run uses the same loop with only the worker faked.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:12:- **CFO agent.** Sizer, price history with a no-data prior, routing rule with budget check and a one-sentence reason, ceiling watcher, escalation, baseline mode.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:13:- **Adapters.** Agent Orchestrator (spawn, poll, usage, kill), GitHub issue intake with a YAML fallback, test verifier, neatlogs trace, entire-graph sizing, file price history, cfo.ai budget.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:50:| B2 | **Forced failure and handoff.** Run one medium task with a ceiling set far too low. | The worker is stopped at the ceiling, and a stronger model continues on the same branch from the handoff summary and finishes. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:51:| B3 | **Worker setup per task.** Each worker checkout needs `make setup` (about a minute, 450 MB). Run it before the worker starts and keep its cost out of the task's ceiling. | A real task does not fail or get stopped because of environment setup. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:55:| B7 | **Routing from history.** Replace the fixed prior with real cost and pass rate per task size and model from earlier runs. Recompute the cheap threshold from that data. | After two runs, at least one routing decision cites history, and the threshold is no longer the hand-set 0.65. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:69:| N3 | Read history back from neatlogs (cost and outcome per past run) to feed B7. Needs a service-account token from you. | Routing history comes from neatlogs; the local file remains the fallback. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:125:| F2 | **Live view**: tasks appear as running, with cost climbing toward the ceiling, while a real run is in progress. | Watching a real run needs no refresh. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:128:| F5 | **Impact and history** per task: graph numbers (E3) and whether history changed the decision (B7, E6). | Visible on the task row. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:143:**Parked until the end, at your request (10 Oct).** What we learned about the account: it is a hackathon-issued IAM user (`hackathon-user-038`) under an organisation policy that explicitly denies creating access keys. `aws login` from the laptop returned 400, and CloudShell failed to start from the Global view (us-east-1 not yet tried). So Tally cannot deploy from the laptop with this account. H2 therefore changes: `tally publish` produces a ready-to-upload folder plus a short script, and the upload is done either in CloudShell or by hand in the AWS console (S3 static hosting, or Amplify Hosting with a dragged zip). Which services the account allows is unknown; ask the organisers.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:176:- **Before:** every task on the top model, no ceilings.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:191:| T4 | Forced failure | Stopped at the ceiling, continued by a stronger model from the handoff, finished. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:217:- **Time.** The list is long for the time left before the 12 Oct, 23:55 IST deadline. If it slips, cut in this order: Slack alert (not planned above), E6, C4, F8, B9's wizard prompts (keep a hand-written `tally.yaml`), a third repeat, N3 (keep the local history file), H3. Never cut B1, B2, the baseline comparison, or the evidence links: each is named evidence.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-final-plan.md:223:- **Environment setup per worker** on fly-doom-jev may dominate small tasks. B3 keeps it out of the ceiling, but it still costs wall-clock time in the experiment.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-video-kit.md:24:| C | Plan | `tally run --plan` | The task, its size, the tier chosen, the ceiling and the reason |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-video-kit.md:66:> **4. A real run (35 s).** Here's a real GitHub issue. Tally sizes it, picks the cheap tier, and sets a spending ceiling. The agent works on its own branch. You can watch the spend climb live. Tally runs the repo's tests, and only then opens the pull request. It never merges. If the cheap model had failed or hit its ceiling, Tally would have escalated on the same branch.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-video-kit.md:85:  install -> clip A; \\\"tally init\\\" and setup -> clip B; sizing, tier, ceiling -> clip C;\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-video-kit.md:106:- Zoom in on what I am describing: the tier and ceiling in clip C, the spend line in clip D,\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-8/tally-video-kit.md:139:| The agent: handles a substantial task start to finish | 30% | Section 4: one real issue from plan to pull request, with the ceiling and tests visible |\\n\"}\n{\"chunk_id\":\"42726a\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000005667,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":83,\"output\":\"engine/doctor.ts:177:    const repositoryNotAvailable = /no matching repositories|not mirrored|not accessible in the current account/i.test(detail);\\nengine/doctor.ts:184:        : 'Check Entire checkpoint search and account access.',\\ncli/main.ts:182:                    market --plan checks free account pricing, without a search\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","create_time":1791834661.217051}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
