ot permitted. Exit code 0 did NOT mean task success. A live rollout count was observed at 13,615 input, 6,912 cached-input and 200 output. JSON usage arrived only at 41.459 s; process closed at 45.582 s. Final counts: 86,171 input including 75,264 cached, 1,191 output. Twelve queued neatlogs spans were observed.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/direct-runner-spike.md:46:Auto-review proof: edit at 7.221 s; make test passed at 9.647 s; sleep ended at 20.097 s; git add at 30.079 s; commit at 35.373 s. By then 14 JSON events had arrived and NONE contained usage. Live rollout totals increased from 13,331 input / 152 output to 26,840 / 226 and 40,449 / 301; cached totals 6,912, 19,968, 33,024. These are cumulative, not additive per poll. JSON usage arrived at 36.882 s; process closed at 42.529 s. Final: 96,506 input including 85,248 cached, 767 output. Seventeen queued spans were observed, trace id `c2313d2e166407437483b1ddf6069ebd`.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/final-020-ao-sanity-report.md:20:AO session timestamps observed after completion:\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/final-020-ao-sanity-report.md:86:Disk polling observed both tasks running and spend increasing before completion (e.g. 12:03:06 UTC total 0.00259816 USD, eventually 0.01934604). Active ledger rows are not present until attempts finish; live costs appear in run.json. These observations are filesystem reads, not an independent browser-render or HTTP-fetch test.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/ao-parallel-proof.md:31:Dashboard address printed immediately: `http://127.0.0.1:5203/?data=%2Fruns%2Flatest%2F`; HTTP 200 before first spawn. latest held this run while tasks were still pending. Read-only dashboard JSON observations show all three tasks running at 19:04:01.705Z. Combined live spend increased through $0.003044440, $0.005410400, $0.006890880, $0.020295120 and $0.041119320. Each ledger row appeared as its attempt finished, before the other tasks were done; every observed JSON response parsed successfully.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/comparison-results.md:30:- Single sample per task/mode; no confidence intervals or repeat-spread claim. Installed routing requires two observations before using statistics; learned caps require five successful samples for size+tier+actual model. C small/medium initially have only one relevant sample and still use priors; do not claim learned rerouting or caps without evidence.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/part1-spike-findings.md:12:{sessionId, incomplete, totals:{inputTokens, cachedInputTokens, uncachedInputTokens, outputTokens, processedTokens, cacheReadTokens, estimatedCost}, harnesses:[{harness, totals, models:[{modelId, totals}]}]}. estimatedCost = null or {totalNanos, inputNanos, cachedInputNanos, outputNanos, coverage:\"complete\"|\"partial\", providerAttribution:\"observed\"}; unit is nanos of USD (1e9 = $1). List form /api/v1/usage/sessions -> {sessions:[{sessionId, processedTokens, totalTokens, incomplete, estimatedCost}]}.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/video-walkthrough.md:67:The replay core linearly interpolates cost and tokens from final ledger totals, not historical per-poll measurements. The current page can substitute a ceiling-based live estimate while a replay row is running (observed on the four-task replay); treat that intermediate amount as an illustration, not a recorded measurement. This display discrepancy has been routed to lane C. The page labels this as replay; nothing is running now. Completed values are exactly the saved ledger at the end.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/claude-direct-proof.md:59:At 10:24:09.957 and 10:24:16.717 Claude requested the **on-list** command `find js -name '*.js' -print0 | xargs -0 -n1 node --check`. Both were denied in dontAsk mode. Worker also requested `git status` and read-only `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard`; neither emitted a denial in the observed transcript. No git push, gh or network command was attempted. This proof did not independently exercise a refused off-list write/network command. The pipeline permission defect is verified; its exact parser cause is not yet proven. Do not solve it by granting broad Bash permissions or weakening dontAsk.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/claude-direct-proof.md:145:| a: dashboard before worker | Pass | HTTP 200 observed at 11:17:32.809Z, before worker attempt start at 11:17:47.639Z. |\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/claude-direct-proof.md:158:261,840 tokens: 105 uncached input, 241,369 cache-read, 15,598 one-hour cache-write, zero five-minute cache-write, 4,768 output. Cost **$0.079277900** = $0.000105 + $0.0241369 + $0.031196 (1h writes at 2x input) + $0.02384. Cache writes priced and persisted, live usage from the Claude JSON stream at message boundaries, not an invoice. Setup duration zero; Tally verifier time not applicable because commit gate stopped the task. The observed successful Bash script turnarounds were 2.227s and 1.445s, including tool/hook overhead rather than an isolated host-verifier measurement.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/claude-direct-proof.md:178:- Claude tracing hooks synchronously call their own OTLP exporter; Tally's worker spool drain handles Codex queued spans, not these Claude hook exports. The hook source awaits its flush. The worker completed normally and was not killed while active. No late third-run trace appeared in repeated read-backs, so no observed evidence supports a drain-timing explanation.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/claude-direct-proof.md:192:- (a) PASS: dashboard address printed first; HTTP 200 observed at 12:38:14.773Z, before worker started at 12:38:22.345Z.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/first-user-flow-final-run.md:131:| c. Dashboard running state and increasing spend | PASS | Repeated HTTP reads of run.json and ledger.json at half-second intervals. #9 running: `09:22:41.032Z` $0; `09:22:50.594Z` $0.00351720; `09:22:55.637Z` $0.00592932; `09:23:00.684Z` $0.00764112; `09:23:10.771Z` $0.01037548; `09:23:20.875Z` $0.01207248. Ledger was empty while active, then had its passed row at `09:23:48.771Z`; finished JSON observed at `09:23:58.398Z`. |\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/codex-direct-proof.md:36:Worker rollout confirms exact external verification script first (13:03:12.699Z), repository reads, new document, then exact script again (13:03:31.929Z). Worker did not run git add/commit/push or raw verification pipeline. It left edits uncommitted; Tally committed them and ran the real configured command independently before publishing. No observed permission prompt/denial, no bypass flag. Scope warnings empty. Setup duration 0 ms; independent verifier duration is not stored, so not invented. Syntax validation checks JavaScript, not all Markdown semantics.\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/cap-and-escalate-recipe.md:72:observed live. This recipe installs it. If still missing, disclose it and use:\n"},"started_at_ms":1791834667344,"completed_at_ms":1791834667344}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:51:07.488Z","ordinal":17127,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-ee62674e-bb6e-4fd4-b4d2-a08e995cf21a","process_id":"92294","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '350,620p' README.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '350,620p' README.md","name":"README.md","path":"README.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"| `tally market --jobs --limit 25 --max-credits 1` | Find engineering posts with explicit coding-tool evidence; group by posting company and label hiring intent, never confirmed adoption. |\n| `tally market --replay .tally/market/latest/query.json --output /tmp/tally-market-replay` | Render the saved snapshot again with no API calls or credentials. |\n| `tally publish --out site` | Static dashboard export with a sensitive-content scan. No deployment. |\n\n`run --dashboard` starts serving before the first task and prints the URL\nimmediately. `--open` also opens that URL in the system browser. The server\ncontinues after the run for inspection, until Ctrl+C. Terminal output includes\nroutes, worker session IDs, spend updates at most every ten seconds, attempt\nresults, PR URLs and a final summary. `--budget` is a cap for this run, not a\nreplacement for the monthly budget: Tally applies the lower of the run cap and\nthe remaining monthly budget, including for the direct sequential runner. Plan\nand summary show both, including the amount available now and monthly source.\nWhen cfo.ai supplies the monthly budget, init asks only for a local fallback\nafter connecting it. Worker trace IDs require correlated trace read-back;\nunverified delivery is shown as missing rather than success. See\n[Human approval points](docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md) for operations that still\nrequire explicit user consent or review.\n\n### Pilot Discovery\n\n`tally market` finds and qualifies likely pilot companies, not adoption or market\nsize. The default hypothesis is Software Development companies with 20-500\nemployees. It does not infer coding-agent seats or use from headcount. An optional\n`--keyword` filters an exact phrase in the company description, which can also\nmatch vendors and does not prove adoption.\n\nLive searches require `CRUSTDATA_API_KEY` in `~/.tally/.env`. Before each single\nbatch Tally checks free account pricing and prints a conservative maximum estimate\nincluding premium filters and returned fields. `--max-credits` (default 10)\nrefuses an estimated over-cap search; it is a per-call estimate, not a server-side\nhard billing limit. No pagination, enrichment or retries occur. Use `--plan` to\ncheck pricing without a paid search. `--dry-run` requires cached `--replay`.\n\nThe output directory contains the exact `query.json`, `companies.json`, CSV and\n`report.md`: API version, query hash, retrieval timestamp, returned versus API\nmatch count, reported credits, and record-update dates when available. These\ndates are not publication dates. Cached replay preserves provenance and calls\nno API, even without credentials; it does not rerun the search for fresh data.\nLegacy snapshots without matching provenance must be recaptured rather than\nsilently treated as new evidence.\n\nReview a saved snapshot with `--replay <query.json> --review <reviews.json>`.\nReviews are an array of `{ id, label, reason, sourceUrl, sourceDate, excerpt? }`;\nlabels are `candidate`, `vendor`, `hiring intent`, `verified use`, or `rejected`.\nUse `null` for unknown source dates. Intent/use labels require a sourced excerpt;\nTally checks the structure, not the truth of manual judgements. Without a review,\nevery returned row remains an unverified candidate. JSON/CSV retain all rows;\nthe short Markdown report shows at most ten. No deduplication or extrapolation\nis applied to company search.\n\nThe stronger second stage is `--jobs`: a single batch (default 25) of engineering\nposts from the past 90 days whose descriptions mention Claude Code, Cursor,\nGitHub Copilot, Codex or AI coding agents. It uses Job Search, with no premium\ncompany filters. Ambiguous Cursor/Codex mentions need nearby AI/coding context;\nrows missing title, company identity, source URL or date are dropped. Accepted\nrows are grouped by normalized domain (or company id) and labelled hiring intent.\nThat is evidence in a job advertisement, not a verified deployment, current open\nrole, buyer or purchase commitment. Posting entities can be staffing companies;\ncross-posts under different domains are not assumed to be the same buyer.\nDates are the API's `metadata.date_added`, not independently verified publication\ndates. JSON retains grouped job evidence and CSV includes title/excerpt.\n\nUse `--jobs --secondary <saved-company-query.json>` to include a previously\nreviewed firmographic snapshot as a secondary table without re-fetching it.\nThe job snapshot embeds that secondary evidence, so `--replay <job-query.json>`\nlater renders both tables with no API calls. Query match counts remain database\ncounts, never adoption or market size.\n\n## Routing Evidence\n\nSee the [Tally business plan](docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md) for share-of-savings pricing,\nthe measured three-task comparison, explicitly labelled unit-economics and\npilot-funnel assumptions, cfo.ai usage and the remaining commercial risks.\n\nFrom a source checkout, build and analyze an audited list of real run directories:\n\n```sh\nnpm run build\nnode scripts/routing-evidence.mjs --manifest runs.json --out /tmp/routing-evidence\n```\n\n`runs.json` is a JSON array of paths containing `run.json` and `ledger.json`.\nThe script writes a Markdown report, task CSV, JSON provenance and three SVG\ncharts. It excludes simulations and active runs, keeps skipped/interrupted\noutcomes in the denominator, and labels missing historical signals as missing.\nUnattributed historical decisions are not silently assigned to prior or\ntrace-driven cohorts. Correlations are descriptive, not cost predictions.\n\nAdd `--live-traces` for bounded read-only neatlogs verification using Tally's\nread token and project id. Without that flag it only replays the output folder's\ncached `trace-readback.json`, making no API call. Optional `--issues issues.json`\naccepts a URL-keyed map of current issue titles/bodies for historical runs;\nthese are labelled current text, not run-start snapshots. No paid workers or\ncfo.ai writes are performed. Before/after savings require matched measured\ntasks; missing baseline or v2 cohorts are reported as **not measured**.\n\nFor the matched three-pass experiment, add `--comparison-dir <folder>`.\nThe folder contains `mentor-comparison-plan-signals.json` and, when results\narrive, `comparison-passes.json`: an object mapping `A`, `B`, `C` to arrays of\nreal run-directory paths (absolute or relative to that folder). A is top-tier\nbaseline, B is Tally v1/priors, C is Tally v2/trace history. Missing passes stay\npending. The report joins signals only at the exact pinned base SHA and task\ntext, reports cost/verdict/attempts per task and per pass, and charts sizing\nagainst real costs with per-pass correlations. A pass C label does not itself\nprove that history influenced every route or that any cap was learned.\nUse `--comparison-passes <json>` to keep the labelled path manifest elsewhere\nwithout changing the measured signals folder.\n\n## Layout\n\nRouting history is isolated by task size, tier name and actual model id. Changing\na tier's model does not reuse the old model's pass rate or learned cap. Legacy\nhistory without an explicit model remains available for audit but is excluded\nfrom model-specific decisions; these fall back to the prior/configured cap\nuntil enough new matching observations exist. History trace names include the\nmodel id so the public neatlogs read API can identify it without hidden attributes.\n\nExperiment summaries are atomically updated after each completed run at\n`.tally/runs/experiments/<experimentId>/summary.json`. The dashboard server lists\nthem in `/runs/index.json` under `experiments`. Each underlying run retains its\nown folder, `mode`, and `repeat`. Simulated cost remains labeled as simulated.\nThe aggregate watcher polls once a second; reported final cost can exceed the\nstop threshold due to polling or metering lag and is never clipped to the cap.\n\n| Folder | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `schema/` | The contract every part reads: TypeScript types, JSON Schema, validators, and a sample finished run in `schema/fixtures/finished-run/`. |\n| `engine/` | The run loop, run state and ledger. |\n| `adapters/` | One thin module per outside tool (Agent Orchestrator, Entire, neatlogs, cfo.ai, GitHub). |\n| `agent/` | The CFO agent: sizing, routing, ceilings, escalation. |\n| `cli/` | The `tally` command. |\n| `dashboard/` | Static page that reads the ledger and run state. |\n| `demo/` | Sample backlog and scripted outcomes for `tally demo`. |\n\n## Configuration\n\nWith neatlogs on, Tally drains the completed Codex worker's hook spool from\nits own process, outside the worker sandbox. It uses the official package's\nspool API (there is no flush CLI), matching native session IDs or the worktree\npath. Only successfully delivered batches are acknowledged; failed,\nunrelated and malformed batches remain queued. A locked or incomplete drain\nis reported as missing worker trace delivery. After all workers exit, Tally\nretries once when a worker drain reported lock contention and verifies the\nresult with Neatlogs before updating the final run status. If delivery remains\nunverified, the run keeps the batches and shows `tally traces drain --run <id>`\nfor recovery. Dry runs do not drain workers.\n\nWith `integrations.entire: true`, enable Entire in the target repository and\nreview and trust its hooks in the coding agent before starting workers. Tally records\nthe task commit's `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer and its native `entireSessionId`\nseparately from the AO `sessionId`. Logs-only checkpoints retain conversation\ncontext; escalation defaults to a metadata-only handoff containing the\ncheckpoint/session IDs, branch, recorded and changed files, attempt verdict,\nand task text. It does not generate a summary or export a transcript.\n`entire.generatedSummaries: true` explicitly opts into Entire's summary\nprovider (`checkpoint explain --generate`), which may export session content;\nthis setting is off by default and requires the user's approval.\nWhen Tally publishes a verified branch, it also pushes the checkpoint refs\nnamed by its new commits to `origin` and to an `entire` remote when configured.\nCheckpoint upload failures are reported as publishing failures. Dry runs do\nnot publish branches or checkpoints.\n\nEntire checkpoint records live in separate Git refs, not on the task branch.\nTally keeps the checkpoint ID and native session ID, and attaches a clickable\nEntire session link only when the Entire CLI confirms that the GitHub repo has\na ready mirror. The link opens the session that contains the checkpoint; a\nreviewer still needs access to that repo in Entire. Without a ready mirror,\nTally keeps the checkpoint ID available in the local ledger rather than adding\na link that may not open.\n\nAfter each run has completed its Neatlogs read-back, Tally adds an evidence\nsection to the generated PR body when links are available: Entire session and\ncheckpoint, Neatlogs routing and worker traces, and the configured cfo.ai\nbudget and spend tables. Worker trace links are added only after Tally verifies\ntheir read-back. These dashboard links use the viewer's existing account\naccess; Tally does not publish public trace shares.\n\nTally assumes nothing about the repository it works on. The repository, test\ncommand, model tiers, list prices, ceilings and budget all come from\n`tally.yaml` (a tier is a name, harness, model id, provider, list price and ceiling share); see `schema/fixtures/finished-run/tally.yaml` for a complete\nexample and `schema/tally-config.schema.json` for the rules.\n\nMoney in the ledger and run state is stored as integer nanos of USD\n(1 USD = 1,000,000,000). List-price estimates multiply aggregate tokens by the\nconfigured rates in `tally.yaml`; they are normalized estimates, not invoices.\nNew ledger rows snapshot those rates in `price` and record their source and\nverification status in `priceNote`. Unknown models/providers or rates that\ndisagree with the verified snapshot are labelled unverified. Operator-supplied\n`tiers[].priceNote` is retained as a config note, not independently verified.\n\n### Codex Tier Prices\n\nOfficial base text rates checked 2026-10-11, USD per million tokens:\n\n| Model | Input | Cached Input | Output | Source |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| gpt-5.6-luna | 0.20 | 0.02 | 1.20 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-luna) |\n| gpt-5.6-terra | 2.00 | 0.20 | 12.00 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-terra) |\n| gpt-5.6-sol | 4.00 | 0.40 | 20.00 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-sol) |\n\n```yaml\ntiers:\n  - { name: cheap, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-luna, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 0.2, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.02, outputUsdPerMTok: 1.2 }, ceilingFraction: 0.2 }\n  - { name: middle, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-terra, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 2, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.2, outputUsdPerMTok: 12 }, ceilingFraction: 0.5 }\n  - { name: top, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-sol, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 4, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.4, outputUsdPerMTok: 20 }, ceilingFraction: 1 }\n```\n\nPer-request prompts above 272K input tokens have input/output uplifts;\ncache writes also have an uplift. AO's aggregate token buckets cannot resolve\nthese adjustments. Sol's published promotional rates last at least through\n2026-11-21; recheck before subsequent runs. Historical costs are not repriced.\n\nFor dry runs, set `backlog.source: file` and point `backlog.file` at YAML like:\n\n```yaml\ntasks:\n  - id: T1\n    title: Add validation for empty player name\n    size: small\n    outcomes: [passed]\n  - id: T2\n    title: Persist settings after restart\n    size: medium\n    outcomes: [stopped, passed]\n```\n\n`outcomes` is optional and defaults to one passed simulated attempt.\n\nThe latest run is also mirrored to `.tally/runs/latest/run.json` and\n`.tally/runs/latest/ledger.json` so the dashboard has one stable path to poll.\n\n## Secrets\n\nKeys live in `~/.tally/.env`, outside every repository, and are loaded from\nthere. Never put them in this repo; `.env` files are git-ignored.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"| `tally market --jobs --limit 25 --max-credits 1` | Find engineering posts with explicit coding-tool evidence; group by posting company and label hiring intent, never confirmed adoption. |\n| `tally market --replay .tally/market/latest/query.json --output /tmp/tally-market-replay` | Render the saved snapshot again with no API calls or credentials. |\n| `tally publish --out site` | Static dashboard export with a sensitive-content scan. No deployment. |\n\n`run --dashboard` starts serving before the first task and prints the URL\nimmediately. `--open` also opens that URL in the system browser. The server\ncontinues after the run for inspection, until Ctrl+C. Terminal output includes\nroutes, worker session IDs, spend updates at most every ten seconds, attempt\nresults, PR URLs and a final summary. `--budget` is a cap for this run, not a\nreplacement for the monthly budget: Tally applies the lower of the run cap and\nthe remaining monthly budget, including for the direct sequential runner. Plan\nand summary show both, including the amount available now and monthly source.\nWhen cfo.ai supplies the monthly budget, init asks only for a local fallback\nafter connecting it. Worker trace IDs require correlated trace read-back;\nunverified delivery is shown as missing rather than success. See\n[Human approval points](docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md) for operations that still\nrequire explicit user consent or review.\n\n### Pilot Discovery\n\n`tally market` finds and qualifies likely pilot companies, not adoption or market\nsize. The default hypothesis is Software Development companies with 20-500\nemployees. It does not infer coding-agent seats or use from headcount. An optional\n`--keyword` filters an exact phrase in the company description, which can also\nmatch vendors and does not prove adoption.\n\nLive searches require `CRUSTDATA_API_KEY` in `~/.tally/.env`. Before each single\nbatch Tally checks free account pricing and prints a conservative maximum estimate\nincluding premium filters and returned fields. `--max-credits` (default 10)\nrefuses an estimated over-cap search; it is a per-call estimate, not a server-side\nhard billing limit. No pagination, enrichment or retries occur. Use `--plan` to\ncheck pricing without a paid search. `--dry-run` requires cached `--replay`.\n\nThe output directory contains the exact `query.json`, `companies.json`, CSV and\n`report.md`: API version, query hash, retrieval timestamp, returned versus API\nmatch count, reported credits, and record-update dates when available. These\ndates are not publication dates. Cached replay preserves provenance and calls\nno API, even without credentials; it does not rerun the search for fresh data.\nLegacy snapshots without matching provenance must be recaptured rather than\nsilently treated as new evidence.\n\nReview a saved snapshot with `--replay <query.json> --review <reviews.json>`.\nReviews are an array of `{ id, label, reason, sourceUrl, sourceDate, excerpt? }`;\nlabels are `candidate`, `vendor`, `hiring intent`, `verified use`, or `rejected`.\nUse `null` for unknown source dates. Intent/use labels require a sourced excerpt;\nTally checks the structure, not the truth of manual judgements. Without a review,\nevery returned row remains an unverified candidate. JSON/CSV retain all rows;\nthe short Markdown report shows at most ten. No deduplication or extrapolation\nis applied to company search.\n\nThe stronger second stage is `--jobs`: a single batch (default 25) of engineering\nposts from the past 90 days whose descriptions mention Claude Code, Cursor,\nGitHub Copilot, Codex or AI coding agents. It uses Job Search, with no premium\ncompany filters. Ambiguous Cursor/Codex mentions need nearby AI/coding context;\nrows missing title, company identity, source URL or date are dropped. Accepted\nrows are grouped by normalized domain (or company id) and labelled hiring intent.\nThat is evidence in a job advertisement, not a verified deployment, current open\nrole, buyer or purchase commitment. Posting entities can be staffing companies;\ncross-posts under different domains are not assumed to be the same buyer.\nDates are the API's `metadata.date_added`, not independently verified publication\ndates. JSON retains grouped job evidence and CSV includes title/excerpt.\n\nUse `--jobs --secondary <saved-company-query.json>` to include a previously\nreviewed firmographic snapshot as a secondary table without re-fetching it.\nThe job snapshot embeds that secondary evidence, so `--replay <job-query.json>`\nlater renders both tables with no API calls. Query match counts remain database\ncounts, never adoption or market size.\n\n## Routing Evidence\n\nSee the [Tally business plan](docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md) for share-of-savings pricing,\nthe measured three-task comparison, explicitly labelled unit-economics and\npilot-funnel assumptions, cfo.ai usage and the remaining commercial risks.\n\nFrom a source checkout, build and analyze an audited list of real run directories:\n\n```sh\nnpm run build\nnode scripts/routing-evidence.mjs --manifest runs.json --out /tmp/routing-evidence\n```\n\n`runs.json` is a JSON array of paths containing `run.json` and `ledger.json`.\nThe script writes a Markdown report, task CSV, JSON provenance and three SVG\ncharts. It excludes simulations and active runs, keeps skipped/interrupted\noutcomes in the denominator, and labels missing historical signals as missing.\nUnattributed historical decisions are not silently assigned to prior or\ntrace-driven cohorts. Correlations are descriptive, not cost predictions.\n\nAdd `--live-traces` for bounded read-only neatlogs verification using Tally's\nread token and project id. Without that flag it only replays the output folder's\ncached `trace-readback.json`, making no API call. Optional `--issues issues.json`\naccepts a URL-keyed map of current issue titles/bodies for historical runs;\nthese are labelled current text, not run-start snapshots. No paid workers or\ncfo.ai writes are performed. Before/after savings require matched measured\ntasks; missing baseline or v2 cohorts are reported as **not measured**.\n\nFor the matched three-pass experiment, add `--comparison-dir <folder>`.\nThe folder contains `mentor-comparison-plan-signals.json` and, when results\narrive, `comparison-passes.json`: an object mapping `A`, `B`, `C` to arrays of\nreal run-directory paths (absolute or relative to that folder). A is top-tier\nbaseline, B is Tally v1/priors, C is Tally v2/trace history. Missing passes stay\npending. The report joins signals only at the exact pinned base SHA and task\ntext, reports cost/verdict/attempts per task and per pass, and charts sizing\nagainst real costs with per-pass correlations. A pass C label does not itself\nprove that history influenced every route or that any cap was learned.\nUse `--comparison-passes <json>` to keep the labelled path manifest elsewhere\nwithout changing the measured signals folder.\n\n## Layout\n\nRouting history is isolated by task size, tier name and actual model id. Changing\na tier's model does not reuse the old model's pass rate or learned cap. Legacy\nhistory without an explicit model remains available for audit but is excluded\nfrom model-specific decisions; these fall back to the prior/configured cap\nuntil enough new matching observations exist. History trace names include the\nmodel id so the public neatlogs read API can identify it without hidden attributes.\n\nExperiment summaries are atomically updated after each completed run at\n`.tally/runs/experiments/<experimentId>/summary.json`. The dashboard server lists\nthem in `/runs/index.json` under `experiments`. Each underlying run retains its\nown folder, `mode`, and `repeat`. Simulated cost remains labeled as simulated.\nThe aggregate watcher polls once a second; reported final cost can exceed the\nstop threshold due to polling or metering lag and is never clipped to the cap.\n\n| Folder | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `schema/` | The contract every part reads: TypeScript types, JSON Schema, validators, and a sample finished run in `schema/fixtures/finished-run/`. |\n| `engine/` | The run loop, run state and ledger. |\n| `adapters/` | One thin module per outside tool (Agent Orchestrator, Entire, neatlogs, cfo.ai, GitHub). |\n| `agent/` | The CFO agent: sizing, routing, ceilings, escalation. |\n| `cli/` | The `tally` command. |\n| `dashboard/` | Static page that reads the ledger and run state. |\n| `demo/` | Sample backlog and scripted outcomes for `tally demo`. |\n\n## Configuration\n\nWith neatlogs on, Tally drains the completed Codex worker's hook spool from\nits own process, outside the worker sandbox. It uses the official package's\nspool API (there is no flush CLI), matching native session IDs or the worktree\npath. Only successfully delivered batches are acknowledged; failed,\nunrelated and malformed batches remain queued. A locked or incomplete drain\nis reported as missing worker trace delivery. After all workers exit, Tally\nretries once when a worker drain reported lock contention and verifies the\nresult with Neatlogs before updating the final run status. If delivery remains\nunverified, the run keeps the batches and shows `tally traces drain --run <id>`\nfor recovery. Dry runs do not drain workers.\n\nWith `integrations.entire: true`, enable Entire in the target repository and\nreview and trust its hooks in the coding agent before starting workers. Tally records\nthe task commit's `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer and its native `entireSessionId`\nseparately from the AO `sessionId`. Logs-only checkpoints retain conversation\ncontext; escalation defaults to a metadata-only handoff containing the\ncheckpoint/session IDs, branch, recorded and changed files, attempt verdict,\nand task text. It does not generate a summary or export a transcript.\n`entire.generatedSummaries: true` explicitly opts into Entire's summary\nprovider (`checkpoint explain --generate`), which may export session content;\nthis setting is off by default and requires the user's approval.\nWhen Tally publishes a verified branch, it also pushes the checkpoint refs\nnamed by its new commits to `origin` and to an `entire` remote when configured.\nCheckpoint upload failures are reported as publishing failures. Dry runs do\nnot publish branches or checkpoints.\n\nEntire checkpoint records live in separate Git refs, not on the task branch.\nTally keeps the checkpoint ID and native session ID, and attaches a clickable\nEntire session link only when the Entire CLI confirms that the GitHub repo has\na ready mirror. The link opens the session that contains the checkpoint; a\nreviewer still needs access to that repo in Entire. Without a ready mirror,\nTally keeps the checkpoint ID available in the local ledger rather than adding\na link that may not open.\n\nAfter each run has completed its Neatlogs read-back, Tally adds an evidence\nsection to the generated PR body when links are available: Entire session and\ncheckpoint, Neatlogs routing and worker traces, and the configured cfo.ai\nbudget and spend tables. Worker trace links are added only after Tally verifies\ntheir read-back. These dashboard links use the viewer's existing account\naccess; Tally does not publish public trace shares.\n\nTally assumes nothing about the repository it works on. The repository, test\ncommand, model tiers, list prices, ceilings and budget all come from\n`tally.yaml` (a tier is a name, harness, model id, provider, list price and ceiling share); see `schema/fixtures/finished-run/tally.yaml` for a complete\nexample and `schema/tally-config.schema.json` for the rules.\n\nMoney in the ledger and run state is stored as integer nanos of USD\n(1 USD = 1,000,000,000). List-price estimates multiply aggregate tokens by the\nconfigured rates in `tally.yaml`; they are normalized estimates, not invoices.\nNew ledger rows snapshot those rates in `price` and record their source and\nverification status in `priceNote`. Unknown models/providers or rates that\ndisagree with the verified snapshot are labelled unverified. Operator-supplied\n`tiers[].priceNote` is retained as a config note, not independently verified.\n\n### Codex Tier Prices\n\nOfficial base text rates checked 2026-10-11, USD per million tokens:\n\n| Model | Input | Cached Input | Output | Source |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| gpt-5.6-luna | 0.20 | 0.02 | 1.20 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-luna) |\n| gpt-5.6-terra | 2.00 | 0.20 | 12.00 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-terra) |\n| gpt-5.6-sol | 4.00 | 0.40 | 20.00 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-sol) |\n\n```yaml\ntiers:\n  - { name: cheap, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-luna, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 0.2, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.02, outputUsdPerMTok: 1.2 }, ceilingFraction: 0.2 }\n  - { name: middle, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-terra, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 2, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.2, outputUsdPerMTok: 12 }, ceilingFraction: 0.5 }\n  - { name: top, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-sol, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 4, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.4, outputUsdPerMTok: 20 }, ceilingFraction: 1 }\n```\n\nPer-request prompts above 272K input tokens have input/output uplifts;\ncache writes also have an uplift. AO's aggregate token buckets cannot resolve\nthese adjustments. Sol's published promotional rates last at least through\n2026-11-21; recheck before subsequent runs. Historical costs are not repriced.\n\nFor dry runs, set `backlog.source: file` and point `backlog.file` at YAML like:\n\n```yaml\ntasks:\n  - id: T1\n    title: Add validation for empty player name\n    size: small\n    outcomes: [passed]\n  - id: T2\n    title: Persist settings after restart\n    size: medium\n    outcomes: [stopped, passed]\n```\n\n`outcomes` is optional and defaults to one passed simulated attempt.\n\nThe latest run is also mirrored to `.tally/runs/latest/run.json` and\n`.tally/runs/latest/ledger.json` so the dashboard has one stable path to poll.\n\n## Secrets\n\nKeys live in `~/.tally/.env`, outside every repository, and are loaded from\nthere. Never put them in this repo; `.env` files are git-ignored.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":5458},"formatted_output":"| `tally market --jobs --limit 25 --max-credits 1` | Find engineering posts with explicit coding-tool evidence; group by posting company and label hiring intent, never confirmed adoption. |\n| `tally market --replay .tally/market/latest/query.json --output /tmp/tally-market-replay` | Render the saved snapshot again with no API calls or credentials. |\n| `tally publish --out site` | Static dashboard export with a sensitive-content scan. No deployment. |\n\n`run --dashboard` starts serving before the first task and prints the URL\nimmediately. `--open` also opens that URL in the system browser. The server\ncontinues after the run for inspection, until Ctrl+C. Terminal output includes\nroutes, worker session IDs, spend updates at most every ten seconds, attempt\nresults, PR URLs and a final summary. `--budget` is a cap for this run, not a\nreplacement for the monthly budget: Tally applies the lower of the run cap and\nthe remaining monthly budget, including for the direct sequential runner. Plan\nand summary show both, including the amount available now and monthly source.\nWhen cfo.ai supplies the monthly budget, init asks only for a local fallback\nafter connecting it. Worker trace IDs require correlated trace read-back;\nunverified delivery is shown as missing rather than success. See\n[Human approval points](docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md) for operations that still\nrequire explicit user consent or review.\n\n### Pilot Discovery\n\n`tally market` finds and qualifies likely pilot companies, not adoption or market\nsize. The default hypothesis is Software Development companies with 20-500\nemployees. It does not infer coding-agent seats or use from headcount. An optional\n`--keyword` filters an exact phrase in the company description, which can also\nmatch vendors and does not prove adoption.\n\nLive searches require `CRUSTDATA_API_KEY` in `~/.tally/.env`. Before each single\nbatch Tally checks free account pricing and prints a conservative maximum estimate\nincluding premium filters and returned fields. `--max-credits` (default 10)\nrefuses an estimated over-cap search; it is a per-call estimate, not a server-side\nhard billing limit. No pagination, enrichment or retries occur. Use `--plan` to\ncheck pricing without a paid search. `--dry-run` requires cached `--replay`.\n\nThe output directory contains the exact `query.json`, `companies.json`, CSV and\n`report.md`: API version, query hash, retrieval timestamp, returned versus API\nmatch count, reported credits, and record-update dates when available. These\ndates are not publication dates. Cached replay preserves provenance and calls\nno API, even without credentials; it does not rerun the search for fresh data.\nLegacy snapshots without matching provenance must be recaptured rather than\nsilently treated as new evidence.\n\nReview a saved snapshot with `--replay <query.json> --review <reviews.json>`.\nReviews are an array of `{ id, label, reason, sourceUrl, sourceDate, excerpt? }`;\nlabels are `candidate`, `vendor`, `hiring intent`, `verified use`, or `rejected`.\nUse `null` for unknown source dates. Intent/use labels require a sourced excerpt;\nTally checks the structure, not the truth of manual judgements. Without a review,\nevery returned row remains an unverified candidate. JSON/CSV retain all rows;\nthe short Markdown report shows at most ten. No deduplication or extrapolation\nis applied to company search.\n\nThe stronger second stage is `--jobs`: a single batch (default 25) of engineering\nposts from the past 90 days whose descriptions mention Claude Code, Cursor,\nGitHub Copilot, Codex or AI coding agents. It uses Job Search, with no premium\ncompany filters. Ambiguous Cursor/Codex mentions need nearby AI/coding context;\nrows missing title, company identity, source URL or date are dropped. Accepted\nrows are grouped by normalized domain (or company id) and labelled hiring intent.\nThat is evidence in a job advertisement, not a verified deployment, current open\nrole, buyer or purchase commitment. Posting entities can be staffing companies;\ncross-posts under different domains are not assumed to be the same buyer.\nDates are the API's `metadata.date_added`, not independently verified publication\ndates. JSON retains grouped job evidence and CSV includes title/excerpt.\n\nUse `--jobs --secondary <saved-company-query.json>` to include a previously\nreviewed firmographic snapshot as a secondary table without re-fetching it.\nThe job snapshot embeds that secondary evidence, so `--replay <job-query.json>`\nlater renders both tables with no API calls. Query match counts remain database\ncounts, never adoption or market size.\n\n## Routing Evidence\n\nSee the [Tally business plan](docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md) for share-of-savings pricing,\nthe measured three-task comparison, explicitly labelled unit-economics and\npilot-funnel assumptions, cfo.ai usage and the remaining commercial risks.\n\nFrom a source checkout, build and analyze an audited list of real run directories:\n\n```sh\nnpm run build\nnode scripts/routing-evidence.mjs --manifest runs.json --out /tmp/routing-evidence\n```\n\n`runs.json` is a JSON array of paths containing `run.json` and `ledger.json`.\nThe script writes a Markdown report, task CSV, JSON provenance and three SVG\ncharts. It excludes simulations and active runs, keeps skipped/interrupted\noutcomes in the denominator, and labels missing historical signals as missing.\nUnattributed historical decisions are not silently assigned to prior or\ntrace-driven cohorts. Correlations are descriptive, not cost predictions.\n\nAdd `--live-traces` for bounded read-only neatlogs verification using Tally's\nread token and project id. Without that flag it only replays the output folder's\ncached `trace-readback.json`, making no API call. Optional `--issues issues.json`\naccepts a URL-keyed map of current issue titles/bodies for historical runs;\nthese are labelled current text, not run-start snapshots. No paid workers or\ncfo.ai writes are performed. Before/after savings require matched measured\ntasks; missing baseline or v2 cohorts are reported as **not measured**.\n\nFor the matched three-pass experiment, add `--comparison-dir <folder>`.\nThe folder contains `mentor-comparison-plan-signals.json` and, when results\narrive, `comparison-passes.json`: an object mapping `A`, `B`, `C` to arrays of\nreal run-directory paths (absolute or relative to that folder). A is top-tier\nbaseline, B is Tally v1/priors, C is Tally v2/trace history. Missing passes stay\npending. The report joins signals only at the exact pinned base SHA and task\ntext, reports cost/verdict/attempts per task and per pass, and charts sizing\nagainst real costs with per-pass correlations. A pass C label does not itself\nprove that history influenced every route or that any cap was learned.\nUse `--comparison-passes <json>` to keep the labelled path manifest elsewhere\nwithout changing the measured signals folder.\n\n## Layout\n\nRouting history is isolated by task size, tier name and actual model id. Changing\na tier's model does not reuse the old model's pass rate or learned cap. Legacy\nhistory without an explicit model remains available for audit but is excluded\nfrom model-specific decisions; these fall back to the prior/configured cap\nuntil enough new matching observations exist. History trace names include the\nmodel id so the public neatlogs read API can identify it without hidden attributes.\n\nExperiment summaries are atomically updated after each completed run at\n`.tally/runs/experiments/<experimentId>/summary.json`. The dashboard server lists\nthem in `/runs/index.json` under `experiments`. Each underlying run retains its\nown folder, `mode`, and `repeat`. Simulated cost remains labeled as simulated.\nThe aggregate watcher polls once a second; reported final cost can exceed the\nstop threshold due to polling or metering lag and is never clipped to the cap.\n\n| Folder | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `schema/` | The contract every part reads: TypeScript types, JSON Schema, validators, and a sample finished run in `schema/fixtures/finished-run/`. |\n| `engine/` | The run loop, run state and ledger. |\n| `adapters/` | One thin module per outside tool (Agent Orchestrator, Entire, neatlogs, cfo.ai, GitHub). |\n| `agent/` | The CFO agent: sizing, routing, ceilings, escalation. |\n| `cli/` | The `tally` command. |\n| `dashboard/` | Static page that reads the ledger and run state. |\n| `demo/` | Sample backlog and scripted outcomes for `tally demo`. |\n\n## Configuration\n\nWith neatlogs on, Tally drains the completed Codex worker's hook spool from\nits own process, outside the worker sandbox. It uses the official package's\nspool API (there is no flush CLI), matching native session IDs or the worktree\npath. Only successfully delivered batches are acknowledged; failed,\nunrelated and malformed batches remain queued. A locked or incomplete drain\nis reported as missing worker trace delivery. After all workers exit, Tally\nretries once when a worker drain reported lock contention and verifies the\nresult with Neatlogs before updating the final run status. If delivery remains\nunverified, the run keeps the batches and shows `tally traces drain --run <id>`\nfor recovery. Dry runs do not drain workers.\n\nWith `integrations.entire: true`, enable Entire in the target repository and\nreview and trust its hooks in the coding agent before starting workers. Tally records\nthe task commit's `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer and its native `entireSessionId`\nseparately from the AO `sessionId`. Logs-only checkpoints retain conversation\ncontext; escalation defaults to a metadata-only handoff containing the\ncheckpoint/session IDs, branch, recorded and changed files, attempt verdict,\nand task text. It does not generate a summary or export a transcript.\n`entire.generatedSummaries: true` explicitly opts into Entire's summary\nprovider (`checkpoint explain --generate`), which may export session content;\nthis setting is off by default and requires the user's approval.\nWhen Tally publishes a verified branch, it also pushes the checkpoint refs\nnamed by its new commits to `origin` and to an `entire` remote when configured.\nCheckpoint upload failures are reported as publishing failures. Dry runs do\nnot publish branches or checkpoints.\n\nEntire checkpoint records live in separate Git refs, not on the task branch.\nTally keeps the checkpoint ID and native session ID, and attaches a clickable\nEntire session link only when the Entire CLI confirms that the GitHub repo has\na ready mirror. The link opens the session that contains the checkpoint; a\nreviewer still needs access to that repo in Entire. Without a ready mirror,\nTally keeps the checkpoint ID available in the local ledger rather than adding\na link that may not open.\n\nAfter each run has completed its Neatlogs read-back, Tally adds an evidence\nsection to the generated PR body when links are available: Entire session and\ncheckpoint, Neatlogs routing and worker traces, and the configured cfo.ai\nbudget and spend tables. Worker trace links are added only after Tally verifies\ntheir read-back. These dashboard links use the viewer's existing account\naccess; Tally does not publish public trace shares.\n\nTally assumes nothing about the repository it works on. The repository, test\ncommand, model tiers, list prices, ceilings and budget all come from\n`tally.yaml` (a tier is a name, harness, model id, provider, list price and ceiling share); see `schema/fixtures/finished-run/tally.yaml` for a complete\nexample and `schema/tally-config.schema.json` for the rules.\n\nMoney in the ledger and run state is stored as integer nanos of USD\n(1 USD = 1,000,000,000). List-price estimates multiply aggregate tokens by the\nconfigured rates in `tally.yaml`; they are normalized estimates, not invoices.\nNew ledger rows snapshot those rates in `price` and record their source and\nverification status in `priceNote`. Unknown models/providers or rates that\ndisagree with the verified snapshot are labelled unverified. Operator-supplied\n`tiers[].priceNote` is retained as a config note, not independently verified.\n\n### Codex Tier Prices\n\nOfficial base text rates checked 2026-10-11, USD per million tokens:\n\n| Model | Input | Cached Input | Output | Source |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| gpt-5.6-luna | 0.20 | 0.02 | 1.20 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-luna) |\n| gpt-5.6-terra | 2.00 | 0.20 | 12.00 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-terra) |\n| gpt-5.6-sol | 4.00 | 0.40 | 20.00 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-sol) |\n\n```yaml\ntiers:\n  - { name: cheap, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-luna, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 0.2, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.02, outputUsdPerMTok: 1.2 }, ceilingFraction: 0.2 }\n  - { name: middle, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-terra, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 2, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.2, outputUsdPerMTok: 12 }, ceilingFraction: 0.5 }\n  - { name: top, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-sol, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 4, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.4, outputUsdPerMTok: 20 }, ceilingFraction: 1 }\n```\n\nPer-request prompts above 272K input tokens have input/output uplifts;\ncache writes also have an uplift. AO's aggregate token buckets cannot resolve\nthese adjustments. Sol's published promotional rates last at least through\n2026-11-21; recheck before subsequent runs. Historical costs are not repriced.\n\nFor dry runs, set `backlog.source: file` and point `backlog.file` at YAML like:\n\n```yaml\ntasks:\n  - id: T1\n    title: Add validation for empty player name\n    size: small\n    outcomes: [passed]\n  - id: T2\n    title: Persist settings after restart\n    size: medium\n    outcomes: [stopped, passed]\n```\n\n`outcomes` is optional and defaults to one passed simulated attempt.\n\nThe latest run is also mirrored to `.tally/runs/latest/run.json` and\n`.tally/runs/latest/ledger.json` so the dashboard has one stable path to poll.\n\n## Secrets\n\nKeys live in `~/.tally/.env`, outside every repository, and are loaded from\nthere. Never put them in this repo; `.env` files are git-ignored.\n"},"started_at_ms":1791834667488,"completed_at_ms":1791834667488}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:51:07.942Z","ordinal":17128,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-22a792eb-25e1-46c3-9d9d-6c4bd433b28d","process_id":"64834","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","npm run build"],"cwd":"file:///Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"npm run build"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"\n> tally-agents@0.2.1 build\n> tsc -p tsconfig.build.json\n\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"\n> tally-agents@0.2.1 build\n> tsc -p tsconfig.build.json\n\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":202872542},"formatted_output":"\n> tally-agents@0.2.1 build\n> tsc -p tsconfig.build.json\n\n"},"started_at_ms":1791834667739,"completed_at_ms":1791834667942}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:51:07.992Z","ordinal":17129,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a131ab-3bd7-79b2-8c1f-36ebffd58f06","call_id":"call_ca41539f4b26460b96304ef5b1b4ecdc","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.9 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"91c570\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000005583,\"exit_code\":2,\"original_token_count\":874,\"output\":\"rg: engine/agent-readiness.ts: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\nengine/init.ts:22:import { readAoDaemonStatus, type AoDaemonStatus } from './ao-status.js';\\nengine/init.ts:228:  let aoStatus: AoDaemonStatus = { state: 'unknown', detail: 'AO daemon status could not be checked' };\\nengine/init.ts:235:    catch (error) { aoStatus = { state: 'unknown', detail: error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'AO status could not be checked' }; }\\nengine/init.ts:236:    if (aoStatus.state !== 'running') print(`${aoStatus.detail}. AO is unavailable. Direct is the default. ${aoStatus.state === 'stopped' ? 'Run ao start to use AO.' : 'Check ao status --json before choosing AO.'}`);\\nengine/init.ts:247:      const guidance = aoStatus.state === 'stopped' ? 'run ao start and retry' : 'check ao status --json, then retry';\\nengine/init.ts:256:  const statuses = [];\\nengine/init.ts:258:    const status = await checkHarness(harness, command, cwd);\\nengine/init.ts:259:    statuses.push(status);\\nengine/init.ts:260:    print(`${harness}: ${status.detail}`);\\nengine/init.ts:261:    if (!status.signedIn) print(`  Next: ${status.fix}`);\\nengine/init.ts:263:  const ready = new Set(statuses.filter(s => s.installed && s.signedIn).map(s => s.harness));\\nengine/init.ts:264:  if (!ready.size) throw new InitError('No installed and signed-in Codex or Claude Code agent detected; run claude auth login or codex login, then retry.');\\nengine/init.ts:317:    let status: AoDaemonStatus;\\nengine/init.ts:318:    try { status = await readAoDaemonStatus(command, cwd); }\\nengine/init.ts:319:    catch (error) { throw new InitError(`AO daemon status could not be checked (${error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'unknown error'}); check ao status --json and retry, or select direct. Nothing was changed.`); }\\nengine/init.ts:320:    if (status.state !== 'running') {\\nengine/init.ts:321:      const guidance = status.state === 'stopped' ? 'run ao start and retry' : 'check ao status --json and retry';\\nengine/init.ts:322:      throw new InitError(`${status.detail}; ${guidance}, or select direct. Nothing was changed.`);\\nengine/init.ts:326:  await command('gh', ['auth', 'status'], { cwd });\\nengine/init.ts:328:    const status = await checkHarness(harness, command, cwd);\\nengine/init.ts:329:    if (!status.installed || !status.signedIn) throw new InitError(`${status.detail}; fix: ${status.fix}. No harness substitution or changes were made.`);\\nengine/init.ts:334:    const status = await (options.cfoaiCheck ?? checkCfoaiConnection)();\\nengine/init.ts:335:    if (status.status !== 'ok') throw new InitError('cfo.ai was requested but is not connected; run tally login cfoai, then rerun init; nothing was changed.');\\nengine/init.ts:340:    const status = JSON.parse((await command('entire', ['status', '--json'], { cwd })).stdout);\\nengine/init.ts:341:    if (status.enabled !== true) throw new InitError(`Entire was requested but is not enabled in ${cwd}; run cd ${JSON.stringify(cwd)} && entire enable, review its repository/hook changes and trust the harness hooks, then rerun init; nothing was changed.`);\\nengine/model-access.ts:7:    .replace(/\\\\b(authorization\\\\s*[:=]\\\\s*bearer\\\\s+)\\\\S+/gi, '$1[redacted]')\\nengine/model-access.ts:21:    /\\\\b(?:not available|unavailable|not found|not authorized|not allowed|no access|don't have access|do not have access).{0,120}\\\\bmodel\\\\b/i,\\nengine/model-access.ts:22:    /\\\\b(?:account|subscription|plan).{0,100}\\\\b(?:cannot|can't|not authorized|not allowed|no access).{0,100}\\\\bmodel\\\\b/i,\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"10e194\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000005209,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":1854,\"output\":\"/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/direct-runner-spike.md:44:No-prompt proof: the first patch incorrectly prepended after, so make test initially failed; the worker later corrected it. Git add/commit returned index.lock Operation not permitted. Exit code 0 did NOT mean task success. A live rollout count was observed at 13,615 input, 6,912 cached-input and 200 output. JSON usage arrived only at 41.459 s; process closed at 45.582 s. Final counts: 86,171 input including 75,264 cached, 1,191 output. Twelve queued neatlogs spans were observed.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/direct-runner-spike.md:46:Auto-review proof: edit at 7.221 s; make test passed at 9.647 s; sleep ended at 20.097 s; git add at 30.079 s; commit at 35.373 s. By then 14 JSON events had arrived and NONE contained usage. Live rollout totals increased from 13,331 input / 152 output to 26,840 / 226 and 40,449 / 301; cached totals 6,912, 19,968, 33,024. These are cumulative, not additive per poll. JSON usage arrived at 36.882 s; process closed at 42.529 s. Final: 96,506 input including 85,248 cached, 767 output. Seventeen queued spans were observed, trace id `c2313d2e166407437483b1ddf6069ebd`.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/final-020-ao-sanity-report.md:20:AO session timestamps observed after completion:\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/final-020-ao-sanity-report.md:86:Disk polling observed both tasks running and spend increasing before completion (e.g. 12:03:06 UTC total 0.00259816 USD, eventually 0.01934604). Active ledger rows are not present until attempts finish; live costs appear in run.json. These observations are filesystem reads, not an independent browser-render or HTTP-fetch test.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/ao-parallel-proof.md:31:Dashboard address printed immediately: `http://127.0.0.1:5203/?data=%2Fruns%2Flatest%2F`; HTTP 200 before first spawn. latest held this run while tasks were still pending. Read-only dashboard JSON observations show all three tasks running at 19:04:01.705Z. Combined live spend increased through $0.003044440, $0.005410400, $0.006890880, $0.020295120 and $0.041119320. Each ledger row appeared as its attempt finished, before the other tasks were done; every observed JSON response parsed successfully.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/comparison-results.md:30:- Single sample per task/mode; no confidence intervals or repeat-spread claim. Installed routing requires two observations before using statistics; learned caps require five successful samples for size+tier+actual model. C small/medium initially have only one relevant sample and still use priors; do not claim learned rerouting or caps without evidence.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/part1-spike-findings.md:12:{sessionId, incomplete, totals:{inputTokens, cachedInputTokens, uncachedInputTokens, outputTokens, processedTokens, cacheReadTokens, estimatedCost}, harnesses:[{harness, totals, models:[{modelId, totals}]}]}. estimatedCost = null or {totalNanos, inputNanos, cachedInputNanos, outputNanos, coverage:\\\"complete\\\"|\\\"partial\\\", providerAttribution:\\\"observed\\\"}; unit is nanos of USD (1e9 = $1). List form /api/v1/usage/sessions -> {sessions:[{sessionId, processedTokens, totalTokens, incomplete, estimatedCost}]}.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/video-walkthrough.md:67:The replay core linearly interpolates cost and tokens from final ledger totals, not historical per-poll measurements. The current page can substitute a ceiling-based live estimate while a replay row is running (observed on the four-task replay); treat that intermediate amount as an illustration, not a recorded measurement. This display discrepancy has been routed to lane C. The page labels this as replay; nothing is running now. Completed values are exactly the saved ledger at the end.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/claude-direct-proof.md:59:At 10:24:09.957 and 10:24:16.717 Claude requested the **on-list** command `find js -name '*.js' -print0 | xargs -0 -n1 node --check`. Both were denied in dontAsk mode. Worker also requested `git status` and read-only `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard`; neither emitted a denial in the observed transcript. No git push, gh or network command was attempted. This proof did not independently exercise a refused off-list write/network command. The pipeline permission defect is verified; its exact parser cause is not yet proven. Do not solve it by granting broad Bash permissions or weakening dontAsk.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/claude-direct-proof.md:145:| a: dashboard before worker | Pass | HTTP 200 observed at 11:17:32.809Z, before worker attempt start at 11:17:47.639Z. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/claude-direct-proof.md:158:261,840 tokens: 105 uncached input, 241,369 cache-read, 15,598 one-hour cache-write, zero five-minute cache-write, 4,768 output. Cost **$0.079277900** = $0.000105 + $0.0241369 + $0.031196 (1h writes at 2x input) + $0.02384. Cache writes priced and persisted, live usage from the Claude JSON stream at message boundaries, not an invoice. Setup duration zero; Tally verifier time not applicable because commit gate stopped the task. The observed successful Bash script turnarounds were 2.227s and 1.445s, including tool/hook overhead rather than an isolated host-verifier measurement.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/claude-direct-proof.md:178:- Claude tracing hooks synchronously call their own OTLP exporter; Tally's worker spool drain handles Codex queued spans, not these Claude hook exports. The hook source awaits its flush. The worker completed normally and was not killed while active. No late third-run trace appeared in repeated read-backs, so no observed evidence supports a drain-timing explanation.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/claude-direct-proof.md:192:- (a) PASS: dashboard address printed first; HTTP 200 observed at 12:38:14.773Z, before worker started at 12:38:22.345Z.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/first-user-flow-final-run.md:131:| c. Dashboard running state and increasing spend | PASS | Repeated HTTP reads of run.json and ledger.json at half-second intervals. #9 running: `09:22:41.032Z` $0; `09:22:50.594Z` $0.00351720; `09:22:55.637Z` $0.00592932; `09:23:00.684Z` $0.00764112; `09:23:10.771Z` $0.01037548; `09:23:20.875Z` $0.01207248. Ledger was empty while active, then had its passed row at `09:23:48.771Z`; finished JSON observed at `09:23:58.398Z`. |\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/codex-direct-proof.md:36:Worker rollout confirms exact external verification script first (13:03:12.699Z), repository reads, new document, then exact script again (13:03:31.929Z). Worker did not run git add/commit/push or raw verification pipeline. It left edits uncommitted; Tally committed them and ran the real configured command independently before publishing. No observed permission prompt/denial, no bypass flag. Scope warnings empty. Setup duration 0 ms; independent verifier duration is not stored, so not invented. Syntax validation checks JavaScript, not all Markdown semantics.\\n/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/artifacts/neatlogs-hack-2/cap-and-escalate-recipe.md:72:observed live. This recipe installs it. If still missing, disclose it and use:\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"c89f16\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000005458,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":3518,\"output\":\"| `tally market --jobs --limit 25 --max-credits 1` | Find engineering posts with explicit coding-tool evidence; group by posting company and label hiring intent, never confirmed adoption. |\\n| `tally market --replay .tally/market/latest/query.json --output /tmp/tally-market-replay` | Render the saved snapshot again with no API calls or credentials. |\\n| `tally publish --out site` | Static dashboard export with a sensitive-content scan. No deployment. |\\n\\n`run --dashboard` starts serving before the first task and prints the URL\\nimmediately. `--open` also opens that URL in the system browser. The server\\ncontinues after the run for inspection, until Ctrl+C. Terminal output includes\\nroutes, worker session IDs, spend updates at most every ten seconds, attempt\\nresults, PR URLs and a final summary. `--budget` is a cap for this run, not a\\nreplacement for the monthly budget: Tally applies the lower of the run cap and\\nthe remaining monthly budget, including for the direct sequential runner. Plan\\nand summary show both, including the amount available now and monthly source.\\nWhen cfo.ai supplies the monthly budget, init asks only for a local fallback\\nafter connecting it. Worker trace IDs require correlated trace read-back;\\nunverified delivery is shown as missing rather than success. See\\n[Human approval points](docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md) for operations that still\\nrequire explicit user consent or review.\\n\\n### Pilot Discovery\\n\\n`tally market` finds and qualifies likely pilot companies, not adoption or market\\nsize. The default hypothesis is Software Development companies with 20-500\\nemployees. It does not infer coding-agent seats or use from headcount. An optional\\n`--keyword` filters an exact phrase in the company description, which can also\\nmatch vendors and does not prove adoption.\\n\\nLive searches require `CRUSTDATA_API_KEY` in `~/.tally/.env`. Before each single\\nbatch Tally checks free account pricing and prints a conservative maximum estimate\\nincluding premium filters and returned fields. `--max-credits` (default 10)\\nrefuses an estimated over-cap search; it is a per-call estimate, not a server-side\\nhard billing limit. No pagination, enrichment or retries occur. Use `--plan` to\\ncheck pricing without a paid search. `--dry-run` requires cached `--replay`.\\n\\nThe output directory contains the exact `query.json`, `companies.json`, CSV and\\n`report.md`: API version, query hash, retrieval timestamp, returned versus API\\nmatch count, reported credits, and record-update dates when available. These\\ndates are not publication dates. Cached replay preserves provenance and calls\\nno API, even without credentials; it does not rerun the search for fresh data.\\nLegacy snapshots without matching provenance must be recaptured rather than\\nsilently treated as new evidence.\\n\\nReview a saved snapshot with `--replay <query.json> --review <reviews.json>`.\\nReviews are an array of `{ id, label, reason, sourceUrl, sourceDate, excerpt? }`;\\nlabels are `candidate`, `vendor`, `hiring intent`, `verified use`, or `rejected`.\\nUse `null` for unknown source dates. Intent/use labels require a sourced excerpt;\\nTally checks the structure, not the truth of manual judgements. Without a review,\\nevery returned row remains an unverified candidate. JSON/CSV retain all rows;\\nthe short Markdown report shows at most ten. No deduplication or extrapolation\\nis applied to company search.\\n\\nThe stronger second stage is `--jobs`: a single batch (default 25) of engineering\\nposts from the past 90 days whose descriptions mention Claude Code, Cursor,\\nGitHub Copilot, Codex or AI coding agents. It uses Job Search, with no premium\\ncompany filters. Ambiguous Cursor/Codex mentions need nearby AI/coding context;\\nrows missing title, company identity, source URL or date are dropped. Accepted\\nrows are grouped by normalized domain (or company id) and labelled hiring intent.\\nThat is evidence in a job advertisement, not a verified deployment, current open\\nrole, buyer or purchase commitment. Posting entities can be staffing companies;\\ncross-posts under different domains are not assumed to be the same buyer.\\nDates are the API's `metadata.date_added`, not independently verified publication\\ndates. JSON retains grouped job evidence and CSV includes title/excerpt.\\n\\nUse `--jobs --secondary <saved-company-query.json>` to include a previously\\nreviewed firmographic snapshot as a secondary table without re-fetching it.\\nThe job snapshot embeds that secondary evidence, so `--replay <job-query.json>`\\nlater renders both tables with no API calls. Query match counts remain database\\ncounts, never adoption or market size.\\n\\n## Routing Evidence\\n\\nSee the [Tally business plan](docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md) for share-of-savings pricing,\\nthe measured three-task comparison, explicitly labelled unit-economics and\\npilot-funnel assumptions, cfo.ai usage and the remaining commercial risks.\\n\\nFrom a source checkout, build and analyze an audited list of real run directories:\\n\\n```sh\\nnpm run build\\nnode scripts/routing-evidence.mjs --manifest runs.json --out /tmp/routing-evidence\\n```\\n\\n`runs.json` is a JSON array of paths containing `run.json` and `ledger.json`.\\nThe script writes a Markdown report, task CSV, JSON provenance and three SVG\\ncharts. It excludes simulations and active runs, keeps skipped/interrupted\\noutcomes in the denominator, and labels missing historical signals as missing.\\nUnattributed historical decisions are not silently assigned to prior or\\ntrace-driven cohorts. Correlations are descriptive, not cost predictions.\\n\\nAdd `--live-traces` for bounded read-only neatlogs verification using Tally's\\nread token and project id. Without that flag it only replays the output folder's\\ncached `trace-readback.json`, making no API call. Optional `--issues issues.json`\\naccepts a URL-keyed map of current issue titles/bodies for historical runs;\\nthese are labelled current text, not run-start snapshots. No paid workers or\\ncfo.ai writes are performed. Before/after savings require matched measured\\ntasks; missing baseline or v2 cohorts are reported as **not measured**.\\n\\nFor the matched three-pass experiment, add `--comparison-dir <folder>`.\\nThe folder contains `mentor-comparison-plan-signals.json` and, when results\\narrive, `comparison-passes.json`: an object mapping `A`, `B`, `C` to arrays of\\nreal run-directory paths (absolute or relative to that folder). A is top-tier\\nbaseline, B is Tally v1/priors, C is Tally v2/trace history. Missing passes stay\\npending. The report joins signals only at the exact pinned base SHA and task\\ntext, reports cost/verdict/attempts per task and per pass, and charts sizing\\nagainst real costs with per-pass correlations. A pass C label does not itself\\nprove that history influenced every route or that any cap was learned.\\nUse `--comparison-passes <json>` to keep the labelled path manifest elsewhere\\nwithout changing the measured signals folder.\\n\\n## Layout\\n\\nRouting history is isolated by task size, tier name and actual model id. Changing\\na tier's model does not reuse the old model's pass rate or learned cap. Legacy\\nhistory without an explicit model remains available for audit but is excluded\\nfrom model-specific decisions; these fall back to the prior/configured cap\\nuntil enough new matching observations exist. History trace names include the\\nmodel id so the public neatlogs read API can identify it without hidden attributes.\\n\\nExperiment summaries are atomically updated after each completed run at\\n`.tally/runs/experiments/<experimentId>/summary.json`. The dashboard server lists\\nthem in `/runs/index.json` under `experiments`. Each underlying run retains its\\nown folder, `mode`, and `repeat`. Simulated cost remains labeled as simulated.\\nThe aggregate watcher polls once a second; reported final cost can exceed the\\nstop threshold due to polling or metering lag and is never clipped to the cap.\\n\\n| Folder | Holds |\\n|---|---|\\n| `schema/` | The contract every part reads: TypeScript types, JSON Schema, validators, and a sample finished run in `schema/fixtures/finished-run/`. |\\n| `engine/` | The run loop, run state and ledger. |\\n| `adapters/` | One thin module per outside tool (Agent Orchestrator, Entire, neatlogs, cfo.ai, GitHub). |\\n| `agent/` | The CFO agent: sizing, routing, ceilings, escalation. |\\n| `cli/` | The `tally` command. |\\n| `dashboard/` | Static page that reads the ledger and run state. |\\n| `demo/` | Sample backlog and scripted outcomes for `tally demo`. |\\n\\n## Configuration\\n\\nWith neatlogs on, Tally drains the completed Codex worker's hook spool from\\nits own process, outside the worker sandbox. It uses the official package's\\nspool API (there is no flush CLI), matching native session IDs or the worktree\\npath. Only successfully delivered batches are acknowledged; failed,\\nunrelated and malformed batches remain queued. A locked or incomplete drain\\nis reported as missing worker trace delivery. After all workers exit, Tally\\nretries once when a worker drain reported lock contention and verifies the\\nresult with Neatlogs before updating the final run status. If delivery remains\\nunverified, the run keeps the batches and shows `tally traces drain --run <id>`\\nfor recovery. Dry runs do not drain workers.\\n\\nWith `integrations.entire: true`, enable Entire in the target repository and\\nreview and trust its hooks in the coding agent before starting workers. Tally records\\nthe task commit's `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer and its native `entireSessionId`\\nseparately from the AO `sessionId`. Logs-only checkpoints retain conversation\\ncontext; escalation defaults to a metadata-only handoff containing the\\ncheckpoint/session IDs, branch, recorded and changed files, attempt verdict,\\nand task text. It does not generate a summary or export a transcript.\\n`entire.generatedSummaries: true` explicitly opts into Entire's summary\\nprovider (`checkpoint explain --generate`), which may export session content;\\nthis setting is off by default and requires the user's approval.\\nWhen Tally publishes a verified branch, it also pushes the checkpoint refs\\nnamed by its new commits to `origin` and to an `entire` remote when configured.\\nCheckpoint upload failures are reported as publishing failures. Dry runs do\\nnot publish branches or checkpoints.\\n\\nEntire checkpoint records live in separate Git refs, not on the task branch.\\nTally keeps the checkpoint ID and native session ID, and attaches a clickable\\nEntire session link only when the Entire CLI confirms that the GitHub repo has\\na ready mirror. The link opens the session that contains the checkpoint; a\\nreviewer still needs access to that repo in Entire. Without a ready mirror,\\nTally keeps the checkpoint ID available in the local ledger rather than adding\\na link that may not open.\\n\\nAfter each run has completed its Neatlogs read-back, Tally adds an evidence\\nsection to the generated PR body when links are available: Entire session and\\ncheckpoint, Neatlogs routing and worker traces, and the configured cfo.ai\\nbudget and spend tables. Worker trace links are added only after Tally verifies\\ntheir read-back. These dashboard links use the viewer's existing account\\naccess; Tally does not publish public trace shares.\\n\\nTally assumes nothing about the repository it works on. The repository, test\\ncommand, model tiers, list prices, ceilings and budget all come from\\n`tally.yaml` (a tier is a name, harness, model id, provider, list price and ceiling share); see `schema/fixtures/finished-run/tally.yaml` for a complete\\nexample and `schema/tally-config.schema.json` for the rules.\\n\\nMoney in the ledger and run state is stored as integer nanos of USD\\n(1 USD = 1,000,000,000). List-price estimates multiply aggregate tokens by the\\nconfigured rates in `tally.yaml`; they are normalized estimates, not invoices.\\nNew ledger rows snapshot those rates in `price` and record their source and\\nverification status in `priceNote`. Unknown models/providers or rates that\\ndisagree with the verified snapshot are labelled unverified. Operator-supplied\\n`tiers[].priceNote` is retained as a config note, not independently verified.\\n\\n### Codex Tier Prices\\n\\nOfficial base text rates checked 2026-10-11, USD per million tokens:\\n\\n| Model | Input | Cached Input | Output | Source |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n| gpt-5.6-luna | 0.20 | 0.02 | 1.20 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-luna) |\\n| gpt-5.6-terra | 2.00 | 0.20 | 12.00 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-terra) |\\n| gpt-5.6-sol | 4.00 | 0.40 | 20.00 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-sol) |\\n\\n```yaml\\ntiers:\\n  - { name: cheap, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-luna, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 0.2, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.02, outputUsdPerMTok: 1.2 }, ceilingFraction: 0.2 }\\n  - { name: middle, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-terra, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 2, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.2, outputUsdPerMTok: 12 }, ceilingFraction: 0.5 }\\n  - { name: top, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-sol, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 4, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.4, outputUsdPerMTok: 20 }, ceilingFraction: 1 }\\n```\\n\\nPer-request prompts above 272K input tokens have input/output uplifts;\\ncache writes also have an uplift. AO's aggregate token buckets cannot resolve\\nthese adjustments. Sol's published promotional rates last at least through\\n2026-11-21; recheck before subsequent runs. Historical costs are not repriced.\\n\\nFor dry runs, set `backlog.source: file` and point `backlog.file` at YAML like:\\n\\n```yaml\\ntasks:\\n  - id: T1\\n    title: Add validation for empty player name\\n    size: small\\n    outcomes: [passed]\\n  - id: T2\\n    title: Persist settings after restart\\n    size: medium\\n    outcomes: [stopped, passed]\\n```\\n\\n`outcomes` is optional and defaults to one passed simulated attempt.\\n\\nThe latest run is also mirrored to `.tally/runs/latest/run.json` and\\n`.tally/runs/latest/ledger.json` so the dashboard has one stable path to poll.\\n\\n## Secrets\\n\\nKeys live in `~/.tally/.env`, outside every repository, and are loaded from\\nthere. Never put them in this repo; `.env` files are git-ignored.\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"54fc53\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.202889209,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":15,\"output\":\"\\n> tally-agents@0.2.1 build\\n> tsc -p tsconfig.build.json\\n\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","create_time":1791834667.991969}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
