{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:55:49.552Z","ordinal":17204,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_072dfc8f7183a0b2016acd3b43e714819191da177f1dbb83a1","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"I read both supplied ledgers. They show live P90-derived caps, and the self-host run shows both stopped tasks passing on the same branches after escalation. Both `failureAssessment` records are explicitly ledger fallbacks because the worker traces could not be read.\n"}],"phase":"commentary"},"started_at_ms":1791834948189,"completed_at_ms":1791834949552}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:55:49.554Z","ordinal":17205,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_072dfc8f7183a0b2016acd3b43e714819191da177f1dbb83a1","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"I read both supplied ledgers. They show live P90-derived caps, and the self-host run shows both stopped tasks passing on the same branches after escalation. Both `failureAssessment` records are explicitly ledger fallbacks because the worker traces could not be read.\n"}],"phase":"commentary","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","create_time":1791834948.2478077,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}},"metadata":{"retained_source":{"id":{"message_id":"msg_072dfc8f7183a0b2016acd3b43e714819191da177f1dbb83a1","turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","role":"assistant"},"revision":"retained_01a131af-87b2-7811-8629-6f23283f9ff3","complete":true},"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":1689}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:57:58.611Z","ordinal":17206,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_072dfc8f7183a0b2016acd3b45c11c8191b9e4e1fa6426e6a3","status":"completed","call_id":"call_fcaa125d333e499c82b0ee3498a5bc51","name":"exec","input":"text(await tools.apply_patch(\"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Delete File: /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/README.md\\n*** End Patch\"));\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(\"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/README.md\\n+![Meet Tally, the CFO for your agent fleet: pixel tally mark on a dark background](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/main/docs/assets/tally-banner.png)\\n+\\n+Tally is a CFO for coding agents: give it GitHub issues and a budget, and it routes, caps and verifies the work before opening pull requests.\\n+\\n+## Install\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+npm install -g tally-agents\\n+```\\n+\\n+Published version: **0.2.1**, checked on npm on 13 October 2026.\\n+Requires Node 20+; real work also needs Git, signed-in GitHub CLI and at least\\n+one signed-in coding agent. Optional integrations are not required.\\n+\\n+## 60-Second Quick Start\\n+\\n+From your repository root, with open issues carrying your chosen label:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+tally init\\n+tally run --plan\\n+tally run --budget 3 --dashboard\\n+```\\n+\\n+Init asks about runner, agents, verification, budget and integrations. Review\\n+the plan before the last command: **it starts paid workers and may push task\\n+branches/open PRs**. Replace `3` with your approved USD run budget. Connection\\n+and setup can take longer than a minute; the dashboard serves until Ctrl+C.\\n+\\n+Excerpt from a real AO run, not the simulated demo:\\n+\\n+```text\\n+#21 attempt 1: passed $0.0126 https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/22\\n+#20 attempt 1: passed $0.0136 https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/23\\n+#19 attempt 1: passed $0.0150 https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/24\\n+finished run-20261011-190131Z: 3 tasks, 3 attempts\\n+summary: 3 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped; total $0.0411 against run cap $2.00\\n+```\\n+\\n+Try it without accounts or paid workers: `tally demo --dashboard --port 0`.\\n+The seven demo tasks and token usage are scripted, not real savings evidence.\\n+\\n+## What Tally Does\\n+\\n+1. **Route:** size tasks and choose configured tiers using confidence, cost\\n+   and past outcomes when available.\\n+2. **Cap:** reserve budget and meter workers against their attempt ceilings.\\n+3. **Escalate:** stop capped/failed work and, when appropriate and affordable,\\n+   pass its branch and available checkpoint metadata to a stronger tier.\\n+4. **Verify:** independently run your configured repository checks.\\n+5. **Open a pull request:** publish verified work with a cost/evidence ledger.\\n+   **Tally never merges its task PRs.**\\n+\\n+Both the run cap and remaining monthly budget apply. Polling/metering can\\n+overshoot a ceiling; this is not a provider-side hard billing limit.\\n+\\n+## Runners and Agents\\n+\\n+| Runner | Execution | Agents |\\n+| --- | --- | --- |\\n+| Agent Orchestrator (`ao`) | AO manages sessions; opt into parallel workers at init or with `--parallel N` | Claude Code, Codex or configured mixed tiers |\\n+| Direct (`direct`) | Tally launches workers itself, one task at a time; no AO daemon needed | Claude Code or Codex, selected per tier |\\n+\\n+Install/sign in to every configured harness. Tally never silently substitutes\\n+one for a missing agent. Login does not prove model entitlement. Claude uses\\n+a scoped consented allow-list; no runner uses bypass-permissions.\\n+[Runner details and proofs](docs/EVIDENCE.md).\\n+\\n+## Optional Integrations\\n+\\n+| Product | Adds | When Off or Unavailable |\\n+| --- | --- | --- |\\n+| neatlogs | Decision/worker traces, read-back evidence, cost/pass-rate history and learned caps keyed by size and actual model | No Tally decision export when off; local history, then explicit priors/config caps. Previously installed global hooks may still trace sessions. |\\n+| Entire | Graph sizing, checkpoints, past-failure recall and metadata-only handoff | Repository search sizes tasks; no Entire checkpoints/hosted recall promised. No symbol matches falls back without calling a working graph disconnected. |\\n+| cfo.ai | Monthly budget read, reconciled real spend and workspace-shared per-attempt Ari reports when enabled | Local budget/ledger; failed writes retain `cfoai-spend-fallback.csv` for reconciliation. |\\n+\\n+Credentials stay in their login tools or `~/.tally/`, not the repository.\\n+A stored trace ID does not itself prove delivery.\\n+\\n+## Evidence, Not Promises\\n+\\n+| Matched Three-Task Pass | Estimated Cost | Final Tasks Passing |\\n+| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n+| Every task on the top tier | $1.22 | 3/3 |\\n+| Tally v1, built-in priors | $0.31 | 3/3 |\\n+| Tally v2, history enabled | $0.43 | 3/3 |\\n+\\n+**Three tasks, one run each per pass**, same initial commit, list-price token\\n+estimates rather than invoices: about 75% and 65% cheaper on that sample. Each\\n+pass also had three separately recorded startup retries. Only C's large task\\n+used observed history; no tier/cap changed. C cost more than B: this does not\\n+prove history improved on priors or guarantee savings on another workload.\\n+\\n+- **Cap and escalation:** [fly-doom-jev PR #17](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/fly-doom-jev/pull/17),\\n+  `run-20261012-100856Z`: cheap stopped at $0.018 (metered $0.01956), middle\\n+  passed at $0.16958; the failure classification was ledger-derived.\\n+- **Parallel AO:** the three overlapping workers above cost $0.04111932.\\n+  The 0.2.1 sanity run also produced\\n+  [PR #35](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/35) and\\n+  [PR #36](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/36),\\n+  2/2 passing at $0.02483996 with reconciled spend/read-back traces.\\n+- **Live learned ceiling:** [Othello PR #5](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/Othello/pull/5),\\n+  `run-20261012-153240Z`: six successful small/luna observations from neatlogs\\n+  set a P90 x 1.5 cap of **$0.02284986**, below the configured $0.30; passed\\n+  at $0.01816744.\\n+- **Learned cap caused escalation:** in self-host run `run-20261012-162757Z`,\\n+  tasks #155/#157 stopped at a learned **$0.02725116** cap (metered $0.02817828\\n+  and $0.02770580). Middle/terra passed on each same branch at $0.19549280\\n+  and $0.38153360: [PR #163](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/pull/163)\\n+  and [PR #162](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/pull/162).\\n+  Both assessments are `ceiling`, source `ledger`, because worker traces were\\n+  unreadable then; not trace-derived failure diagnoses.\\n+- **Four paths:** individual real PRs exist for Claude and Codex through AO\\n+  and directly. [Full evidence and reference](docs/EVIDENCE.md).\\n+\\n+These later user runs demonstrate learned caps, not a new controlled savings\\n+comparison. Caps require five successful samples per size/tier/model, never\\n+exceed config, and revert to config when insufficient data exists.\\n+\\n+## Consent and Data Sharing\\n+\\n+Review init's proposal. Integration setup can change external systems before\\n+the final config write. [Approval details](docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md).\\n+\\n+- **Claude:** approve scoped file edits, git add/commit/status/diff and exact\\n+  external test/setup scripts. Missing/stale consent blocks launch; no broad Bash.\\n+- **Entire:** capture changes listed settings/local hooks. Publishing them to\\n+  the default branch requires separate confirmation. Trust agent hooks yourself;\\n+  hosted mirroring is another choice, generated summaries are off by default.\\n+- **neatlogs:** key verification sends a synthetic probe; enabled tracing sends\\n+  decisions. Optional machine-wide hooks can trace agent sessions outside Tally.\\n+- **cfo.ai:** fresh init asks for budget, spend and Ari context. Real attempts\\n+  write spend and, when enabled, organization-memory reports with task/title/IDs,\\n+  model/tokens/cost/decision/outcome, budgets and evidence URLs. They exclude\\n+  issue bodies, source/diffs, prompts/transcripts and raw spans. Re-init currently\\n+  uses a generic enable question: inspect its reporting disclosure/proposal.\\n+- **PR evidence:** available Entire/neatlogs/cfo.ai links are appended to task\\n+  PR bodies. There is **no separate init question for finance-URL sharing**.\\n+  Links do not grant access, but PR readers can see their addresses.\\n+\\n+Never paste keys into YAML, issues, PRs or diagnostics. Free-text labels and\\n+result notes still need care; sanitization is not a complete privacy guarantee.\\n+Dry runs never write simulated spend/Ari reports, but can export decision\\n+traces when neatlogs is enabled.\\n+\\n+## Known Limits\\n+\\n+- Early product; verification is only as strong as your configured checks.\\n+- Never run two Tally processes against one cfo.ai page: cross-process spend\\n+  writes are not atomic and can overwrite one another.\\n+- Mixed AO permission switching is locked only within one Tally process.\\n+  Direct escalation and no-account onboarding need broader live validation.\\n+- Direct processes cannot be reattached after exit. Codex metering uses an\\n+  internal rollout format; missing metering stops the worker.\\n+- Model access, trace delivery and hosted recall are separate evidence checks.\\n+- 0.1.0 configs load sequentially unchanged; new config fields do not work on\\n+  0.1.0. Built runtime supports Node 20; source tests need a Vitest-supported\\n+  runtime (22.12+ on Node 22).\\n+\\n+## For AI Agents\\n+\\n+Read [the self-contained agent guide](docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md) before using Tally\\n+on a person's repository: human choices, verified flags, plan/state/ledger\\n+interpretation and failure handling. It ships with npm; [llms.txt](llms.txt)\\n+provides discovery links.\\n+\\n+## More\\n+\\n+- [Getting started](docs/GETTING_STARTED.md)\\n+- [Business plan and labelled assumptions](docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md)\\n+- [Detailed evidence and operational reference](docs/EVIDENCE.md)\\n+- [Website](https://tally-agents-pi.vercel.app/)\\n+- [npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/tally-agents)\\n+\\n+GitHub alternative: `npm install -g github:Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack#release`.\\n+The release branch is prebuilt; main is source-only.\\n+\\n+## Licence\\n+\\n+[MIT](LICENSE).\\n*** Add File: /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md\\n+# Tally Agent Guide\\n+\\n+Instructions for an AI coding agent asked to \\\"use Tally on this repo\\\".\\n+Self-contained for tally-agents 0.2.1. Work from the target repository root.\\n+That request alone is not permission to spend, publish or change external data.\\n+\\n+## Hard Rules\\n+\\n+- Never merge Tally's PRs; the human reviews/merges.\\n+- Never push to a default branch. If Entire setup offers a settings commit\\n+  and default-branch push, stop and hand that step to the human. Do not approve\\n+  it on their behalf. Normal task publication targets worker branches.\\n+- Never use bypass-permissions, danger/full-access flags or loosen a sandbox.\\n+- Never edit tally.yaml/install records to skip a consent. Have the human\\n+  rerun init and approve the exact current rules instead.\\n+- Never run two Tally runs against one cfo.ai page at once; coordinate with\\n+  other operators. Cross-process spend updates are not atomic.\\n+- Never paste/read out keys or tokens, reuse another tool's tokens, or put\\n+  secrets in YAML/PRs/issues. Tally stores its secrets outside the repository.\\n+- Never guess tests, model access, prices or savings. Do not create issues,\\n+  install global hooks or start paid work without the applicable approval.\\n+\\n+## Preconditions: Read-Only Checks\\n+\\n+Run these; summarize status without exposing account details or credentials:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+pwd\\n+git status --short\\n+git rev-parse --show-toplevel\\n+node --version\\n+npm --version\\n+git --version\\n+gh --version\\n+gh auth status\\n+tally --version\\n+tally init --help\\n+tally run --help\\n+```\\n+\\n+Node must be 20+. Published version checked 13 October 2026: 0.2.1. Installed\\n+and source versions can differ. If absent, ask before installing with\\n+`npm install -g tally-agents`. The installed CLI is `tally`, not `tally-agents`.\\n+\\n+Check the human's chosen coding CLIs without making inference calls:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+claude --version\\n+claude auth status\\n+codex --version\\n+codex login status\\n+```\\n+\\n+Claude must report loggedIn true; Codex must report logged in. Missing/sign-out\\n+blocks that harness; no silent substitution. Hand login to the human. Sign-in\\n+does not prove model entitlement; ask about access to each configured model.\\n+\\n+If AO is installed and requested:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+ao version\\n+ao status --json\\n+```\\n+\\n+Require ready/healthy; direct needs no AO. Tally also resolves AO through\\n+TALLY_AO_BIN, repo.aoBin and macOS app bundles, so absent PATH alone is not\\n+proof it is missing. Do not modify project settings to make a check pass.\\n+\\n+For enabled Entire, read state first:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+entire --version\\n+entire auth status --json\\n+entire plugin list\\n+entire status --json\\n+```\\n+\\n+## Named Human Decisions\\n+\\n+Put each decision to the human and retain the answer:\\n+\\n+| Decision | Human Choice |\\n+| --- | --- |\\n+| Budget and caps | Monthly/local fallback budget, USD run cap and per-size/tier ceilings; escalation reserves; possible metering overshoot |\\n+| Runner | AO with approved parallel count, or sequential direct |\\n+| Agents and tiers | Claude, Codex or mixed; model IDs, rates, entitlement and any access-denial fallback |\\n+| Tasks/verification | Exact issue label/task set, meaningful test and optional setup commands; setup executes repo code |\\n+| neatlogs integration | Decision export, history read/project access and optional global hooks affecting sessions outside Tally |\\n+| Entire integration | Login/plugin/capture/hook trust/mirror and human-owned default-branch settings publication |\\n+| cfo.ai integration | Workspace/scenario, monthly budget/spend write-back, and separate missing-table creation approval |\\n+| Claude allow-list | Scoped file writes, git add/commit/status/diff and content-bound test/setup script rules |\\n+| Entire default-branch commit | Human reviews files and performs this separately; agent must stop before approving any push |\\n+| cfo.ai reports | Managed guide and organization-memory attempt reports; includes task titles/IDs, model/tokens/pricing/cost/result/decision, budgets and links |\\n+| PR evidence links | Entire/neatlogs/cfo.ai URLs become visible to PR readers; no dedicated init finance-sharing question, so ask yourself |\\n+| Paid run/publication | Explicit approval for this task set, cap, worker branch pushes and PR creation, never merges |\\n+\\n+Fresh cfo.ai init names Ari context; re-init asks a generic integration question\\n+and can add reports to an existing installation. Explain before re-init.\\n+Reports exclude issue bodies/source/diffs/prompts/transcripts/raw spans, but\\n+titles/result notes are shared metadata; sanitization is not a privacy guarantee.\\n+Declined integrations must stay off; do not force them on in YAML.\\n+\\n+## Init: Preview Before Applying\\n+\\n+Prefer interactive `tally init` for human-owned account/consent steps. Piped\\n+input is rejected. Do not automate browser approval or secret entry.\\n+\\n+Explicit non-interactive preview, integrations off:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+tally init --dry-run --yes --runner direct --harness codex \\\\\\n+  --budget 5 --issue-label tally --test-command 'npm test' \\\\\\n+  --setup-command '' --neatlogs no --entire no --cfoai no --verbose\\n+```\\n+\\n+Replace budget/label/test/setup with approved values. Do not assume npm test\\n+exists. Empty setup means none. After explicit approval the apply form removes\\n+`--dry-run`; `--yes` is not blanket consent to external integration actions.\\n+\\n+Verified init flags:\\n+\\n+- `--config PATH`: config location (default tally.yaml).\\n+- `--runner ao|direct`, `--harness claude-code|codex|mixed`: chosen execution.\\n+- `--parallel N`: approved AO concurrency; direct stays sequential.\\n+- `--budget USD`, `--issue-label LABEL`, `--test-command CMD`, `--setup-command CMD`.\\n+- `--allow-claude`: approves the printed repository allow-list; only after the\\n+  human reviewed those exact test/setup script contents.\\n+- `--model-access-fallback-tier NAME`: one budget-checked explicit model-access\\n+  denial fallback, not permission to choose another model arbitrarily.\\n+- `--neatlogs yes|no`, `--entire yes|no`, `--cfoai yes|no`: explicit opt-ins.\\n+- `--verbose`: full proposed YAML; `--dry-run`: preview only.\\n+\\n+Yes integration flags do not guarantee unattended onboarding: OAuth, project/\\n+workspace choice, table creation and hook trust can still need the human.\\n+Selecting cfo.ai includes reporting when supported; neatlogs hooks can be global.\\n+If any required decision is missing, stop rather than infer it.\\n+\\n+After init, using the chosen label/budget:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+tally doctor --config tally.yaml\\n+gh issue list --label tally --state open\\n+tally run --plan --config tally.yaml --budget 5\\n+```\\n+\\n+Do not create/label issues without approval. Doctor checks connection, not\\n+paid model entitlement or delivery of every checkpoint/trace feature.\\n+\\n+## Read the Plan Before Spending\\n+\\n+Plan starts no worker and writes no spend/PR; it may read GitHub, repository/\\n+Entire signals, history and cfo.ai budget. It is not offline.\\n+Tell the human task count/IDs, model/harness/tier, size/confidence, per-attempt\\n+ceilings, reason and history source. Distinguish observed samples versus priors,\\n+and configured versus P90-derived caps (five successful size/tier/model samples).\\n+Report run-cap/monthly-remaining minimum and escalation reservations, not just\\n+the first-attempt spend. Off/missing integrations and local fallbacks matter.\\n+Ask again if effective task set, budget or permissions changed since approval.\\n+\\n+Optional simulation with the reviewed configuration:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+tally run --dry-run --plan --config tally.yaml --budget 5\\n+tally run --dry-run --config tally.yaml --budget 5\\n+```\\n+\\n+Fake workers do not prove model access or work quality. Dry runs can export\\n+decision traces with neatlogs on, but never write simulated spend/Ari reports.\\n+`tally demo` uses bundled integrations-off data for a no-account simulation.\\n+\\n+## Start Only the Approved Run\\n+\\n+After explicit approval for spending and task-branch/PR publication:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+tally run --config tally.yaml --budget 5 --dashboard --port 0\\n+```\\n+\\n+Run flags verified in current help: `--only TASK_ID` (use plan's exact ID),\\n+`--parallel N` (AO only), `--open` (browser), `--config PATH`, `--budget USD`,\\n+`--dashboard`, `--port N`, `--plan`, `--dry-run` and `--baseline`.\\n+Baseline removes task ceilings and sends work to the strongest tier: never use\\n+without separate approval. Ordinary run needs no `--yes`; executing it is the\\n+paid/publication action. Do not add paid experiment/retry runs opportunistically.\\n+\\n+Record the printed run ID/output path/dashboard URL immediately. Serving\\n+continues after completion until Ctrl+C. Direct workers cannot be reattached\\n+after Tally exits. Inspect existing PRs and saved state before restarting;\\n+restarting can spend again.\\n+\\n+## Read run.json and ledger.json\\n+\\n+Default output: `.tally/runs/RUN_ID/run.json` and `ledger.json`, relative to\\n+the current working directory; trust printed paths. `latest/` mirrors the\\n+latest run. Ledger is an array of attempt rows; run.json contains task/budget/\\n+integration state. Parse JSON, do not infer success from a terminal exit alone:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+node -e 'const fs=require(\\\"node:fs\\\");const r=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(\\\".tally/runs/latest/run.json\\\",\\\"utf8\\\"));const l=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(\\\".tally/runs/latest/ledger.json\\\",\\\"utf8\\\"));console.log(JSON.stringify({runId:r.runId,status:r.status,dryRun:r.dryRun,budget:r.budget,integrations:r.integrations,tasks:r.tasks.map(t=>({id:t.taskId,status:t.status,reason:t.statusReason,usd:t.spentNanos/1e9,pr:t.prUrl})),attempts:l.map(a=>({id:a.attemptId,tier:a.tier,model:a.model,usd:a.estimatedCostNanos/1e9,result:a.result,reason:a.resultReason,failureAssessment:a.failureAssessment,pr:a.prUrl}))},null,2));'\\n+```\\n+\\n+USD fields are integer nanos: divide by 1e9. Run status running/finished/\\n+interrupted differs from task pending/running/passed/failed/skipped;\\n+**finished does not mean all tasks passed**. Live task costs can precede ended\\n+ledger rows. Sum all attempts including stops/errors/escalations, not only\\n+passing ones. price/priceNote/costSource explain estimates, not invoices.\\n+\\n+failureAssessment, when present, records kind/source/evidence/retryable.\\n+Ledger fallback is not trace-derived. traceId is CFO decision; workerTraceId is\\n+the separate worker. Null/unverified IDs are not evidence of delivery.\\n+checkpointId/entireSessionId differ from AO sessionId. Review scopeWarnings,\\n+permissionWarnings and integration detail; do not disclose raw transcripts or\\n+financial snapshots without approval.\\n+\\n+## Failures: Correct Responses\\n+\\n+| Symptom | Response |\\n+| --- | --- |\\n+| Reservation/next ceiling does not fit | Report task and available budget; ask about priority/new cap, never increase it yourself |\\n+| AO project not found/not ready | Inspect doctor/displayed AO diagnostic; human repairs/re-inits or chooses direct; never silently substitute |\\n+| Signed-out/missing harness | Report the selected CLI, hand login to human, rerun doctor/plan |\\n+| Model unavailable | Only the configured budget-checked access fallback may retry; do not choose a guessed model |\\n+| Missing/stale Claude consent | Human reruns init and reviews exact rules; never patch/bypass permissions |\\n+| Ceiling stop | Read spend/branch/checkpoint and classification source; Tally may perform the approved affordable escalation, not your same-tier retry |\\n+| Verify failed | Report command/verdict; preserve branch, never weaken tests or merge; escalation stays inside the approved budget |\\n+| Tool/permission/environment error | Stronger models may hit the same wall; ask for repair, not speculative paid retries |\\n+| neatlogs error/pending worker trace | Preserve queued batches/ledger; report local fallback versus unverified delivery, not all-features success |\\n+| Entire no symbols/hosted recall unavailable | Code-search sizing can be valid; capture and hosted recall are different; no mirror without consent |\\n+| cfo.ai read/write/report error | Report budget source/sanitized detail/CSV fallback; reconcile before rerunning and avoid concurrent writers |\\n+| PR push/evidence failure | Inspect existing branches/PRs first; publishing retries push once automatically; no force/default-branch push |\\n+\\n+Finished-run trace recovery is `tally traces drain --run RUN_ID`: network\\n+export/read-back, not inference. Ask before export; restrict it to this run,\\n+never delete unrelated queued batches.\\n+\\n+## Report Back\\n+\\n+Give run ID/state, passed/failed/skipped counts, each PR link, cost including\\n+failures/retries versus cap, verification outcomes, failure classification and\\n+source/evidence, off/broken integrations and fallback, unverified evidence,\\n+and spend/report write-back status. Distinguish simulations from real work and\\n+estimates from invoices. Name what is unproven; leave PRs open and state that\\n+you merged nothing.\\n*** Add File: /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/llms.txt\\n+# Tally\\n+\\n+> A CFO for coding agents: route, cap, escalate, verify and open PRs. Never merge.\\n+\\n+## Instructions\\n+\\n+- [Agent guide](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/main/docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md): Self-contained commands, human choices, safety and failure responses; read first.\\n+- [Getting started](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/main/docs/GETTING_STARTED.md): Human onboarding and troubleshooting.\\n+- [Evidence](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/main/docs/EVIDENCE.md): Recorded proofs and limits.\\n+- [Business plan](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/main/docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md): Measured results and labelled assumptions.\\n*** Update File: /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/package.json\\n@@\\n     \\\"docs/\\\",\\n+    \\\"docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md\\\",\\n+    \\\"llms.txt\\\",\\n@@\\n     \\\"!dist/**/testing/**\\\",\\n+    \\\"!docs/assets/**\\\",\\n*** End Patch\"));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","create_time":1791834949.756919}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:57:58.693Z","ordinal":17207,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","session_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","root_turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","response_id":"resp_072dfc8f7183a0b2016acd3b3f61bc81918b6ebf354a08fb55","usage":{"input_tokens":219644,"cached_input_tokens":214912,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":6410,"reasoning_output_tokens":40,"total_tokens":226054},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":2365142,"cached_input_tokens":2313600,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":15946,"reasoning_output_tokens":1105,"total_tokens":2381088},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":272933488,"cached_input_tokens":265330816,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":958006,"reasoning_output_tokens":207321,"total_tokens":273891494}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:57:58.728Z","ordinal":17208,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","item":{"type":"FileChange","id":"exec-31e870bd-9a1c-4cdc-a7fb-2babd839f2b8","changes":{"/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/README.md":{"type":"delete","content":"# Tally\n\nA CFO for a fleet of coding agents. Give it a backlog and a budget. It sizes\neach task, sends it to the cheapest model likely to finish it, stops any task\nthat overruns its spend ceiling, hands failed work to a stronger model, and\nends with a ledger tying spend to each attempt, its test result and any opened PR.\n\n**Start here: [Getting Started](docs/GETTING_STARTED.md)** covers the no-account demo, every init question, your first real run, troubleshooting and uninstall.\n\nStatus: early build. The next release is `tally-agents` version `0.2.0`; npm\n`latest` remains `0.1.0` until the maintainer publishes it.\n\n0.2.0 adds direct runners, opt-in parallel AO workers, model-isolated\ntrace-history routing and caps, failure diagnosis, and reviewed CrustData\npilot-company reports. Configurations produced by 0.1.0 init continue to run\nsequentially unchanged; its legacy `concurrency` field does not opt into\n`parallelWorkers`. New 0.2.0 configuration fields are not accepted by 0.1.0.\nThe GitHub release alternative is also available below. The seven-task demo uses simulated workers; real\nruns use Agent Orchestrator by default, or the direct runner (Claude Code or Codex),\nand record their attempts and evidence separately.\n\n## Install\n\nPrerequisites before you install:\n\n- Node 20 or later and npm.\n- Git, for installing from GitHub or from a source checkout.\n\nThe built CLI runtime is checked on Node 20 (20.20.2), including the seven-task\ndry demo. Vitest 5 is development-only and requires Node 22.12+ on the supported\n22.x line (or supported 24/26+ versions); source contributors should use that\nruntime for tests. The built npm release includes neither Vitest nor other dev\ndependencies and keeps its runtime requirement at Node 20+.\n\nThat is all the simulated demo and the dashboard need. They use no Agent\nOrchestrator account, coding-agent login or GitHub credentials.\n\nA real run on your own repository also needs, today:\n\n- **Agent Orchestrator (AO), installed and running**, for the default `runner: ao`.\n  Alternatively, set `runner: direct` in `tally.yaml`; Tally creates worktrees\n  and starts `claude -p` or `codex exec` itself, by each tier's harness, without\n  an AO daemon. A missing or signed-out harness stops the run; Tally never\n  substitutes one for another.\n- At least one coding agent that AO can start, installed and signed in: Codex\n  or Claude Code. `tally init` proposes model tiers only for the agents it\n  finds installed.\n- GitHub CLI, signed in with `gh auth login`, with access to the repository.\n  Tally reads issues and opens pull requests through it.\n- A test command for the repository that exits 0 when the work is good. Tally\n  accepts a task only when that command passes, so `tally init` writes no\n  configuration without one.\n\nneatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai are optional. Each is off unless you turn it on\nduring `tally init`, and the dashboard shows which are on, off or broken.\nWith Entire off or unavailable, sizing uses named paths and bounded identifier\nsearches in tracked repository files. It counts explicit new files and nearby\ntests without claiming graph dependencies. Specific target evidence increases\nconfidence; ambiguous or truncated searches remain cautious. `run --plan`\nprints the size, confidence, route, ceiling and price-history source without\nstarting a worker or writing history.\n\n### Direct Codex Runner\n\nThe direct Codex path has completed a real run: `run-20261011-130259Z`, using\nluna, produced [chip-design-groundup PR #18](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/18),\nwith both decision and worker trace ids and a Tally-created commit.\n\nWith `runner: direct`, a tier with `harness: codex` runs `codex exec --json` in\nthe task's worktree. It needs Codex on PATH and signed in with `codex login`.\nThe worker runs in Codex's `workspace-write` sandbox with approvals off and the\nnetwork off; it can write only in its worktree and one scratch directory, and\nTally never passes a bypass or full-access flag. That sandbox cannot write git\nmetadata, so the worker leaves its edits in the working tree and Tally commits\nthem. The repository's test command reaches the worker as a Tally-written\nscript outside the worktree; a test that needs the network fails inside the\nworker, and Tally's own verification afterwards is what decides the result.\n\nLive spend is read from that session's own Codex rollout file and priced at the\ntier's list price. Counts arrive at model-call boundaries, so a ceiling stop\ncan overshoot by one call. If the rollout file is missing or unreadable the\nworker is stopped rather than left unmetered. The rollout is an internal Codex\nformat and may change between Codex versions.\n\n### Headless Claude Runner\n\n`runner: direct` needs Claude Code installed on PATH and signed in with\n`claude auth login`. Keep each tier's `harness: claude-code` and explicit model;\nthe usual test command, list prices, budgets and integrations still apply.\n`claudeCode.allow` must contain the tool rules consented to in `tally init`;\nTally passes that list verbatim and refuses to launch Claude without it.\nDirect workers use scoped file-edit and git/test/setup allow rules with\n`--permission-mode dontAsk`, never bypass-permissions. Push and `gh` are denied\nto workers; Tally verifies and publishes. Setup runs in the new worktree before\nthe agent starts and is recorded separately as `setupDurationMs`.\n\nThe live meter reads [Claude's partial stream events](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/streaming-output)\nand deduplicates API message ids; final JSON usage settles the same session,\nnot an additional charge. Usage arrives at message boundaries, so a ceiling\ncan overshoot during a long generation or between watcher polls. Prompt-cache\nwrites use [Anthropic's 5-minute / 1-hour multipliers](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching)\nof 1.25x / 2x the configured input price. Existing ledger costs are unchanged.\nSubagents and MCP tools are disabled so their unmetered work cannot escape the\ntask ceiling. These are harness permission rules, not an OS isolation guarantee;\nconfigured commands and installed hooks must be trusted. Entire and neatlogs\nhooks are retained (no bare mode); verify capture through the run's recorded\nevidence and read-back rather than assuming that installed hooks prove delivery.\nPublished worktrees are removed; stopped/failed checkouts stay available for\nhandoff or recovery. A live direct process cannot be reattached after Tally exits.\n\n### AO Workers\n\nWith `runner: ao`, each tier chooses its own `harness` and model. Codex workers\nuse the project's consented `auto` mode. Claude Code workers use `default` at\nlaunch and a worktree-local `.claude/settings.local.json` with the consented\n`dontAsk` rules and exact Tally test/setup scripts. The local settings and\ninstructions are excluded from commits. No bypass-permissions mode is used.\nMixed AO tiers select the harness mode immediately before spawning under an\nin-process project lock; separate Tally processes and manual AO changes are not\ncoordinated by that lock. Both Codex AO and Claude Code AO have real-run evidence.\nCorrelated worker-trace read-back covers all four runner/harness paths; delivery\nstill needs capture hooks and read access, and unverified traces remain missing.\n\n### Real-Run Evidence\n\nAll four runner/harness paths have completed real runs on chip-design-groundup:\n\n| Path | Real-run PR evidence |\n|---|---|\n| Codex via AO | [#10](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/10) |\n| Claude Code via AO | [#13](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/13), [#16](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/16) |\n| Claude Code direct | [#14](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/14) |\n| Codex direct | [#18](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/18) |\n\nThese runs do not prove every workflow. The following remain unproven live:\n\n- Mixed AO spawning a real Codex worker after a Claude Code worker. The mixed\n  setup proof completed on Claude Code without escalating to Codex.\n- Parallel workers.\n- Escalation on the direct runner.\n- The no-account onboarding path.\n\nThe simulated demo is not evidence of any real worker path.\n\nThree ways in:\n\n1. **npm (primary):** `npm install -g tally-agents`.\n2. **GitHub (alternative, available now):** `npm install -g github:Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack#release`.\n   The `release` branch includes the compiled CLI and records its source commit\n   in `release-source.json`; no TypeScript or manual build is needed. The main\n   branch is source-only and is not the global-install target.\n3. **Source checkout:** `git clone https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack`,\n   enter `neatlogs-hack`, and run `npm install` followed by `npm run build`.\n   Then use `node bin/tally.js` in place of `tally` in the commands below.\n   After editing TypeScript sources, run `npm run build` again. Generated\n   `dist/` files are not committed to main.\n\nThe npm package is named `tally-agents`; the installed command is still\n`tally`.\n\nMaintainers can build a release tree with\n`npm run release:branch -- --source origin/main --out /tmp/tally-release`.\nAdd `--push` to update the dedicated `release` branch (never publishes to npm).\nThe main-push workflow builds and smoke-checks that tree before updating it.\nFor npm publication, pack the built `release` tree, not the source-only main\ncheckout. Check that `release-source.json` identifies the intended main commit,\nthen inspect and smoke-test the tarball before publishing that exact artifact.\nThe built package includes `dist/` and has no build or install lifecycle scripts.\n`npm run test:git-install` checks the GitHub `#release` URL with an empty cache\nand temporary global prefix, then checks version, help, demo and dashboard\noutside the checkout. Pass another Git URL after `--` to check a candidate.\n\n## Quickstart\n\nAfter either global installation, start in an empty directory:\n\n```bash\ntally demo\ntally dashboard\n```\n\nThe demo writes `.tally/runs/` in that directory. Open the URL printed by\n`tally dashboard`; it reads the latest run and lists recorded runs. Keep it\nrunning while viewing the page, and press Ctrl-C to stop it. If its default\nport is occupied, use `tally dashboard --port 0` for a free port.\n\n`tally demo --live` keeps workers simulated while checking the configured\nread-only integrations. Bundled cfo.ai workspace/table values are placeholders;\nmissing credentials are reported, and no real worker is launched.\n\n### A Provided Tarball\n\nWith only a packed `tally-agents-0.2.0.tgz` in your current directory, these\ncommands install into a temporary prefix without changing your normal global\ninstallation, then follow the same quickstart:\n\n```bash\nnpm install -g --prefix \"$PWD/tally-install\" ./tally-agents-0.2.0.tgz\nexport PATH=\"$PWD/tally-install/bin:$PATH\"\ntally demo\ntally dashboard --port 0\n```\n\nNo checkout, manual build or `tally init` is needed for the demo.\n\n### First Run On Your Own Repo\n\nCheck the prerequisites for a real run above first. Then, from the root of the\nrepository you want Tally to work on:\n\n1. **Label the issues Tally should take.** Tally reads open issues that carry\n   one label (`tally` unless you choose another). The label must exist and be\n   on at least one open issue:\n\n   ```bash\n   gh label create tally --description \"Work for Tally\"\n   gh issue edit 12 --add-label tally\n   gh issue list --label tally --state open\n   ```\n\n   The last command is the query Tally makes. If it prints nothing, Tally has\n   no tasks to work on.\n2. **`tally init`** asks for a budget and the issue label, shows the\n   short configuration summary and the AO changes it would make, and applies\n   nothing until you confirm. Type the label exactly as it is on GitHub.\n3. **`tally doctor`** prints one line per integration: connected, missing or\n   broken, with the fix.\n4. **`tally run --plan`** lists the tasks it found and the model each would go\n   to. It starts no workers and spends nothing. Check the task count here\n   before going further.\n5. **`tally run`** starts real workers through the configured AO or direct runner. It spends against your\n   agent accounts and can push branches and open pull requests on the\n   repository.\n6. **`tally dashboard`**, in a second terminal in the same directory, prints a\n   local URL showing the run as it happens.\n\nMore on `tally init`: neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai are separate opt-in prompts,\ndefaulting to No. When both coding CLIs are available, a numbered menu defaults\nto Codex; if only one is signed in, init selects it automatically. A stopped\nor unavailable AO daemon selects the direct runner without asking. New configs\ndefault to one budget-checked retry on the `cheap` tier after explicit model\naccess rejection; re-init preserves an existing fallback setting. Set\n`modelAccessFallbackTier` in `tally.yaml` or use\n`--model-access-fallback-tier <name>` to change it. It displays a compact proposal (full YAML with `--verbose`), install record in\n`.tally/`, AO project registration/configuration and setup hook, then asks for\nfinal confirmation (default No) before applying changes. Use `tally init\n--dry-run` to preview without applying, or `--yes` only when you consent to\napplying detected defaults without prompts. Review model tiers, prices, tests\nand ceilings before starting workers. Claude's consented rules are installed\nlocally before launch. If Neatlogs is enabled, init also installs its hooks in\nthe selected agents' global settings; those hooks trace matching sessions on\nthis computer, including sessions outside Tally.\nThe final summary shows the permission policy for this AO project's workers.\nUninstall restores owned project settings; it never enables bypass-permissions.\n\nInteractive init requires a terminal. Piped input exits with a plain message\ninstead of waiting for answers. For scripted onboarding, pass values explicitly:\n\n```sh\ntally init --yes --test-command \"node --check js/app.js\" \\\n  --setup-command \"\" --budget 5 --issue-label tally \\\n  --neatlogs yes --entire no --cfoai yes\n```\n\nAn empty setup flag means no setup. Selecting Neatlogs runs a synthetic key\nprobe and installs global worker hooks for the selected coding agent(s).\nPast-run history connects automatically when its read credentials are already\nconfigured; otherwise Tally uses local history. Selecting cfo.ai enables its\nmanaged scenario guide and per-attempt reports; creating missing spend tables\nstill has a separate confirmation. Entire installation, login, repository\ncapture, settings publication, and mirror creation keep their own steps.\n\nInit detects test/setup commands from repository evidence and proposes tiers\nonly for installed Codex or Claude Code agents. When no test command is found,\nit asks for a shell command: Tally cannot verify work without one. Blank input\nor `--yes` without either a detected command or `--test-command` fails without writing configuration\nor changing AO settings. An undetected setup command is optional and never\nguessed.\n\nAO does not need to be on your ordinary terminal's PATH. Tally resolves the\nCLI from PATH first, then `TALLY_AO_BIN`, then `repo.aoBin` in `tally.yaml`\n(relative to that file), then the macOS Agent Orchestrator app bundle under\n`/Applications` or `~/Applications`. For a custom installation:\n\n```bash\nexport TALLY_AO_BIN=\"/Applications/Agent Orchestrator.app/Contents/Resources/daemon/ao\"\ntally init\n```\n\nDoctor and real runs use the same resolver. Init checks AO, git, GitHub auth\nand requested integrations before applying changes. An Entire opt-in requires\nEntire to be enabled in the target repo; init prints `entire enable` and stops\nif it is not, rather than installing capture hooks without consent.\n\nOnce confirmed, init records progress in `.tally/init-pending.json`. If an AO\nor file operation fails, it lists confirmed changes and unwritten files and\nprints the exact init command to resume. Re-running resumes only its owned,\nunchanged files; edited configs or changed AO settings are never overwritten.\nThe recovery record is removed after init and doctor complete. This is a\nrecoverable transaction, not an atomic transaction spanning the AO daemon and\nthe filesystem: an interrupted call may require checking its outcome.\n\nRun `tally init` again to connect a product you skipped. It proposes an\nintegration-only diff, preserves the rest of your config and AO ownership,\nand asks for confirmation (default No). `--dry-run` shows the diff without\nwriting. With no proposed changes it exits cleanly. Interrupted re-init\nwrites can be resumed using the command printed in the error.\n\n`tally run --dry-run` runs the same loop with simulated workers instead of\nreal ones.\n\n`tally uninstall` lists the files and AO settings it owns and asks for\nconfirmation before removing them. It refuses to overwrite edits made since\ninit. It leaves run history, credentials and the globally installed CLI in\nplace; remove that CLI separately with `npm uninstall -g tally-agents`.\n\n### Static Snapshot\n\n`tally publish --out site` writes a self-contained static dashboard,\ndefaulting to real recorded runs. Select folders explicitly with\n`tally publish --out site --runs run-id-1,run-id-2`; use this for simulated demo\ndata. It scans exported files for sensitive content and prints file sizes and\nthe total. Serve the directory on a static HTTP host, including under a\nsub-path. This creates local files and does not deploy them.\n\nDuring development, `npm run tally -- <command>` runs the CLI from source.\n\nFor routine validation, run:\n\n```bash\nnpm test\n```\n\n## Commands\n\n| Command | What it will do |\n|---|---|\n| `tally run` | Run the backlog against the budget in `tally.yaml`. Flags: `--dry-run`, `--budget <usd>`, `--only <task>`, `--baseline`, `--config <path>`, `--plan`, `--dashboard`/`--open`. |\n| `tally experiment` | Compare baseline and Tally modes with `--dry-run --repeats N` (default 2). Paid AO experiments require explicit `--yes --budget <usd>` consent and a configured test timeout. The budget is an aggregate safety cap; baseline has no per-task ceiling. Both modes verify locally without pushing or opening PRs, run sequentially from the same pinned base, and close workers on interruption. Real comparison safety monitoring has not yet been exercised live. |\n| `tally demo` | Run the full loop with simulated workers on `demo/tally.yaml` and `demo/sample-backlog.yaml`. No accounts or keys. Writes `.tally/runs/<run-id>/run.json` and `ledger.json`; add `--dashboard` for the one-command demo, or `--live` to use read-only integrations while workers stay fake. `--live` does not write CFO spend, but may send decision traces to Neatlogs. |\n| `tally dashboard` | Serve `dashboard/` and `.tally/runs/` read-only on `127.0.0.1`. The printed URL passes `?data=/runs/latest/` so the page reads the stable latest-run mirror. |\n| `tally init` | Preview files and AO changes, then ask final default-No consent. `--dry-run` previews; `--yes` applies defaults without prompts. Claude workers require the consented scoped allow-list. |\n| `tally uninstall` | Confirm removal of init-owned configuration and AO settings; preserve history and credentials. |\n| `tally logo` | Replay the first-run logo animation. It plays once by itself, the first time you run bare `tally` or `tally init` in a terminal; after that bare `tally` and `--help` show a small still mark. Nothing is drawn when output is piped, in CI, on `TERM=dumb`, or for any other command. `TALLY_NO_ANIMATION=1` turns the motion off and `NO_COLOR` gives plain text. |\n| `tally doctor` | One line per integration: connected, missing or broken, with the fix. |\n| `tally login cfoai` | Sign in to cfo.ai; tokens are cached outside the repository. |\n| `tally traces drain --run <id>` | Recover only that finished run's worker traces, verify readable spans, then update saved status and worker trace ids. Use `--output <run-dir>` for a run stored elsewhere. |\n| `tally market --limit 5 --max-credits 4` | Find and qualify likely pilot companies with CrustData; checks account pricing before a bounded search. |\n| `tally market --plan --limit 10` | Free account-pricing check; prints a conservative maximum credit estimate without searching. |\n| `tally market --jobs --limit 25 --max-credits 1` | Find engineering posts with explicit coding-tool evidence; group by posting company and label hiring intent, never confirmed adoption. |\n| `tally market --replay .tally/market/latest/query.json --output /tmp/tally-market-replay` | Render the saved snapshot again with no API calls or credentials. |\n| `tally publish --out site` | Static dashboard export with a sensitive-content scan. No deployment. |\n\n`run --dashboard` starts serving before the first task and prints the URL\nimmediately. `--open` also opens that URL in the system browser. The server\ncontinues after the run for inspection, until Ctrl+C. Terminal output includes\nroutes, worker session IDs, spend updates at most every ten seconds, attempt\nresults, PR URLs and a final summary. `--budget` is a cap for this run, not a\nreplacement for the monthly budget: Tally applies the lower of the run cap and\nthe remaining monthly budget, including for the direct sequential runner. Plan\nand summary show both, including the amount available now and monthly source.\nWhen cfo.ai supplies the monthly budget, init asks only for a local fallback\nafter connecting it. Worker trace IDs require correlated trace read-back;\nunverified delivery is shown as missing rather than success. See\n[Human approval points](docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md) for operations that still\nrequire explicit user consent or review.\n\n### Pilot Discovery\n\n`tally market` finds and qualifies likely pilot companies, not adoption or market\nsize. The default hypothesis is Software Development companies with 20-500\nemployees. It does not infer coding-agent seats or use from headcount. An optional\n`--keyword` filters an exact phrase in the company description, which can also\nmatch vendors and does not prove adoption.\n\nLive searches require `CRUSTDATA_API_KEY` in `~/.tally/.env`. Before each single\nbatch Tally checks free account pricing and prints a conservative maximum estimate\nincluding premium filters and returned fields. `--max-credits` (default 10)\nrefuses an estimated over-cap search; it is a per-call estimate, not a server-side\nhard billing limit. No pagination, enrichment or retries occur. Use `--plan` to\ncheck pricing without a paid search. `--dry-run` requires cached `--replay`.\n\nThe output directory contains the exact `query.json`, `companies.json`, CSV and\n`report.md`: API version, query hash, retrieval timestamp, returned versus API\nmatch count, reported credits, and record-update dates when available. These\ndates are not publication dates. Cached replay preserves provenance and calls\nno API, even without credentials; it does not rerun the search for fresh data.\nLegacy snapshots without matching provenance must be recaptured rather than\nsilently treated as new evidence.\n\nReview a saved snapshot with `--replay <query.json> --review <reviews.json>`.\nReviews are an array of `{ id, label, reason, sourceUrl, sourceDate, excerpt? }`;\nlabels are `candidate`, `vendor`, `hiring intent`, `verified use`, or `rejected`.\nUse `null` for unknown source dates. Intent/use labels require a sourced excerpt;\nTally checks the structure, not the truth of manual judgements. Without a review,\nevery returned row remains an unverified candidate. JSON/CSV retain all rows;\nthe short Markdown report shows at most ten. No deduplication or extrapolation\nis applied to company search.\n\nThe stronger second stage is `--jobs`: a single batch (default 25) of engineering\nposts from the past 90 days whose descriptions mention Claude Code, Cursor,\nGitHub Copilot, Codex or AI coding agents. It uses Job Search, with no premium\ncompany filters. Ambiguous Cursor/Codex mentions need nearby AI/coding context;\nrows missing title, company identity, source URL or date are dropped. Accepted\nrows are grouped by normalized domain (or company id) and labelled hiring intent.\nThat is evidence in a job advertisement, not a verified deployment, current open\nrole, buyer or purchase commitment. Posting entities can be staffing companies;\ncross-posts under different domains are not assumed to be the same buyer.\nDates are the API's `metadata.date_added`, not independently verified publication\ndates. JSON retains grouped job evidence and CSV includes title/excerpt.\n\nUse `--jobs --secondary <saved-company-query.json>` to include a previously\nreviewed firmographic snapshot as a secondary table without re-fetching it.\nThe job snapshot embeds that secondary evidence, so `--replay <job-query.json>`\nlater renders both tables with no API calls. Query match counts remain database\ncounts, never adoption or market size.\n\n## Routing Evidence\n\nSee the [Tally business plan](docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md) for share-of-savings pricing,\nthe measured three-task comparison, explicitly labelled unit-economics and\npilot-funnel assumptions, cfo.ai usage and the remaining commercial risks.\n\nFrom a source checkout, build and analyze an audited list of real run directories:\n\n```sh\nnpm run build\nnode scripts/routing-evidence.mjs --manifest runs.json --out /tmp/routing-evidence\n```\n\n`runs.json` is a JSON array of paths containing `run.json` and `ledger.json`.\nThe script writes a Markdown report, task CSV, JSON provenance and three SVG\ncharts. It excludes simulations and active runs, keeps skipped/interrupted\noutcomes in the denominator, and labels missing historical signals as missing.\nUnattributed historical decisions are not silently assigned to prior or\ntrace-driven cohorts. Correlations are descriptive, not cost predictions.\n\nAdd `--live-traces` for bounded read-only neatlogs verification using Tally's\nread token and project id. Without that flag it only replays the output folder's\ncached `trace-readback.json`, making no API call. Optional `--issues issues.json`\naccepts a URL-keyed map of current issue titles/bodies for historical runs;\nthese are labelled current text, not run-start snapshots. No paid workers or\ncfo.ai writes are performed. Before/after savings require matched measured\ntasks; missing baseline or v2 cohorts are reported as **not measured**.\n\nFor the matched three-pass experiment, add `--comparison-dir <folder>`.\nThe folder contains `mentor-comparison-plan-signals.json` and, when results\narrive, `comparison-passes.json`: an object mapping `A`, `B`, `C` to arrays of\nreal run-directory paths (absolute or relative to that folder). A is top-tier\nbaseline, B is Tally v1/priors, C is Tally v2/trace history. Missing passes stay\npending. The report joins signals only at the exact pinned base SHA and task\ntext, reports cost/verdict/attempts per task and per pass, and charts sizing\nagainst real costs with per-pass correlations. A pass C label does not itself\nprove that history influenced every route or that any cap was learned.\nUse `--comparison-passes <json>` to keep the labelled path manifest elsewhere\nwithout changing the measured signals folder.\n\n## Layout\n\nRouting history is isolated by task size, tier name and actual model id. Changing\na tier's model does not reuse the old model's pass rate or learned cap. Legacy\nhistory without an explicit model remains available for audit but is excluded\nfrom model-specific decisions; these fall back to the prior/configured cap\nuntil enough new matching observations exist. History trace names include the\nmodel id so the public neatlogs read API can identify it without hidden attributes.\n\nExperiment summaries are atomically updated after each completed run at\n`.tally/runs/experiments/<experimentId>/summary.json`. The dashboard server lists\nthem in `/runs/index.json` under `experiments`. Each underlying run retains its\nown folder, `mode`, and `repeat`. Simulated cost remains labeled as simulated.\nThe aggregate watcher polls once a second; reported final cost can exceed the\nstop threshold due to polling or metering lag and is never clipped to the cap.\n\n| Folder | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `schema/` | The contract every part reads: TypeScript types, JSON Schema, validators, and a sample finished run in `schema/fixtures/finished-run/`. |\n| `engine/` | The run loop, run state and ledger. |\n| `adapters/` | One thin module per outside tool (Agent Orchestrator, Entire, neatlogs, cfo.ai, GitHub). |\n| `agent/` | The CFO agent: sizing, routing, ceilings, escalation. |\n| `cli/` | The `tally` command. |\n| `dashboard/` | Static page that reads the ledger and run state. |\n| `demo/` | Sample backlog and scripted outcomes for `tally demo`. |\n\n## Configuration\n\nWith neatlogs on, Tally drains the completed Codex worker's hook spool from\nits own process, outside the worker sandbox. It uses the official package's\nspool API (there is no flush CLI), matching native session IDs or the worktree\npath. Only successfully delivered batches are acknowledged; failed,\nunrelated and malformed batches remain queued. A locked or incomplete drain\nis reported as missing worker trace delivery. After all workers exit, Tally\nretries once when a worker drain reported lock contention and verifies the\nresult with Neatlogs before updating the final run status. If delivery remains\nunverified, the run keeps the batches and shows `tally traces drain --run <id>`\nfor recovery. Dry runs do not drain workers.\n\nWith `integrations.entire: true`, enable Entire in the target repository and\nreview and trust its hooks in the coding agent before starting workers. Tally records\nthe task commit's `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer and its native `entireSessionId`\nseparately from the AO `sessionId`. Logs-only checkpoints retain conversation\ncontext; escalation defaults to a metadata-only handoff containing the\ncheckpoint/session IDs, branch, recorded and changed files, attempt verdict,\nand task text. It does not generate a summary or export a transcript.\n`entire.generatedSummaries: true` explicitly opts into Entire's summary\nprovider (`checkpoint explain --generate`), which may export session content;\nthis setting is off by default and requires the user's approval.\nWhen Tally publishes a verified branch, it also pushes the checkpoint refs\nnamed by its new commits to `origin` and to an `entire` remote when configured.\nCheckpoint upload failures are reported as publishing failures. Dry runs do\nnot publish branches or checkpoints.\n\nEntire checkpoint records live in separate Git refs, not on the task branch.\nTally keeps the checkpoint ID and native session ID, and attaches a clickable\nEntire session link only when the Entire CLI confirms that the GitHub repo has\na ready mirror. The link opens the session that contains the checkpoint; a\nreviewer still needs access to that repo in Entire. Without a ready mirror,\nTally keeps the checkpoint ID available in the local ledger rather than adding\na link that may not open.\n\nAfter each run has completed its Neatlogs read-back, Tally adds an evidence\nsection to the generated PR body when links are available: Entire session and\ncheckpoint, Neatlogs routing and worker traces, and the configured cfo.ai\nbudget and spend tables. Worker trace links are added only after Tally verifies\ntheir read-back. These dashboard links use the viewer's existing account\naccess; Tally does not publish public trace shares.\n\nTally assumes nothing about the repository it works on. The repository, test\ncommand, model tiers, list prices, ceilings and budget all come from\n`tally.yaml` (a tier is a name, harness, model id, provider, list price and ceiling share); see `schema/fixtures/finished-run/tally.yaml` for a complete\nexample and `schema/tally-config.schema.json` for the rules.\n\nMoney in the ledger and run state is stored as integer nanos of USD\n(1 USD = 1,000,000,000). List-price estimates multiply aggregate tokens by the\nconfigured rates in `tally.yaml`; they are normalized estimates, not invoices.\nNew ledger rows snapshot those rates in `price` and record their source and\nverification status in `priceNote`. Unknown models/providers or rates that\ndisagree with the verified snapshot are labelled unverified. Operator-supplied\n`tiers[].priceNote` is retained as a config note, not independently verified.\n\n### Codex Tier Prices\n\nOfficial base text rates checked 2026-10-11, USD per million tokens:\n\n| Model | Input | Cached Input | Output | Source |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| gpt-5.6-luna | 0.20 | 0.02 | 1.20 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-luna) |\n| gpt-5.6-terra | 2.00 | 0.20 | 12.00 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-terra) |\n| gpt-5.6-sol | 4.00 | 0.40 | 20.00 | [OpenAI](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-sol) |\n\n```yaml\ntiers:\n  - { name: cheap, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-luna, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 0.2, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.02, outputUsdPerMTok: 1.2 }, ceilingFraction: 0.2 }\n  - { name: middle, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-terra, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 2, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.2, outputUsdPerMTok: 12 }, ceilingFraction: 0.5 }\n  - { name: top, harness: codex, sessionMode: tui, provider: openai, model: gpt-5.6-sol, price: { inputUsdPerMTok: 4, cachedInputUsdPerMTok: 0.4, outputUsdPerMTok: 20 }, ceilingFraction: 1 }\n```\n\nPer-request prompts above 272K input tokens have input/output uplifts;\ncache writes also have an uplift. AO's aggregate token buckets cannot resolve\nthese adjustments. Sol's published promotional rates last at least through\n2026-11-21; recheck before subsequent runs. Historical costs are not repriced.\n\nFor dry runs, set `backlog.source: file` and point `backlog.file` at YAML like:\n\n```yaml\ntasks:\n  - id: T1\n    title: Add validation for empty player name\n    size: small\n    outcomes: [passed]\n  - id: T2\n    title: Persist settings after restart\n    size: medium\n    outcomes: [stopped, passed]\n```\n\n`outcomes` is optional and defaults to one passed simulated attempt.\n\nThe latest run is also mirrored to `.tally/runs/latest/run.json` and\n`.tally/runs/latest/ledger.json` so the dashboard has one stable path to poll.\n\n## Secrets\n\nKeys live in `~/.tally/.env`, outside every repository, and are loaded from\nthere. Never put them in this repo; `.env` files are git-ignored.\n"}},"status":"completed","stdout":"Success. Updated the following files:\nD /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/README.md\n","stderr":""},"started_at_ms":1791835078710,"completed_at_ms":1791835078728}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:57:59.026Z","ordinal":17209,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","item":{"type":"FileChange","id":"exec-a125f23d-f81b-42ee-9957-726fd0b1fe6d","changes":{"/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md":{"type":"add","content":"# Tally Agent Guide\n\nInstructions for an AI coding agent asked to \"use Tally on this repo\".\nSelf-contained for tally-agents 0.2.1. Work from the target repository root.\nThat request alone is not permission to spend, publish or change external data.\n\n## Hard Rules\n\n- Never merge Tally's PRs; the human reviews/merges.\n- Never push to a default branch. If Entire setup offers a settings commit\n  and default-branch push, stop and hand that step to the human. Do not approve\n  it on their behalf. Normal task publication targets worker branches.\n- Never use bypass-permissions, danger/full-access flags or loosen a sandbox.\n- Never edit tally.yaml/install records to skip a consent. Have the human\n  rerun init and approve the exact current rules instead.\n- Never run two Tally runs against one cfo.ai page at once; coordinate with\n  other operators. Cross-process spend updates are not atomic.\n- Never paste/read out keys or tokens, reuse another tool's tokens, or put\n  secrets in YAML/PRs/issues. Tally stores its secrets outside the repository.\n- Never guess tests, model access, prices or savings. Do not create issues,\n  install global hooks or start paid work without the applicable approval.\n\n## Preconditions: Read-Only Checks\n\nRun these; summarize status without exposing account details or credentials:\n\n```sh\npwd\ngit status --short\ngit rev-parse --show-toplevel\nnode --version\nnpm --version\ngit --version\ngh --version\ngh auth status\ntally --version\ntally init --help\ntally run --help\n```\n\nNode must be 20+. Published version checked 13 October 2026: 0.2.1. Installed\nand source versions can differ. If absent, ask before installing with\n`npm install -g tally-agents`. The installed CLI is `tally`, not `tally-agents`.\n\nCheck the human's chosen coding CLIs without making inference calls:\n\n```sh\nclaude --version\nclaude auth status\ncodex --version\ncodex login status\n```\n\nClaude must report loggedIn true; Codex must report logged in. Missing/sign-out\nblocks that harness; no silent substitution. Hand login to the human. Sign-in\ndoes not prove model entitlement; ask about access to each configured model.\n\nIf AO is installed and requested:\n\n```sh\nao version\nao status --json\n```\n\nRequire ready/healthy; direct needs no AO. Tally also resolves AO through\nTALLY_AO_BIN, repo.aoBin and macOS app bundles, so absent PATH alone is not\nproof it is missing. Do not modify project settings to make a check pass.\n\nFor enabled Entire, read state first:\n\n```sh\nentire --version\nentire auth status --json\nentire plugin list\nentire status --json\n```\n\n## Named Human Decisions\n\nPut each decision to the human and retain the answer:\n\n| Decision | Human Choice |\n| --- | --- |\n| Budget and caps | Monthly/local fallback budget, USD run cap and per-size/tier ceilings; escalation reserves; possible metering overshoot |\n| Runner | AO with approved parallel count, or sequential direct |\n| Agents and tiers | Claude, Codex or mixed; model IDs, rates, entitlement and any access-denial fallback |\n| Tasks/verification | Exact issue label/task set, meaningful test and optional setup commands; setup executes repo code |\n| neatlogs integration | Decision export, history read/project access and optional global hooks affecting sessions outside Tally |\n| Entire integration | Login/plugin/capture/hook trust/mirror and human-owned default-branch settings publication |\n| cfo.ai integration | Workspace/scenario, monthly budget/spend write-back, and separate missing-table creation approval |\n| Claude allow-list | Scoped file writes, git add/commit/status/diff and content-bound test/setup script rules |\n| Entire default-branch commit | Human reviews files and performs this separately; agent must stop before approving any push |\n| cfo.ai reports | Managed guide and organization-memory attempt reports; includes task titles/IDs, model/tokens/pricing/cost/result/decision, budgets and links |\n| PR evidence links | Entire/neatlogs/cfo.ai URLs become visible to PR readers; no dedicated init finance-sharing question, so ask yourself |\n| Paid run/publication | Explicit approval for this task set, cap, worker branch pushes and PR creation, never merges |\n\nFresh cfo.ai init names Ari context; re-init asks a generic integration question\nand can add reports to an existing installation. Explain before re-init.\nReports exclude issue bodies/source/diffs/prompts/transcripts/raw spans, but\ntitles/result notes are shared metadata; sanitization is not a privacy guarantee.\nDeclined integrations must stay off; do not force them on in YAML.\n\n## Init: Preview Before Applying\n\nPrefer interactive `tally init` for human-owned account/consent steps. Piped\ninput is rejected. Do not automate browser approval or secret entry.\n\nExplicit non-interactive preview, integrations off:\n\n```sh\ntally init --dry-run --yes --runner direct --harness codex \\\n  --budget 5 --issue-label tally --test-command 'npm test' \\\n  --setup-command '' --neatlogs no --entire no --cfoai no --verbose\n```\n\nReplace budget/label/test/setup with approved values. Do not assume npm test\nexists. Empty setup means none. After explicit approval the apply form removes\n`--dry-run`; `--yes` is not blanket consent to external integration actions.\n\nVerified init flags:\n\n- `--config PATH`: config location (default tally.yaml).\n- `--runner ao|direct`, `--harness claude-code|codex|mixed`: chosen execution.\n- `--parallel N`: approved AO concurrency; direct stays sequential.\n- `--budget USD`, `--issue-label LABEL`, `--test-command CMD`, `--setup-command CMD`.\n- `--allow-claude`: approves the printed repository allow-list; only after the\n  human reviewed those exact test/setup script contents.\n- `--model-access-fallback-tier NAME`: one budget-checked explicit model-access\n  denial fallback, not permission to choose another model arbitrarily.\n- `--neatlogs yes|no`, `--entire yes|no`, `--cfoai yes|no`: explicit opt-ins.\n- `--verbose`: full proposed YAML; `--dry-run`: preview only.\n\nYes integration flags do not guarantee unattended onboarding: OAuth, project/\nworkspace choice, table creation and hook trust can still need the human.\nSelecting cfo.ai includes reporting when supported; neatlogs hooks can be global.\nIf any required decision is missing, stop rather than infer it.\n\nAfter init, using the chosen label/budget:\n\n```sh\ntally doctor --config tally.yaml\ngh issue list --label tally --state open\ntally run --plan --config tally.yaml --budget 5\n```\n\nDo not create/label issues without approval. Doctor checks connection, not\npaid model entitlement or delivery of every checkpoint/trace feature.\n\n## Read the Plan Before Spending\n\nPlan starts no worker and writes no spend/PR; it may read GitHub, repository/\nEntire signals, history and cfo.ai budget. It is not offline.\nTell the human task count/IDs, model/harness/tier, size/confidence, per-attempt\nceilings, reason and history source. Distinguish observed samples versus priors,\nand configured versus P90-derived caps (five successful size/tier/model samples).\nReport run-cap/monthly-remaining minimum and escalation reservations, not just\nthe first-attempt spend. Off/missing integrations and local fallbacks matter.\nAsk again if effective task set, budget or permissions changed since approval.\n\nOptional simulation with the reviewed configuration:\n\n```sh\ntally run --dry-run --plan --config tally.yaml --budget 5\ntally run --dry-run --config tally.yaml --budget 5\n```\n\nFake workers do not prove model access or work quality. Dry runs can export\ndecision traces with neatlogs on, but never write simulated spend/Ari reports.\n`tally demo` uses bundled integrations-off data for a no-account simulation.\n\n## Start Only the Approved Run\n\nAfter explicit approval for spending and task-branch/PR publication:\n\n```sh\ntally run --config tally.yaml --budget 5 --dashboard --port 0\n```\n\nRun flags verified in current help: `--only TASK_ID` (use plan's exact ID),\n`--parallel N` (AO only), `--open` (browser), `--config PATH`, `--budget USD`,\n`--dashboard`, `--port N`, `--plan`, `--dry-run` and `--baseline`.\nBaseline removes task ceilings and sends work to the strongest tier: never use\nwithout separate approval. Ordinary run needs no `--yes`; executing it is the\npaid/publication action. Do not add paid experiment/retry runs opportunistically.\n\nRecord the printed run ID/output path/dashboard URL immediately. Serving\ncontinues after completion until Ctrl+C. Direct workers cannot be reattached\nafter Tally exits. Inspect existing PRs and saved state before restarting;\nrestarting can spend again.\n\n## Read run.json and ledger.json\n\nDefault output: `.tally/runs/RUN_ID/run.json` and `ledger.json`, relative to\nthe current working directory; trust printed paths. `latest/` mirrors the\nlatest run. Ledger is an array of attempt rows; run.json contains task/budget/\nintegration state. Parse JSON, do not infer success from a terminal exit alone:\n\n```sh\nnode -e 'const fs=require(\"node:fs\");const r=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(\".tally/runs/latest/run.json\",\"utf8\"));const l=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(\".tally/runs/latest/ledger.json\",\"utf8\"));console.log(JSON.stringify({runId:r.runId,status:r.status,dryRun:r.dryRun,budget:r.budget,integrations:r.integrations,tasks:r.tasks.map(t=>({id:t.taskId,status:t.status,reason:t.statusReason,usd:t.spentNanos/1e9,pr:t.prUrl})),attempts:l.map(a=>({id:a.attemptId,tier:a.tier,model:a.model,usd:a.estimatedCostNanos/1e9,result:a.result,reason:a.resultReason,failureAssessment:a.failureAssessment,pr:a.prUrl}))},null,2));'\n```\n\nUSD fields are integer nanos: divide by 1e9. Run status running/finished/\ninterrupted differs from task pending/running/passed/failed/skipped;\n**finished does not mean all tasks passed**. Live task costs can precede ended\nledger rows. Sum all attempts including stops/errors/escalations, not only\npassing ones. price/priceNote/costSource explain estimates, not invoices.\n\nfailureAssessment, when present, records kind/source/evidence/retryable.\nLedger fallback is not trace-derived. traceId is CFO decision; workerTraceId is\nthe separate worker. Null/unverified IDs are not evidence of delivery.\ncheckpointId/entireSessionId differ from AO sessionId. Review scopeWarnings,\npermissionWarnings and integration detail; do not disclose raw transcripts or\nfinancial snapshots without approval.\n\n## Failures: Correct Responses\n\n| Symptom | Response |\n| --- | --- |\n| Reservation/next ceiling does not fit | Report task and available budget; ask about priority/new cap, never increase it yourself |\n| AO project not found/not ready | Inspect doctor/displayed AO diagnostic; human repairs/re-inits or chooses direct; never silently substitute |\n| Signed-out/missing harness | Report the selected CLI, hand login to human, rerun doctor/plan |\n| Model unavailable | Only the configured budget-checked access fallback may retry; do not choose a guessed model |\n| Missing/stale Claude consent | Human reruns init and reviews exact rules; never patch/bypass permissions |\n| Ceiling stop | Read spend/branch/checkpoint and classification source; Tally may perform the approved affordable escalation, not your same-tier retry |\n| Verify failed | Report command/verdict; preserve branch, never weaken tests or merge; escalation stays inside the approved budget |\n| Tool/permission/environment error | Stronger models may hit the same wall; ask for repair, not speculative paid retries |\n| neatlogs error/pending worker trace | Preserve queued batches/ledger; report local fallback versus unverified delivery, not all-features success |\n| Entire no symbols/hosted recall unavailable | Code-search sizing can be valid; capture and hosted recall are different; no mirror without consent |\n| cfo.ai read/write/report error | Report budget source/sanitized detail/CSV fallback; reconcile before rerunning and avoid concurrent writers |\n| PR push/evidence failure | Inspect existing branches/PRs first; publishing retries push once automatically; no force/default-branch push |\n\nFinished-run trace recovery is `tally traces drain --run RUN_ID`: network\nexport/read-back, not inference. Ask before export; restrict it to this run,\nnever delete unrelated queued batches.\n\n## Report Back\n\nGive run ID/state, passed/failed/skipped counts, each PR link, cost including\nfailures/retries versus cap, verification outcomes, failure classification and\nsource/evidence, off/broken integrations and fallback, unverified evidence,\nand spend/report write-back status. Distinguish simulations from real work and\nestimates from invoices. Name what is unproven; leave PRs open and state that\nyou merged nothing.\n"},"/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/package.json":{"type":"update","unified_diff":"@@ -34,2 +34,4 @@\n     \"docs/\",\n+    \"docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md\",\n+    \"llms.txt\",\n     \"schema/*.schema.json\",\n@@ -42,2 +44,3 @@\n     \"!dist/**/testing/**\",\n+    \"!docs/assets/**\",\n     \"!dashboard/.gitkeep\"\n","move_path":null},"/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/llms.txt":{"type":"add","content":"# Tally\n\n> A CFO for coding agents: route, cap, escalate, verify and open PRs. Never merge.\n\n## Instructions\n\n- [Agent guide](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/main/docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md): Self-contained commands, human choices, safety and failure responses; read first.\n- [Getting started](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/main/docs/GETTING_STARTED.md): Human onboarding and troubleshooting.\n- [Evidence](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/main/docs/EVIDENCE.md): Recorded proofs and limits.\n- [Business plan](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/main/docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md): Measured results and labelled assumptions.\n"},"/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/README.md":{"type":"add","content":"![Meet Tally, the CFO for your agent fleet: pixel tally mark on a dark background](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/main/docs/assets/tally-banner.png)\n\nTally is a CFO for coding agents: give it GitHub issues and a budget, and it routes, caps and verifies the work before opening pull requests.\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\nnpm install -g tally-agents\n```\n\nPublished version: **0.2.1**, checked on npm on 13 October 2026.\nRequires Node 20+; real work also needs Git, signed-in GitHub CLI and at least\none signed-in coding agent. Optional integrations are not required.\n\n## 60-Second Quick Start\n\nFrom your repository root, with open issues carrying your chosen label:\n\n```sh\ntally init\ntally run --plan\ntally run --budget 3 --dashboard\n```\n\nInit asks about runner, agents, verification, budget and integrations. Review\nthe plan before the last command: **it starts paid workers and may push task\nbranches/open PRs**. Replace `3` with your approved USD run budget. Connection\nand setup can take longer than a minute; the dashboard serves until Ctrl+C.\n\nExcerpt from a real AO run, not the simulated demo:\n\n```text\n#21 attempt 1: passed $0.0126 https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/22\n#20 attempt 1: passed $0.0136 https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/23\n#19 attempt 1: passed $0.0150 https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/24\nfinished run-20261011-190131Z: 3 tasks, 3 attempts\nsummary: 3 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped; total $0.0411 against run cap $2.00\n```\n\nTry it without accounts or paid workers: `tally demo --dashboard --port 0`.\nThe seven demo tasks and token usage are scripted, not real savings evidence.\n\n## What Tally Does\n\n1. **Route:** size tasks and choose configured tiers using confidence, cost\n   and past outcomes when available.\n2. **Cap:** reserve budget and meter workers against their attempt ceilings.\n3. **Escalate:** stop capped/failed work and, when appropriate and affordable,\n   pass its branch and available checkpoint metadata to a stronger tier.\n4. **Verify:** independently run your configured repository checks.\n5. **Open a pull request:** publish verified work with a cost/evidence ledger.\n   **Tally never merges its task PRs.**\n\nBoth the run cap and remaining monthly budget apply. Polling/metering can\novershoot a ceiling; this is not a provider-side hard billing limit.\n\n## Runners and Agents\n\n| Runner | Execution | Agents |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Agent Orchestrator (`ao`) | AO manages sessions; opt into parallel workers at init or with `--parallel N` | Claude Code, Codex or configured mixed tiers |\n| Direct (`direct`) | Tally launches workers itself, one task at a time; no AO daemon needed | Claude Code or Codex, selected per tier |\n\nInstall/sign in to every configured harness. Tally never silently substitutes\none for a missing agent. Login does not prove model entitlement. Claude uses\na scoped consented allow-list; no runner uses bypass-permissions.\n[Runner details and proofs](docs/EVIDENCE.md).\n\n## Optional Integrations\n\n| Product | Adds | When Off or Unavailable |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| neatlogs | Decision/worker traces, read-back evidence, cost/pass-rate history and learned caps keyed by size and actual model | No Tally decision export when off; local history, then explicit priors/config caps. Previously installed global hooks may still trace sessions. |\n| Entire | Graph sizing, checkpoints, past-failure recall and metadata-only handoff | Repository search sizes tasks; no Entire checkpoints/hosted recall promised. No symbol matches falls back without calling a working graph disconnected. |\n| cfo.ai | Monthly budget read, reconciled real spend and workspace-shared per-attempt Ari reports when enabled | Local budget/ledger; failed writes retain `cfoai-spend-fallback.csv` for reconciliation. |\n\nCredentials stay in their login tools or `~/.tally/`, not the repository.\nA stored trace ID does not itself prove delivery.\n\n## Evidence, Not Promises\n\n| Matched Three-Task Pass | Estimated Cost | Final Tasks Passing |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Every task on the top tier | $1.22 | 3/3 |\n| Tally v1, built-in priors | $0.31 | 3/3 |\n| Tally v2, history enabled | $0.43 | 3/3 |\n\n**Three tasks, one run each per pass**, same initial commit, list-price token\nestimates rather than invoices: about 75% and 65% cheaper on that sample. Each\npass also had three separately recorded startup retries. Only C's large task\nused observed history; no tier/cap changed. C cost more than B: this does not\nprove history improved on priors or guarantee savings on another workload.\n\n- **Cap and escalation:** [fly-doom-jev PR #17](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/fly-doom-jev/pull/17),\n  `run-20261012-100856Z`: cheap stopped at $0.018 (metered $0.01956), middle\n  passed at $0.16958; the failure classification was ledger-derived.\n- **Parallel AO:** the three overlapping workers above cost $0.04111932.\n  The 0.2.1 sanity run also produced\n  [PR #35](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/35) and\n  [PR #36](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/chip-design-groundup/pull/36),\n  2/2 passing at $0.02483996 with reconciled spend/read-back traces.\n- **Live learned ceiling:** [Othello PR #5](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/Othello/pull/5),\n  `run-20261012-153240Z`: six successful small/luna observations from neatlogs\n  set a P90 x 1.5 cap of **$0.02284986**, below the configured $0.30; passed\n  at $0.01816744.\n- **Learned cap caused escalation:** in self-host run `run-20261012-162757Z`,\n  tasks #155/#157 stopped at a learned **$0.02725116** cap (metered $0.02817828\n  and $0.02770580). Middle/terra passed on each same branch at $0.19549280\n  and $0.38153360: [PR #163](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/pull/163)\n  and [PR #162](https://github.com/Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack/pull/162).\n  Both assessments are `ceiling`, source `ledger`, because worker traces were\n  unreadable then; not trace-derived failure diagnoses.\n- **Four paths:** individual real PRs exist for Claude and Codex through AO\n  and directly. [Full evidence and reference](docs/EVIDENCE.md).\n\nThese later user runs demonstrate learned caps, not a new controlled savings\ncomparison. Caps require five successful samples per size/tier/model, never\nexceed config, and revert to config when insufficient data exists.\n\n## Consent and Data Sharing\n\nReview init's proposal. Integration setup can change external systems before\nthe final config write. [Approval details](docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md).\n\n- **Claude:** approve scoped file edits, git add/commit/status/diff and exact\n  external test/setup scripts. Missing/stale consent blocks launch; no broad Bash.\n- **Entire:** capture changes listed settings/local hooks. Publishing them to\n  the default branch requires separate confirmation. Trust agent hooks yourself;\n  hosted mirroring is another choice, generated summaries are off by default.\n- **neatlogs:** key verification sends a synthetic probe; enabled tracing sends\n  decisions. Optional machine-wide hooks can trace agent sessions outside Tally.\n- **cfo.ai:** fresh init asks for budget, spend and Ari context. Real attempts\n  write spend and, when enabled, organization-memory reports with task/title/IDs,\n  model/tokens/cost/decision/outcome, budgets and evidence URLs. They exclude\n  issue bodies, source/diffs, prompts/transcripts and raw spans. Re-init currently\n  uses a generic enable question: inspect its reporting disclosure/proposal.\n- **PR evidence:** available Entire/neatlogs/cfo.ai links are appended to task\n  PR bodies. There is **no separate init question for finance-URL sharing**.\n  Links do not grant access, but PR readers can see their addresses.\n\nNever paste keys into YAML, issues, PRs or diagnostics. Free-text labels and\nresult notes still need care; sanitization is not a complete privacy guarantee.\nDry runs never write simulated spend/Ari reports, but can export decision\ntraces when neatlogs is enabled.\n\n## Known Limits\n\n- Early product; verification is only as strong as your configured checks.\n- Never run two Tally processes against one cfo.ai page: cross-process spend\n  writes are not atomic and can overwrite one another.\n- Mixed AO permission switching is locked only within one Tally process.\n  Direct escalation and no-account onboarding need broader live validation.\n- Direct processes cannot be reattached after exit. Codex metering uses an\n  internal rollout format; missing metering stops the worker.\n- Model access, trace delivery and hosted recall are separate evidence checks.\n- 0.1.0 configs load sequentially unchanged; new config fields do not work on\n  0.1.0. Built runtime supports Node 20; source tests need a Vitest-supported\n  runtime (22.12+ on Node 22).\n\n## For AI Agents\n\nRead [the self-contained agent guide](docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md) before using Tally\non a person's repository: human choices, verified flags, plan/state/ledger\ninterpretation and failure handling. It ships with npm; [llms.txt](llms.txt)\nprovides discovery links.\n\n## More\n\n- [Getting started](docs/GETTING_STARTED.md)\n- [Business plan and labelled assumptions](docs/BUSINESS_PLAN.md)\n- [Detailed evidence and operational reference](docs/EVIDENCE.md)\n- [Website](https://tally-agents-pi.vercel.app/)\n- [npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/tally-agents)\n\nGitHub alternative: `npm install -g github:Vaibhaav-Tiwari/neatlogs-hack#release`.\nThe release branch is prebuilt; main is source-only.\n\n## Licence\n\n[MIT](LICENSE).\n"}},"status":"completed","stdout":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/README.md\nA /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md\nA /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/llms.txt\nM /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5/package.json\n","stderr":""},"started_at_ms":1791835078970,"completed_at_ms":1791835079026}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:57:59.079Z","ordinal":17210,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a131b1-81a7-7733-a62e-26162708691a","call_id":"call_fcaa125d333e499c82b0ee3498a5bc51","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","create_time":1791835079.079239}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
