{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T20:01:30.076Z","ordinal":17235,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-7ea22651-a728-40c8-a637-79faeb8d86f6","process_id":"85280","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","git status --short"],"cwd":"file:///Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git status --short"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":" M README.md\n M package.json\n?? docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md\n?? docs/EVIDENCE.md\n?? docs/assets/\n?? llms.txt\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":" M README.md\n M package.json\n?? docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md\n?? docs/EVIDENCE.md\n?? docs/assets/\n?? llms.txt\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":15791},"formatted_output":" M README.md\n M package.json\n?? docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md\n?? docs/EVIDENCE.md\n?? docs/assets/\n?? llms.txt\n"},"started_at_ms":1791835290076,"completed_at_ms":1791835290076}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T20:01:30.330Z","ordinal":17236,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-bce7d5b6-d755-43b6-858b-b1101a249756","process_id":"51529","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '290,380p' docs/EVIDENCE.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '290,380p' docs/EVIDENCE.md","name":"EVIDENCE.md","path":"docs/EVIDENCE.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"\n1. **Label the issues Tally should take.** Tally reads open issues that carry\n   one label (`tally` unless you choose another). The label must exist and be\n   on at least one open issue:\n\n   ```bash\n   gh label create tally --description \"Work for Tally\"\n   gh issue edit 12 --add-label tally\n   gh issue list --label tally --state open\n   ```\n\n   The last command is the query Tally makes. If it prints nothing, Tally has\n   no tasks to work on.\n2. **`tally init`** asks for a budget and the issue label, shows the\n   short configuration summary and the AO changes it would make, and applies\n   nothing until you confirm. Type the label exactly as it is on GitHub.\n3. **`tally doctor`** prints one line per integration: connected, missing or\n   broken, with the fix.\n4. **`tally run --plan`** lists the tasks it found and the model each would go\n   to. It starts no workers and spends nothing. Check the task count here\n   before going further.\n5. **`tally run`** starts real workers through the configured AO or direct runner. It spends against your\n   agent accounts and can push branches and open pull requests on the\n   repository.\n6. **`tally dashboard`**, in a second terminal in the same directory, prints a\n   local URL showing the run as it happens.\n\nMore on `tally init`: neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai are separate opt-in prompts,\ndefaulting to No. When both coding CLIs are available, a numbered menu defaults\nto Codex; if only one is signed in, init selects it automatically. A stopped\nor unavailable AO daemon selects the direct runner without asking. New configs\ndefault to one budget-checked retry on the `cheap` tier after explicit model\naccess rejection; re-init preserves an existing fallback setting. Set\n`modelAccessFallbackTier` in `tally.yaml` or use\n`--model-access-fallback-tier <name>` to change it. It displays a compact proposal (full YAML with `--verbose`), install record in\n`.tally/`, AO project registration/configuration and setup hook, then asks for\nfinal confirmation (default No) before applying changes. Use `tally init\n--dry-run` to preview without applying, or `--yes` only when you consent to\napplying detected defaults without prompts. Review model tiers, prices, tests\nand ceilings before starting workers. Claude's consented rules are installed\nlocally before launch. If Neatlogs is enabled, init also installs its hooks in\nthe selected agents' global settings; those hooks trace matching sessions on\nthis computer, including sessions outside Tally.\nThe final summary shows the permission policy for this AO project's workers.\nUninstall restores owned project settings; it never enables bypass-permissions.\n\nInteractive init requires a terminal. Piped input exits with a plain message\ninstead of waiting for answers. For scripted onboarding, pass values explicitly:\n\n```sh\ntally init --yes --test-command \"node --check js/app.js\" \\\n  --setup-command \"\" --budget 5 --issue-label tally \\\n  --neatlogs yes --entire no --cfoai yes\n```\n\nAn empty setup flag means no setup. Selecting Neatlogs runs a synthetic key\nprobe and installs global worker hooks for the selected coding agent(s).\nPast-run history connects automatically when its read credentials are already\nconfigured; otherwise Tally uses local history. Selecting cfo.ai enables its\nmanaged scenario guide and per-attempt reports; creating missing spend tables\nstill has a separate confirmation. Entire installation, login, repository\ncapture, settings publication, and mirror creation keep their own steps.\n\nInit detects test/setup commands from repository evidence and proposes tiers\nonly for installed Codex or Claude Code agents. When no test command is found,\nit asks for a shell command: Tally cannot verify work without one. Blank input\nor `--yes` without either a detected command or `--test-command` fails without writing configuration\nor changing AO settings. An undetected setup command is optional and never\nguessed.\n\nAO does not need to be on your ordinary terminal's PATH. Tally resolves the\nCLI from PATH first, then `TALLY_AO_BIN`, then `repo.aoBin` in `tally.yaml`\n(relative to that file), then the macOS Agent Orchestrator app bundle under\n`/Applications` or `~/Applications`. For a custom installation:\n\n```bash\nexport TALLY_AO_BIN=\"/Applications/Agent Orchestrator.app/Contents/Resources/daemon/ao\"\ntally init\n```\n\nDoctor and real runs use the same resolver. Init checks AO, git, GitHub auth\nand requested integrations before applying changes. An Entire opt-in requires\nEntire to be enabled in the target repo; init prints `entire enable` and stops\nif it is not, rather than installing capture hooks without consent.\n\nOnce confirmed, init records progress in `.tally/init-pending.json`. If an AO\nor file operation fails, it lists confirmed changes and unwritten files and\nprints the exact init command to resume. Re-running resumes only its owned,\nunchanged files; edited configs or changed AO settings are never overwritten.\nThe recovery record is removed after init and doctor complete. This is a\nrecoverable transaction, not an atomic transaction spanning the AO daemon and\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"\n1. **Label the issues Tally should take.** Tally reads open issues that carry\n   one label (`tally` unless you choose another). The label must exist and be\n   on at least one open issue:\n\n   ```bash\n   gh label create tally --description \"Work for Tally\"\n   gh issue edit 12 --add-label tally\n   gh issue list --label tally --state open\n   ```\n\n   The last command is the query Tally makes. If it prints nothing, Tally has\n   no tasks to work on.\n2. **`tally init`** asks for a budget and the issue label, shows the\n   short configuration summary and the AO changes it would make, and applies\n   nothing until you confirm. Type the label exactly as it is on GitHub.\n3. **`tally doctor`** prints one line per integration: connected, missing or\n   broken, with the fix.\n4. **`tally run --plan`** lists the tasks it found and the model each would go\n   to. It starts no workers and spends nothing. Check the task count here\n   before going further.\n5. **`tally run`** starts real workers through the configured AO or direct runner. It spends against your\n   agent accounts and can push branches and open pull requests on the\n   repository.\n6. **`tally dashboard`**, in a second terminal in the same directory, prints a\n   local URL showing the run as it happens.\n\nMore on `tally init`: neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai are separate opt-in prompts,\ndefaulting to No. When both coding CLIs are available, a numbered menu defaults\nto Codex; if only one is signed in, init selects it automatically. A stopped\nor unavailable AO daemon selects the direct runner without asking. New configs\ndefault to one budget-checked retry on the `cheap` tier after explicit model\naccess rejection; re-init preserves an existing fallback setting. Set\n`modelAccessFallbackTier` in `tally.yaml` or use\n`--model-access-fallback-tier <name>` to change it. It displays a compact proposal (full YAML with `--verbose`), install record in\n`.tally/`, AO project registration/configuration and setup hook, then asks for\nfinal confirmation (default No) before applying changes. Use `tally init\n--dry-run` to preview without applying, or `--yes` only when you consent to\napplying detected defaults without prompts. Review model tiers, prices, tests\nand ceilings before starting workers. Claude's consented rules are installed\nlocally before launch. If Neatlogs is enabled, init also installs its hooks in\nthe selected agents' global settings; those hooks trace matching sessions on\nthis computer, including sessions outside Tally.\nThe final summary shows the permission policy for this AO project's workers.\nUninstall restores owned project settings; it never enables bypass-permissions.\n\nInteractive init requires a terminal. Piped input exits with a plain message\ninstead of waiting for answers. For scripted onboarding, pass values explicitly:\n\n```sh\ntally init --yes --test-command \"node --check js/app.js\" \\\n  --setup-command \"\" --budget 5 --issue-label tally \\\n  --neatlogs yes --entire no --cfoai yes\n```\n\nAn empty setup flag means no setup. Selecting Neatlogs runs a synthetic key\nprobe and installs global worker hooks for the selected coding agent(s).\nPast-run history connects automatically when its read credentials are already\nconfigured; otherwise Tally uses local history. Selecting cfo.ai enables its\nmanaged scenario guide and per-attempt reports; creating missing spend tables\nstill has a separate confirmation. Entire installation, login, repository\ncapture, settings publication, and mirror creation keep their own steps.\n\nInit detects test/setup commands from repository evidence and proposes tiers\nonly for installed Codex or Claude Code agents. When no test command is found,\nit asks for a shell command: Tally cannot verify work without one. Blank input\nor `--yes` without either a detected command or `--test-command` fails without writing configuration\nor changing AO settings. An undetected setup command is optional and never\nguessed.\n\nAO does not need to be on your ordinary terminal's PATH. Tally resolves the\nCLI from PATH first, then `TALLY_AO_BIN`, then `repo.aoBin` in `tally.yaml`\n(relative to that file), then the macOS Agent Orchestrator app bundle under\n`/Applications` or `~/Applications`. For a custom installation:\n\n```bash\nexport TALLY_AO_BIN=\"/Applications/Agent Orchestrator.app/Contents/Resources/daemon/ao\"\ntally init\n```\n\nDoctor and real runs use the same resolver. Init checks AO, git, GitHub auth\nand requested integrations before applying changes. An Entire opt-in requires\nEntire to be enabled in the target repo; init prints `entire enable` and stops\nif it is not, rather than installing capture hooks without consent.\n\nOnce confirmed, init records progress in `.tally/init-pending.json`. If an AO\nor file operation fails, it lists confirmed changes and unwritten files and\nprints the exact init command to resume. Re-running resumes only its owned,\nunchanged files; edited configs or changed AO settings are never overwritten.\nThe recovery record is removed after init and doctor complete. This is a\nrecoverable transaction, not an atomic transaction spanning the AO daemon and\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":8459},"formatted_output":"\n1. **Label the issues Tally should take.** Tally reads open issues that carry\n   one label (`tally` unless you choose another). The label must exist and be\n   on at least one open issue:\n\n   ```bash\n   gh label create tally --description \"Work for Tally\"\n   gh issue edit 12 --add-label tally\n   gh issue list --label tally --state open\n   ```\n\n   The last command is the query Tally makes. If it prints nothing, Tally has\n   no tasks to work on.\n2. **`tally init`** asks for a budget and the issue label, shows the\n   short configuration summary and the AO changes it would make, and applies\n   nothing until you confirm. Type the label exactly as it is on GitHub.\n3. **`tally doctor`** prints one line per integration: connected, missing or\n   broken, with the fix.\n4. **`tally run --plan`** lists the tasks it found and the model each would go\n   to. It starts no workers and spends nothing. Check the task count here\n   before going further.\n5. **`tally run`** starts real workers through the configured AO or direct runner. It spends against your\n   agent accounts and can push branches and open pull requests on the\n   repository.\n6. **`tally dashboard`**, in a second terminal in the same directory, prints a\n   local URL showing the run as it happens.\n\nMore on `tally init`: neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai are separate opt-in prompts,\ndefaulting to No. When both coding CLIs are available, a numbered menu defaults\nto Codex; if only one is signed in, init selects it automatically. A stopped\nor unavailable AO daemon selects the direct runner without asking. New configs\ndefault to one budget-checked retry on the `cheap` tier after explicit model\naccess rejection; re-init preserves an existing fallback setting. Set\n`modelAccessFallbackTier` in `tally.yaml` or use\n`--model-access-fallback-tier <name>` to change it. It displays a compact proposal (full YAML with `--verbose`), install record in\n`.tally/`, AO project registration/configuration and setup hook, then asks for\nfinal confirmation (default No) before applying changes. Use `tally init\n--dry-run` to preview without applying, or `--yes` only when you consent to\napplying detected defaults without prompts. Review model tiers, prices, tests\nand ceilings before starting workers. Claude's consented rules are installed\nlocally before launch. If Neatlogs is enabled, init also installs its hooks in\nthe selected agents' global settings; those hooks trace matching sessions on\nthis computer, including sessions outside Tally.\nThe final summary shows the permission policy for this AO project's workers.\nUninstall restores owned project settings; it never enables bypass-permissions.\n\nInteractive init requires a terminal. Piped input exits with a plain message\ninstead of waiting for answers. For scripted onboarding, pass values explicitly:\n\n```sh\ntally init --yes --test-command \"node --check js/app.js\" \\\n  --setup-command \"\" --budget 5 --issue-label tally \\\n  --neatlogs yes --entire no --cfoai yes\n```\n\nAn empty setup flag means no setup. Selecting Neatlogs runs a synthetic key\nprobe and installs global worker hooks for the selected coding agent(s).\nPast-run history connects automatically when its read credentials are already\nconfigured; otherwise Tally uses local history. Selecting cfo.ai enables its\nmanaged scenario guide and per-attempt reports; creating missing spend tables\nstill has a separate confirmation. Entire installation, login, repository\ncapture, settings publication, and mirror creation keep their own steps.\n\nInit detects test/setup commands from repository evidence and proposes tiers\nonly for installed Codex or Claude Code agents. When no test command is found,\nit asks for a shell command: Tally cannot verify work without one. Blank input\nor `--yes` without either a detected command or `--test-command` fails without writing configuration\nor changing AO settings. An undetected setup command is optional and never\nguessed.\n\nAO does not need to be on your ordinary terminal's PATH. Tally resolves the\nCLI from PATH first, then `TALLY_AO_BIN`, then `repo.aoBin` in `tally.yaml`\n(relative to that file), then the macOS Agent Orchestrator app bundle under\n`/Applications` or `~/Applications`. For a custom installation:\n\n```bash\nexport TALLY_AO_BIN=\"/Applications/Agent Orchestrator.app/Contents/Resources/daemon/ao\"\ntally init\n```\n\nDoctor and real runs use the same resolver. Init checks AO, git, GitHub auth\nand requested integrations before applying changes. An Entire opt-in requires\nEntire to be enabled in the target repo; init prints `entire enable` and stops\nif it is not, rather than installing capture hooks without consent.\n\nOnce confirmed, init records progress in `.tally/init-pending.json`. If an AO\nor file operation fails, it lists confirmed changes and unwritten files and\nprints the exact init command to resume. Re-running resumes only its owned,\nunchanged files; edited configs or changed AO settings are never overwritten.\nThe recovery record is removed after init and doctor complete. This is a\nrecoverable transaction, not an atomic transaction spanning the AO daemon and\n"},"started_at_ms":1791835290330,"completed_at_ms":1791835290330}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-12T20:01:30.576Z","ordinal":17237,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a125af-5d46-7871-9bed-a128df2c2957","turn_id":"01a131aa-8bd4-7dc0-9e91-8071b2de8f12","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-443310fc-8e0d-41dd-89be-3b6121799248","process_id":"73729","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","cat /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/skills/using-ao/SKILL.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/worktrees/neatlogs-hack/neatlogs-hack-5","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"cat /Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/skills/using-ao/SKILL.md","name":"SKILL.md","path":"/Users/vaibhaav/.ao/data/skills/using-ao/SKILL.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"---\nname: using-ao\ndescription: \"Catalog of the AO (Agent Orchestrator) `ao` CLI: spawning workers, managing sessions and projects, creating reusable Cues, sending messages, controlling the shared browser, previewing pages, and daemon control. Use when using the ao CLI, creating Cues, spawning workers, or managing AO sessions in an AO workspace.\"\ntrigger: \"Using the ao CLI in an AO workspace: creating Cues, spawning workers, managing sessions/projects, sending messages, controlling or previewing pages.\"\n---\n\n# AO CLI Catalog\n\n`ao` is a thin CLI over the local AO daemon. Every command is `ao <command> --help` for the authoritative flag list.\n\n| Command | What it does | When to use | Details |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `spawn` | Spawn a project worker, orchestrator, or projectless standalone worker | Starting a new task or issue | [commands/spawn.md](commands/spawn.md) |\n| `session` | Manage agent sessions (list, kill, rename, restore, etc.) | Inspecting or controlling running/terminated sessions | [commands/session.md](commands/session.md) |\n| `agent` | Inspect installed harnesses and launch readiness | Choosing or diagnosing an agent harness | `ao agent ls --refresh` |\n| `pr` | Merge a PR or resolve its review threads | Completing a reviewed change | `ao pr merge` / `ao pr resolve-comments` |\n| `project` | Register, inspect, configure, or remove projects | Setting up or managing repos AO knows about | [commands/project.md](commands/project.md) |\n| `automation` | Manage durable recurring session automations | Creating, editing, disabling, deleting, and inspecting scheduled runs | [commands/automation.md](commands/automation.md) |\n| `cue` | Create or list reusable project Cues | Saving a repetitive command or agent task at the user's request | [commands/cue.md](commands/cue.md) |\n| `orchestrator` | List orchestrator sessions | Viewing which sessions are orchestrators | [commands/orchestrator.md](commands/orchestrator.md) |\n| `review` | Start, list, cancel, or submit AO's native reviewer for a worker's PR (default: this session) | Getting an adversarial review of an AO session's PR; never spawn a worker to review | [commands/review.md](commands/review.md) |\n| `send` | Send a message to a running agent session | Correcting or directing a live agent | [commands/send.md](commands/send.md) |\n| `report` | Persist a meaningful worker report | Checkpoints, blockers, decisions, outputs, and completion | [commands/report.md](commands/report.md) |\n| `preview` | Start a session-owned app or open an exact URL/file | Running and showing the worker's relevant app, Markdown, HTML, PDF, or image | [commands/preview.md](commands/preview.md) |\n| `render` | Show a self-contained HTML page inline in a chat thread | Answering with a chart, table, diagram, or mockup | [commands/render.md](commands/render.md) |\n| `browser` | Inspect and control the session's shared live browser | Verifying a web app through snapshots, interactions, waits, screenshots, console, and errors | [commands/browser.md](commands/browser.md) |\n| `start` | Fetch (if needed) and open the AO desktop app | Launching the app | [commands/start.md](commands/start.md) |\n| `stop` | Stop the AO daemon | Shutting down AO | [commands/stop.md](commands/stop.md) |\n| `status` | Show daemon status | Verifying the daemon is up and healthy | [commands/status.md](commands/status.md) |\n| `doctor` | Run local health checks | Diagnosing AO setup problems | [commands/doctor.md](commands/doctor.md) |\n| `import` | Import projects from a legacy AO install | Migrating from the old flat-file store | [commands/import.md](commands/import.md) |\n| `version` | Print version information | Checking installed version | - |\n| `completion` | Generate shell completion scripts | Setting up tab completion | - |\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Most read commands accept `--json` for machine-readable output.\n- `-p / --project` scopes session subcommand lookups to one project.\n- Session and project ids are shown by `ao session ls` and `ao project ls`.\n- To refer to a session in AO Chat or the AO terminal, use the canonical in-app\n  link `ao://sessions/{project-id}/{session-id}`. Read\n  [commands/session.md](commands/session.md) for identity, encoding, and safety\n  rules before constructing one.\n- When the user asks to save a repeatable task as a Cue, use `ao cue list --json` to check existing definitions, then `ao cue create`. An agent Cue stores a reusable instruction; a command Cue stores an exact shell command. This agent-facing command can create and read Cues, but cannot edit or delete them. Direct the user to project settings for changes to existing Cues.\n- `--agent` is an alias for `--harness` on `ao spawn`.\n- Every command accepts `-h / --help` for the full flag list.\n- For frontend launch, preview selection, or artifact handoff, read\n  [commands/preview.md](commands/preview.md) before acting. Its static-file,\n  project-runtime, and automatic-handoff rules are load-bearing.\n- For page inspection, interaction, or request diagnosis, read\n  [commands/browser.md](commands/browser.md). It defines shared-tab behavior\n  and the opt-in network policy.\n\nUse [references.md](references.md) only when a natural-language request does\nnot map clearly to a command above.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: using-ao\ndescription: \"Catalog of the AO (Agent Orchestrator) `ao` CLI: spawning workers, managing sessions and projects, creating reusable Cues, sending messages, controlling the shared browser, previewing pages, and daemon control. Use when using the ao CLI, creating Cues, spawning workers, or managing AO sessions in an AO workspace.\"\ntrigger: \"Using the ao CLI in an AO workspace: creating Cues, spawning workers, managing sessions/projects, sending messages, controlling or previewing pages.\"\n---\n\n# AO CLI Catalog\n\n`ao` is a thin CLI over the local AO daemon. Every command is `ao <command> --help` for the authoritative flag list.\n\n| Command | What it does | When to use | Details |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `spawn` | Spawn a project worker, orchestrator, or projectless standalone worker | Starting a new task or issue | [commands/spawn.md](commands/spawn.md) |\n| `session` | Manage agent sessions (list, kill, rename, restore, etc.) | Inspecting or controlling running/terminated sessions | [commands/session.md](commands/session.md) |\n| `agent` | Inspect installed harnesses and launch readiness | Choosing or diagnosing an agent harness | `ao agent ls --refresh` |\n| `pr` | Merge a PR or resolve its review threads | Completing a reviewed change | `ao pr merge` / `ao pr resolve-comments` |\n| `project` | Register, inspect, configure, or remove projects | Setting up or managing repos AO knows about | [commands/project.md](commands/project.md) |\n| `automation` | Manage durable recurring session automations | Creating, editing, disabling, deleting, and inspecting scheduled runs | [commands/automation.md](commands/automation.md) |\n| `cue` | Create or list reusable project Cues | Saving a repetitive command or agent task at the user's request | [commands/cue.md](commands/cue.md) |\n| `orchestrator` | List orchestrator sessions | Viewing which sessions are orchestrators | [commands/orchestrator.md](commands/orchestrator.md) |\n| `review` | Start, list, cancel, or submit AO's native reviewer for a worker's PR (default: this session) | Getting an adversarial review of an AO session's PR; never spawn a worker to review | [commands/review.md](commands/review.md) |\n| `send` | Send a message to a running agent session | Correcting or directing a live agent | [commands/send.md](commands/send.md) |\n| `report` | Persist a meaningful worker report | Checkpoints, blockers, decisions, outputs, and completion | [commands/report.md](commands/report.md) |\n| `preview` | Start a session-owned app or open an exact URL/file | Running and showing the worker's relevant app, Markdown, HTML, PDF, or image | [commands/preview.md](commands/preview.md) |\n| `render` | Show a self-contained HTML page inline in a chat thread | Answering with a chart, table, diagram, or mockup | [commands/render.md](commands/render.md) |\n| `browser` | Inspect and control the session's shared live browser | Verifying a web app through snapshots, interactions, waits, screenshots, console, and errors | [commands/browser.md](commands/browser.md) |\n| `start` | Fetch (if needed) and open the AO desktop app | Launching the app | [commands/start.md](commands/start.md) |\n| `stop` | Stop the AO daemon | Shutting down AO | [commands/stop.md](commands/stop.md) |\n| `status` | Show daemon status | Verifying the daemon is up and healthy | [commands/status.md](commands/status.md) |\n| `doctor` | Run local health checks | Diagnosing AO setup problems | [commands/doctor.md](commands/doctor.md) |\n| `import` | Import projects from a legacy AO install | Migrating from the old flat-file store | [commands/import.md](commands/import.md) |\n| `version` | Print version information | Checking installed version | - |\n| `completion` | Generate shell completion scripts | Setting up tab completion | - |\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Most read commands accept `--json` for machine-readable output.\n- `-p / --project` scopes session subcommand lookups to one project.\n- Session and project ids are shown by `ao session ls` and `ao project ls`.\n- To refer to a session in AO Chat or the AO terminal, use the canonical in-app\n  link `ao://sessions/{project-id}/{session-id}`. Read\n  [commands/session.md](commands/session.md) for identity, encoding, and safety\n  rules before constructing one.\n- When the user asks to save a repeatable task as a Cue, use `ao cue list --json` to check existing definitions, then `ao cue create`. An agent Cue stores a reusable instruction; a command Cue stores an exact shell command. This agent-facing command can create and read Cues, but cannot edit or delete them. Direct the user to project settings for changes to existing Cues.\n- `--agent` is an alias for `--harness` on `ao spawn`.\n- Every command accepts `-h / --help` for the full flag list.\n- For frontend launch, preview selection, or artifact handoff, read\n  [commands/preview.md](commands/preview.md) before acting. Its static-file,\n  project-runtime, and automatic-handoff rules are load-bearing.\n- For page inspection, interaction, or request diagnosis, read\n  [commands/browser.md](commands/browser.md). It defines shared-tab behavior\n  and the opt-in network policy.\n\nUse [references.md](references.md) only when a natural-language request does\nnot map clearly to a command above.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":9500},"formatted_output":"---\nname: using-ao\ndescription: \"Catalog of the AO (Agent Orchestrator) `ao` CLI: spawning workers, managing sessions and projects, creating reusable Cues, sending messages, controlling the shared browser, previewing pages, and daemon control. Use when using the ao CLI, creating Cues, spawning workers, or managing AO sessions in an AO workspace.\"\ntrigger: \"Using the ao CLI in an AO workspace: creating Cues, spawning workers, managing sessions/projects, sending messages, controlling or previewing pages.\"\n---\n\n# AO CLI Catalog\n\n`ao` is a thin CLI over the local AO daemon. Every command is `ao <command> --help` for the authoritative flag list.\n\n| Command | What it does | When to use | Details |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `spawn` | Spawn a project worker, orchestrator, or projectless standalone worker | Starting a new task or issue | [commands/spawn.md](commands/spawn.md) |\n| `session` | Manage agent sessions (list, kill, rename, restore, etc.) | Inspecting or controlling running/terminated sessions | [commands/session.md](commands/session.md) |\n| `agent` | Inspect installed harnesses and launch readiness | Choosing or diagnosing an agent harness | `ao agent ls --refresh` |\n| `pr` | Merge a PR or resolve its review threads | Completing a reviewed change | `ao pr merge` / `ao pr resolve-comments` |\n| `project` | Register, inspect, configure, or remove projects | Setting up or managing repos AO knows about | [commands/project.md](commands/project.md) |\n| `automation` | Manage durable recurring session automations | Creating, editing, disabling, deleting, and inspecting scheduled runs | [commands/automation.md](commands/automation.md) |\n| `cue` | Create or list reusable project Cues | Saving a repetitive command or agent task at the user's request | [commands/cue.md](commands/cue.md) |\n| `orchestrator` | List orchestrator sessions | Viewing which sessions are orchestrators | [commands/orchestrator.md](commands/orchestrator.md) |\n| `review` | Start, list, cancel, or submit AO's native reviewer for a worker's PR (default: this session) | Getting an adversarial review of an AO session's PR; never spawn a worker to review | [commands/review.md](commands/review.md) |\n| `send` | Send a message to a running agent session | Correcting or directing a live agent | [commands/send.md](commands/send.md) |\n| `report` | Persist a meaningful worker report | Checkpoints, blockers, decisions, outputs, and completion | [commands/report.md](commands/report.md) |\n| `preview` | Start a session-owned app or open an exact URL/file | Running and showing the worker's relevant app, Markdown, HTML, PDF, or image | [commands/preview.md](commands/preview.md) |\n| `render` | Show a self-contained HTML page inline in a chat thread | Answering with a chart, table, diagram, or mockup | [commands/render.md](commands/render.md) |\n| `browser` | Inspect and control the session's shared live browser | Verifying a web app through snapshots, interactions, waits, screenshots, console, and errors | [commands/browser.md](commands/browser.md) |\n| `start` | Fetch (if needed) and open the AO desktop app | Launching the app | [commands/start.md](commands/start.md) |\n| `stop` | Stop the AO daemon | Shutting down AO | [commands/stop.md](commands/stop.md) |\n| `status` | Show daemon status | Verifying the daemon is up and healthy | [commands/status.md](commands/status.md) |\n| `doctor` | Run local health checks | Diagnosing AO setup problems | [commands/doctor.md](commands/doctor.md) |\n| `import` | Import projects from a legacy AO install | Migrating from the old flat-file store | [commands/import.md](commands/import.md) |\n| `version` | Print version information | Checking installed version | - |\n| `completion` | Generate shell completion scripts | Setting up tab completion | - |\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Most read commands accept `--json` for machine-readable output.\n- `-p / --project` scopes session subcommand lookups to one project.\n- Session and project ids are shown by `ao session ls` and `ao project ls`.\n- To refer to a session in AO Chat or the AO terminal, use the canonical in-app\n  link `ao://sessions/{project-id}/{session-id}`. Read\n  [commands/session.md](commands/session.md) for identity, encoding, and safety\n  rules before constructing one.\n- When the user asks to save a repeatable task as a Cue, use `ao cue list --json` to check existing definitions, then `ao cue create`. An agent Cue stores a reusable instruction; a command Cue stores an exact shell command. This agent-facing command can create and